WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:04.200
Hi, my name is Gabe Newell, and welcome to
Half-Life: Alyx.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.660
To say this product was challenge would be
an understatement:

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.980
it's our first Half-Life title in over ten
years,

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.180
and it's our first one in Virtual Reality.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:15.620
In addition to it being a great VR experience
that still

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:19.200
felt like a Half-Life game, we wanted Half-Life:
Alyx to serve players

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.660
who've been waiting to find out what happens
after Episode 2,

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.740
and at the same time provide an on-ramp to
anyone

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.090
who hasn't played a Half-Life game at all.

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:32.090
Ultimately, whether we succeeded in any of
that is up to you to decide

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:36.140
but hopefully, this commentary mode will give
you some insight into

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:40.329
the set of problems we faced, and how we chose
to tackle them.

00:00:40.329 --> 00:00:43.690
To listen to a commentary node, pick up the
floating hologram of a

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:47.670
radio headset in front of you, and simply
put it on your head.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:52.450
Once you're done listening to the commentary,
you can take the radio headset off, or simply

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:54.290
put on the next one.

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:55.670
Please let me know what you think.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:58.239
I can be reached at gaben@valvesoftware.com.

00:00:58.239 --> 00:01:03.360
While I'm unable to reply to all the emails
I receive, I do read them all,

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.259
and they're a critical part of the feedback
we use to evaluate our work.

00:01:08.259 --> 00:01:10.040
Thanks, and have fun!

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:13.090
a1_01_intro2
As in the case of any Half-Life game, the

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:17.409
first hour of Alyx must convey a
lot of information to the player, hopefully

00:01:17.409 --> 00:01:20.119
in a natural and entertaining way.

00:01:20.119 --> 00:01:23.299
Players need to know who they are, where they
are, what kind of world

00:01:23.299 --> 00:01:28.000
they're in, who the other important characters
and factions are, and so on.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.060
At the same time, they need to learn how to
actually play the game.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:32.310
How do they move?

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:34.100
What can they interact with, and how?

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.700
What should they be trying to do?

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:37.780
etc.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:40.439
Figuring out exactly what information we would
try to convey in

00:01:40.439 --> 00:01:43.549
these first two levels, and how it would be
conveyed,

00:01:43.549 --> 00:01:46.159
took us over two years of playtesting.

00:01:46.159 --> 00:01:49.469
It's tempting to try and include everything
we think a player should know,

00:01:49.469 --> 00:01:52.639
but all information has a priority, and the
more we include,

00:01:52.639 --> 00:01:55.630
the more chance there is that a player misses
something important

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:59.430
because they're distracted or confused by
something less important.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:02.890
So over the next two levels we'll use commentary
nodes to point out some of

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:06.959
the specific places where we grappled with
conveying information to the player.

00:02:06.959 --> 00:02:09.750
In-between these nodes, you'll likely be able
to spot many other

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.659
examples for players to find, if they're interested
in

00:02:12.659 --> 00:02:15.239
paying closer attention and understanding
more.

00:02:15.239 --> 00:02:17.079
a1_01_citadel_vista
One of the pieces of important information

00:02:17.079 --> 00:02:20.640
we struggled to convey was
exactly when Half-Life: Alyx is set within

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.000
the series' timeline.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.110
Whilst we tried to reflect it carefully in
many of the world details,

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:28.400
it was never a hundred percent successful
during playtests.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:31.150
Eventually, we decided it was too important
to be left to players

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:34.709
to figure out, and so we put it in our opening
text crawl.

00:02:34.709 --> 00:02:37.250
The Citadel's incomplete state provided us
with a early

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:40.200
opportunity to reinforce the timeline visually.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.349
Half-Life: Alyx takes place five years before
the events of Half-Life 2,

00:02:43.349 --> 00:02:46.819
in which the towering Citadel represents the
Combine seat of power.

00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:50.630
As we see here, however, Early Combine infrastructure
is a cluttered

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:54.750
mess of cables and unusual structures sprawled
all over the city,

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:59.099
surrounding the Citadel's construction, and
served as a useful indicator of the timeframe.

00:02:59.099 --> 00:03:03.599
We went on to reinforce this impression in
other places throughout the following level.

00:03:03.599 --> 00:03:07.329
Combine architecture tends to be abstract
in scale and form so we

00:03:07.329 --> 00:03:10.340
included some familiar visual cliches of human
construction:

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:14.120
The large, external scaffolds, some distinction
of floor levels,

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:17.010
rows of small lights and the tidying of the
silhouette into more

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:20.540
structured vertical elements, trying to strike
a balance between capturing

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:24.180
the alien nature of the citadel whilst grounding
its construction

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:28.620
in enough familiarity to better understand
its size and state.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:32.739
Large sweeping vistas, such as this one, proved
an interesting challenge in virtual reality.

00:03:32.739 --> 00:03:35.120
Their distance from the viewer means that
they don't benefit from

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.599
stereoscopic vision the way that small close-up
objects do.

00:03:38.599 --> 00:03:41.999
Because of this, the small objects on the
balcony often draw the attention

00:03:41.999 --> 00:03:46.090
of the players before they've even noticed
the imposing structure in the distance.

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:51.310
If we moved the Citadel too close, it felt
absurdly huge and difficult to take in.

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:55.209
Yet, placed alone on the horizon, it felt
two dimensional and flat.

00:03:55.209 --> 00:03:58.579
We found the solution in filling the space
in between the viewer

00:03:58.579 --> 00:04:01.569
and the distant structure with many scale
references.

00:04:01.569 --> 00:04:05.950
The city itself, huge supply cables leading
toward the vanishing point,

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:10.269
large repeated combine buildings, heavy pollution
fog, careful lighting,

00:04:10.269 --> 00:04:14.319
and all the animated elements, especially
the helicopter whose sound begins in the fade

00:04:14.319 --> 00:04:15.319
in.

00:04:15.319 --> 00:04:18.650
All these were carefully composed help draw
the attention of the player

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:22.560
to the Citadel first, before letting their
attention wander to take in

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:27.240
the details of the city and settle ultimately
on the inviting items here on the balcony.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.730
a1_01_greenhouse
In these first areas of the game, we grappled

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.600
a lot with the density
and prioritization of the information we were

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:34.600
conveying to the player.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:37.370
Playtesting showed us that players in VR were
easily distracted,

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:39.920
often due to the better peripheral vision,
such that they'd focus

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:42.920
on one scene element while losing track of
others.

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:45.460
This was exacerbated in these first rooms
because players were

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:48.870
acclimatizing to being in VR, often for their
first time.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:51.700
In addition, different players progress at
different speeds.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:56.270
We saw some players spend 30 seconds on the
opening balcony, and others 30 minutes.

00:04:56.270 --> 00:05:00.220
As a result, we did a lot of iteration over
this set of opening areas,

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:04.020
each time changing what information we tried
to convey, and where.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:07.570
Initially, the video call from Eli took place
in the later refuge room

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:10.660
with the snark and camcorder, but players
were often too engrossed in

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:14.530
interacting with all the detail in the room
to pay attention to the conversation between

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:15.810
Eli and Russell.

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:17.560
We moved the video call out to the starting
balcony,

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:21.930
but there it distracted from the establishing
shot of City 17 and the Citadel.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:25.630
It also came at a time when players were still
figuring out core game elements,

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:28.620
like movement and hand interaction with the
world.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:32.010
That collision made simple things hard, like
when to show the player a

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:35.480
movement tooltip if they immediately started
the video call.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:38.820
Eventually, we settled on inserting the greenhouse
between the starting

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:42.080
balcony and the refuge, which allowed us to
move the call to an area

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:46.080
with a slightly obscured vista and fewer interactable
objects.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:49.720
This avoided the distraction problems, and
ensured players were past the

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:53.170
initial gameplay moments of figuring out their
hardware and movement setup.

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:56.870
It also had the bonus of allowing us to add
further detail to the opening

00:05:56.870 --> 00:06:01.460
balcony and Alyx's refuge without fear of
creating distractions from the video call.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:04.030
a1_01_strider
Beyond conveying critical information about

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:07.800
the state of the world,
the first two levels of Half-Life: Alyx also

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:11.740
needed to show some of VR's strengths.

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:15.890
Conveying a sense of scale is something VR
does much better than flat screens

00:06:15.890 --> 00:06:20.790
and our early playtests showed us that nothing
quite sold that scale more than seeing a Strider

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:22.180
up close.

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:25.610
In addition to the visuals, to fully illustrate
the scale of a Strider

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:30.280
at close range we needed to do a lot of work
on its audio treatment.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:34.090
Players had strong expectations about feeling
the weight, power and presence

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:39.410
of the Strider through its movements, particularly
as it stepped on the balcony the player was

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:40.560
occupying.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:44.630
In order to convey this power through sound,
we layered multiple sound effects,

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:49.020
allowing us to address ranges in the audio
frequency spectrum and the temporal nature

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:56.530
of those elements separately.

00:06:56.530 --> 00:07:01.550
We then combine those elements in the Source
2 Audio Engine to control them as one sound.

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:04.290
a1_01_refuge
This room, internally referred to as Alyx's

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:07.380
refuge, was initially
the location where the Eli video call took

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:08.380
place.

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:11.440
However, once that video call was moved out
to the balcony,

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:13.730
this room was freed up to solve other problems.

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:16.230
In the Half-Life series, we've always tried
to ensure that there's

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:19.070
generally enough narrative for all players
to understand where they're

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:22.480
going and why, but for players who slow down
and pay attention,

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:25.430
there's more detail that can lead to a greater
understanding.

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:28.550
With the balcony being a place to get familiar
with the game's inputs,

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:31.630
the greenhouse being where the story kicks
off, and the Strider there

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:34.640
to surprise players with spectacle, we decided
that the refuge could

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:37.480
be a quiet moment for players, where they
could discover more narrative

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:38.960
detail in the environment.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:41.120
At the same time, it could be a place where
they can play

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:45.020
around a bit more, now that they're acclimated
to being in VR.

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:47.860
Spending time in the refuge is meant to bring
players into Alyx's

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:50.170
role in the resistance and her place in the
world.

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:53.860
We wanted it to feel like a real stake-out
spot, where Alyx had been cooped up for weeks

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:56.020
or
months while planning the heist, doing research

00:07:56.020 --> 00:07:57.150
on the Combine.

00:07:57.150 --> 00:08:01.340
The camcorder, snark and whiteboards are all
toys that respond to detailed

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:04.950
interaction, and reward deeper investigation,
giving curious players more

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:08.830
information about the world of City 17 and
what Alyx has been doing in it.

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:11.200
a1_01_olga
Two critical pieces of information that we

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:15.760
needed players to understand were
simple to state, but not so easy to convey:

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:19.400
that players were playing as
Alyx Vance, not the Gordon Freeman they've

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:25.230
played in all prior Half-Life games,
and that Alyx is going to speak, unlike Gordon.

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:29.250
These probably seem obvious to you now, but
until we focused on them,

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:32.000
it wasn't obvious to playtesters.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:35.160
Ensuring that everyone realized they were
playing as Alyx was something we

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:40.270
decided to hit with the biggest hammer we
could, and named the product after her.

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:44.500
But even with that, we were very careful in
Eli's video call to ensure it's

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:46.320
reinforced immediately.

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:50.310
Olga, the character introduced here, was added
to further drive

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:53.560
it home by calling out when the player exits
the elevator.

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:58.360
Olga is the first character the player meets
in person, and her dialog here,

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:02.090
and again ahead in the alley, is aimed at
driving home the fact that she's

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:05.320
conversing with the player, and that the disembodied
female

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:08.890
voice the player hears is Alyx speaking back.

00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:13.120
This was harder to convey in Eli's video call,
because it already features

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:17.740
Russell speaking 'off screen,' whereas in
this scene there's clearly no-one

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:22.690
else around in the conversational space other
than Olga and the player.

00:09:22.690 --> 00:09:26.480
Without Olga, we found that, even though they
had already spoken to Eli

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:31.280
and Russell over the video call, playtesters
still didn't feel like they were Alyx.

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:34.180
a1_01_music
When we started Half-Life: Alyx, we were very

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:38.940
curious to find
out how VR would impact our music design.

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:43.180
The Half-Life series has always used music
sparingly,

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:47.870
often leaving the ambient sounds of the world
to provide the audio background.

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:52.520
But it is a tool we like to use to highlight
moments where the world state

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:59.490
has changed in some important way - in this
case, that something's riled up the Combine.

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:04.030
In our early playtests of this scene, without
music but with many of the

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:09.090
other visual and audio elements represented,
we found some players didn't

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:13.190
understand that they should move along, and
that others became

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:17.010
distracted by the interactive objects in the
laundry.

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:21.980
We experimented with some very simple 'action'
music to communicate the

00:10:21.980 --> 00:10:25.880
changing of the Combine threat level, as well
as the story's intention for

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:29.500
Alyx to get to safety, and found that players
were less likely

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:32.860
to forget their quest and move towards their
goal.

00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:37.850
The music was kept simple and 'cinematic'
sounding to allow it to blend

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:42.250
with the purposely complementary background
sounds and gently start

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:48.050
to normalize the use of music in VR in a familiar
way.

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:52.140
This success encouraged us to use the same
technique in scenes

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:54.910
with similar requirements later in the game.

00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:57.300
a1_01_alley
The remainder of this level is designed to

00:10:57.300 --> 00:11:01.590
continue informing the player about
the world they're in, and to support the increase

00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:03.470
in the Combine's alertness.

00:11:03.470 --> 00:11:07.160
We also wanted detail to make the city feel
alive,

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:11.320
something that becomes much harder later on,
once the player has a weapon.

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:15.440
This was tricky to design, due to the challenge
we're often faced with:

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:19.200
the friction between narrative state and gameplay
state.

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:22.540
Here, the narrative implies that Alyx should
be making her way

00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:26.820
back to Eli as quickly and quietly as possible.

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:30.400
But gameplay wise, we're at the start of the
game, and players are

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:35.280
still testing the limits of everything they
can see and touch.

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:38.690
Unsurprisingly, playtesting showed us that
some players would dedicate

00:11:38.690 --> 00:11:44.029
themselves to following the narrative and
quickly move through the area, while

00:11:44.029 --> 00:11:49.760
others would completely ignore the narrative
and explore every nook and cranny.

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:54.029
While it's tempting to simply remove all distractions
in an attempt to unify

00:11:54.029 --> 00:11:57.970
all players towards following the narrative,
it would send the wrong signal

00:11:57.970 --> 00:12:02.480
about the density of our world, and how the
game intends to reward players for exploring

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:03.480
it.

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:08.540
So we spent months refining this section,
adding, changing and removing elements.

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:12.529
We tried to find the right level of narrative
tension, where players felt like

00:12:12.529 --> 00:12:16.310
they should be moving along, but not so quickly
that they can't stop to tinker

00:12:16.310 --> 00:12:17.800
with something.

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:22.300
We put in enough interactive objects and narrative
moments where players were

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:26.540
were always rewarded for looking around, but
we designed all those distractions

00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:30.910
to end quickly, so we could encourage players
to continue moving along.

00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:32.810
a1_02_crash
In order to limit the possibility of motion

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:35.620
sickness that could be
induced by moving the player in a vehicle,

00:12:35.620 --> 00:12:37.820
we initially wrote
this scene to have Alyx wake up after the

00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:39.540
van had already crashed.

00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:41.690
Players found this underwhelming, however,
since the game seemed to be

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:45.610
needlessly holding back an exciting moment
that they could have experienced for themselves.

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:47.700
Addressing this meant that that we had to
tackle the technical problem

00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:50.370
of moving the player comfortably in a vehicle.

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:53.101
In this van and in the train at the end of
the map, you'll notice that

00:12:53.101 --> 00:12:55.950
the outside world is only visible through
small windows.

00:12:55.950 --> 00:12:58.930
These outside views are necessary to provide
motion parallax, but the small

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:02.180
window size ensures that the player's field
of view is always dominated by the

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:06.040
vehicle interior, which is naturally in the
player's own frame of reference.

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:08.290
This greatly reduces the occurrence of motion
sickness.

00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:11.560
Aside from the parallax of the outside world,
we use animated lighting

00:13:11.560 --> 00:13:14.600
and careful sound design to produce the effect
of riding in a vehicle.

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:16.530
a1_02_pacing1
Where the last map was focused on conveying

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:20.220
a lot of information about
the world state to the player, this map is

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:23.230
where we need to start the player's larger
quest.

00:13:23.230 --> 00:13:25.650
That means the player must leave this map
with an understanding

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:29.290
of what's happened to Eli, and what they're
going to do about it.

00:13:29.290 --> 00:13:31.930
The player also needs to meet Russell, so
they have a

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:34.830
face behind all the radio dialog throughout
the game.

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:37.800
Finally, we need the player to collect the
gravity gloves

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:40.470
and the pistol, and learn how to use them
both.

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:42.000
This is a daunting list.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:45.500
From experience, we know that if we cram all
that into a single scene,

00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:48.280
players won't be able to remember more than
a couple of highlights.

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:51.200
So this map's purpose is essentially narrative
pacing:

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:54.160
We need to draw apart the required events,
separating them with

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:57.720
enough time and space that players could ingest
and remember them.

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:00.529
At the same time, we're a little under the
gun.

00:14:00.529 --> 00:14:02.810
Playtesting showed us that by now, some players
have been playing

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:06.510
for 30 minutes, and they're wondering when
they're going to see some enemies.

00:14:06.510 --> 00:14:08.360
a1_02_pacing2
One of the first steps the writing team took

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:11.290
on this map was to flesh
out a scene between Russell and Alyx that

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:13.890
covered all the dialog we needed.

00:14:13.890 --> 00:14:17.170
Due to the fact that the player is meeting
Russell for the first time,

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:20.020
and that there are a significant number of
in-fiction events that Alyx

00:14:20.020 --> 00:14:24.950
and Russell need to talk about, the scene
ended up containing a lot of dialog.

00:14:24.950 --> 00:14:28.170
We knew there was no way we could build a
scene that long without boring the

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:31.089
player, and the most important elements would
be lost in the noise.

00:14:31.089 --> 00:14:35.500
One of the first tools we used to address
this was the introduction of Russell's drone.

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:38.800
It allowed us to take chunks of the dialog
from that original scene

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:41.660
and deliver them throughout the area preceding
the scene.

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:43.940
This meant the scene itself could skip over
the in-fiction

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:46.860
preliminaries and get right to the critical
goal delivery.

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:49.940
a1_02_pacing3
In addition to delivering narrative at a consumable

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:53.550
pace, this map needed to
continue communicating the state of the world

00:14:53.550 --> 00:14:56.050
outside of the Quarantine zone.

00:14:56.050 --> 00:14:59.720
We also wanted players to understand when
Half-Life: Alyx takes place in the

00:14:59.720 --> 00:15:06.279
larger Half-Life timeline and to see for themselves
the massive scale of the Citadel under construction.

00:15:06.279 --> 00:15:09.790
The power of the Combine is embodied in their
indifference towards humanity.

00:15:09.790 --> 00:15:14.910
They have conquered Earth with little effort,
and are now plundering it for their own ends.

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:18.940
They put a modicum of effort into their propaganda,
but it's ultimately uncaring.

00:15:18.940 --> 00:15:23.270
Later, in Half-Life 2, we see Breen's attempts
to talk to humanity with

00:15:23.270 --> 00:15:26.700
more seriousness, but it's unclear whether
that directive comes

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:30.890
from the Combine or it is just Breen's attempt
to be useful to them.

00:15:30.890 --> 00:15:35.040
Meanwhile, the Combine construction is brutally
efficient.

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:38.600
The Citadel's pervasive feeder cables make
no attempt to fit into the

00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:42.899
lives of the residents as the Combine drain
the city of its power.

00:15:42.899 --> 00:15:47.459
Within this playground, a nostalgic nod to
Half-Life 2, this enormous cable

00:15:47.459 --> 00:15:50.800
has been run right through the mural on the
wall, demonstrating the

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:54.180
Combine's total lack of interest in the history
behind the city.

00:15:54.180 --> 00:15:56.380
a1_02_russell_choreo
Russell's laboratory is the first time the

00:15:56.380 --> 00:15:59.050
player is in the same room as
another character, and it highlights one of

00:15:59.050 --> 00:16:01.830
the main challenges we
faced when designing choreographed scenes

00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:04.720
in VR:
keeping the player and characters out of each

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:05.720
other's space.

00:16:05.720 --> 00:16:08.660
In previous Half-Life games, we could always
forcefully move the

00:16:08.660 --> 00:16:10.990
player if we really needed them out of the
way.

00:16:10.990 --> 00:16:14.510
We made several attempts at finding a similar
solution in VR, but found

00:16:14.510 --> 00:16:18.270
that it often made players feel deeply uncomfortable
or disoriented.

00:16:18.270 --> 00:16:21.680
So instead, we designed these spaces to allow
for a natural

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:24.450
separation between the player and our characters.

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:27.330
Where natural separators couldn't exist, we
had to restrict players

00:16:27.330 --> 00:16:29.940
from moving into the character's space, removing
that

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:33.420
limitation as soon as the character had moved
out of the way.

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:36.321
Another challenge was balancing how often
characters respond to player

00:16:36.321 --> 00:16:39.360
actions while delivering important narrative
information.

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:43.330
In early experiments, we allowed characters
to stop mid-sentence,

00:16:43.330 --> 00:16:45.950
which allowed them to respond immediately
to the player,

00:16:45.950 --> 00:16:47.700
and then continue on with their dialog.

00:16:47.700 --> 00:16:52.600
While technically straightforward, this was
incredibly difficult to execute in animation

00:16:52.600 --> 00:16:55.050
and dialog without feeling unnatural.

00:16:55.050 --> 00:16:58.030
The combination of custom reactions necessary
to cover the range of

00:16:58.030 --> 00:17:01.029
possible player interaction, and their resulting
return to

00:17:01.029 --> 00:17:04.480
narrative dialog, quickly multiplied out of
control.

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:07.600
Eventually, we settled on a solution where
we allowed only a minimum of

00:17:07.600 --> 00:17:11.419
interactions during important narrative delivery
sections, and a wider

00:17:11.419 --> 00:17:15.789
range of responses to more complex reactions
after the scene has completed.

00:17:15.789 --> 00:17:19.369
In the case of Russell, we layer subtle head
facing, and natural

00:17:19.369 --> 00:17:23.419
eye-contact on top of his performance while
he is talking with Alyx.

00:17:23.419 --> 00:17:26.509
Then, after the scene when he is typing at
the computer, he will

00:17:26.509 --> 00:17:30.039
respond to players trying to touch him, or
objects being thrown around.

00:17:30.039 --> 00:17:32.159
a1_02_russell_typing
Right from our earliest experiments in VR,

00:17:32.159 --> 00:17:35.539
we learned that players expect
a high level of audio fidelity, even from

00:17:35.539 --> 00:17:38.880
minute interactions, particularly
when the sounds are meant to correspond to

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:40.830
clearly observable visuals.

00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:43.720
For instance, in one of our prior games, it
may have been acceptable to

00:17:43.720 --> 00:17:47.409
use a generic sequence of computer key sounds
when Russell was typing.

00:17:47.409 --> 00:17:50.549
In VR, however, we know that a player can
observe the animations very

00:17:50.549 --> 00:17:54.690
closely and even put their head right next
to Russell's keyboard if they choose.

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:56.779
In this case, players would notice a lack
of one-to-one

00:17:56.779 --> 00:18:00.149
correspondence between the keypress animation
and sound.

00:18:00.149 --> 00:18:02.380
For Half-Life: Alyx, we authored a set of
individual key

00:18:02.380 --> 00:18:06.340
press sounds to be driven by animation.

00:18:06.340 --> 00:18:07.580
For example...

00:18:07.580 --> 00:18:10.919
As Russell's fingers animate to press a key,
one of these keypress sounds is

00:18:10.919 --> 00:18:14.409
played, resulting in precise synchronization
of the animation and sound.

00:18:14.409 --> 00:18:17.659
a1_02_gg_training1
One frustration players encounter in VR is

00:18:17.659 --> 00:18:21.100
having to physically bend
down or reach out for objects in the virtual

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:24.220
world,
especially if they have limited real world

00:18:24.220 --> 00:18:25.220
space.

00:18:25.220 --> 00:18:28.450
A common solution for this is to provide a
way to bring objects to

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:31.919
the player instead of forcing the player to
go to them.

00:18:31.919 --> 00:18:34.740
We knew this would be an important feature
in Half-Life: Alyx with its

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:39.370
sprawling levels and players' desire to explore
every corner of the world.

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:43.529
We didn't want this mechanic to feel too gamey
or magical, so we decided to

00:18:43.529 --> 00:18:46.389
integrate it into the fiction of the game
by making it a feature of the

00:18:46.389 --> 00:18:51.700
gravity gloves, which are a natural precursor
to the gravity gun of Half-Life 2.

00:18:51.700 --> 00:18:55.169
This allowed us to present the mechanic as
a real physical action that's

00:18:55.169 --> 00:18:58.409
enabled by a piece of in-fiction technology.

00:18:58.409 --> 00:19:02.419
To emphasize the physicality that is unique
to the medium of VR,

00:19:02.419 --> 00:19:06.840
we used a physical gesture to activate the
pull, instead of a button press,

00:19:06.840 --> 00:19:09.669
and then required the player to actually catch
the object

00:19:09.669 --> 00:19:12.669
instead of automatically attaching it to the
hand.

00:19:12.669 --> 00:19:16.059
This made the mechanic feel more natural and
rooted in the fiction of the

00:19:16.059 --> 00:19:20.119
game's universe, while allowing the player
to feel powerful and accomplished.

00:19:20.119 --> 00:19:22.320
a1_02_gg_training2
When the player 'pulls' an object with the

00:19:22.320 --> 00:19:26.169
gravity gloves, we use the game's
physics engine to apply a launch force to

00:19:26.169 --> 00:19:30.669
that object with a trajectory that
is partially influenced by the direction of

00:19:30.669 --> 00:19:32.010
the player's pull gesture.

00:19:32.010 --> 00:19:36.429
We set the object's launch velocity to result
in a standard flight time from pull

00:19:36.429 --> 00:19:41.230
to catch so the player can develop a rhythm
that eventually becomes automatic.

00:19:41.230 --> 00:19:44.389
We want the object to arrive at the hand as
reliably as possible,

00:19:44.389 --> 00:19:48.559
so after the initial launch we continually
apply small impulse

00:19:48.559 --> 00:19:52.299
forces along the trajectory (represented by
the white arrows).

00:19:52.299 --> 00:19:55.899
Those impulses help counter gravity and compensate
for collisions with

00:19:55.899 --> 00:19:59.450
obstacles along the way, while continuously
steering the object

00:19:59.450 --> 00:20:02.010
toward the hand, which is itself a moving
target.

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:05.519
A generous catch range and subtle haptic feedback
when the object enters

00:20:05.519 --> 00:20:09.049
that range virtually guarantees that it will
end up in the player's

00:20:09.049 --> 00:20:12.900
hand every time, even if their attention is
focused elsewhere.

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:16.320
One other benefit of implementing this with
physics is that we didn't

00:20:16.320 --> 00:20:20.409
need to do custom handling for an object's
size, mass, or inertia,

00:20:20.409 --> 00:20:23.419
and it automatically compensated for the non-standard
gravity

00:20:23.419 --> 00:20:26.509
that players encounter inside the vault at
the end of the game.

00:20:26.509 --> 00:20:28.529
a2_01_resin
Even in our earliest experiments in bringing

00:20:28.529 --> 00:20:31.809
Half-Life to VR,
we found that players enjoyed exploring every

00:20:31.809 --> 00:20:33.899
nook and cranny of the environment.

00:20:33.899 --> 00:20:37.340
With the increased fidelity of VR, players
not only expected to be able to

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:40.700
interact with a vast array of objects, but
they also had a strong desire to find something

00:20:40.700 --> 00:20:43.159
useful from time to time.

00:20:43.159 --> 00:20:46.470
This was so universal among testers that in
addition to more searchable

00:20:46.470 --> 00:20:50.840
locations and interactive objects, we added
a new resource: Resin.

00:20:50.840 --> 00:20:53.989
Level designers could place this new resource
in fun and interesting locations

00:20:53.989 --> 00:20:59.370
that weren’t tied to game progression, rewarding
players' wider-ranging exploration.

00:20:59.370 --> 00:21:02.679
We also tied this new resource to a longer
term goal in the form of weapon

00:21:02.679 --> 00:21:06.350
upgrades, further motivating players to explore
the environment.

00:21:06.350 --> 00:21:10.580
Deep scavenger hunts and keen observation
would result in more resin to spend as a reward.

00:21:10.580 --> 00:21:12.480
a2_01_toner_intro
Internally, we refer to this type of puzzle

00:21:12.480 --> 00:21:15.950
as a 'toner' puzzle,
due to its similarity to a real-world cable

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:19.059
toner tool that
can be used to locate electrical wires in

00:21:19.059 --> 00:21:20.279
walls.

00:21:20.279 --> 00:21:23.340
The toner mechanic was conceived as a tool
for designers to craft player

00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:26.210
experiences around knowing that the player
had to put their

00:21:26.210 --> 00:21:29.109
head and hands in specific locations.

00:21:29.109 --> 00:21:32.330
The initial design merely required the player
to use their multitool to

00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:37.529
push a ball of energy along a path, similar
to a child's bead maze toy.

00:21:37.529 --> 00:21:40.070
While this simple design was successful at
getting the player

00:21:40.070 --> 00:21:43.210
to put their head and hands in particular
locations, the lack of

00:21:43.210 --> 00:21:46.179
branching meant that the player wasn't required
to make any choices,

00:21:46.179 --> 00:21:50.070
nor was there any possibility of negative
consequences.

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:53.500
To address these issues, we added the rotating
junction elements,

00:21:53.500 --> 00:21:56.360
which allowed for branching paths and became
the player's

00:21:56.360 --> 00:21:59.730
primary means of interacting with toner puzzles.

00:21:59.730 --> 00:22:02.659
While the toner puzzles ramp up in complexity
throughout the game,

00:22:02.659 --> 00:22:05.950
this first toner puzzle is designed to use
a minimal number of elements

00:22:05.950 --> 00:22:10.269
to introduce players to the key ideas that
power flows through wires,

00:22:10.269 --> 00:22:13.820
power can be re-routed by rotating junctions
and power has an effect

00:22:13.820 --> 00:22:17.909
on the physical world such as turning on lights
and opening gates.

00:22:17.909 --> 00:22:20.559
The sparking electrical outlet that branches
down toward the floor

00:22:20.559 --> 00:22:23.870
is not strictly necessary for this simple
puzzle, but it was added

00:22:23.870 --> 00:22:26.850
here to expose players to its behavior since
it was causing

00:22:26.850 --> 00:22:28.950
confusion in later puzzles when players were
seeing

00:22:28.950 --> 00:22:31.480
it for the first time in more complex configurations.

00:22:31.480 --> 00:22:34.419
a2_01_lighting
From our earliest experiments in VR, we realized

00:22:34.419 --> 00:22:38.509
how critical high-quality,
high-density lighting was to conveying a sense

00:22:38.509 --> 00:22:39.840
of realism.

00:22:39.840 --> 00:22:42.830
In game development, any technology choice
involves trade-offs

00:22:42.830 --> 00:22:46.690
between quality, runtime performance and development
time.

00:22:46.690 --> 00:22:50.629
For Half-Life: Alyx, we chose a high quality,
high performance light

00:22:50.629 --> 00:22:55.419
mapping solution which required a significant
amount of offline pre-processing.

00:22:55.419 --> 00:22:58.779
We performed the lightmap preprocessing using
a render farm,

00:22:58.779 --> 00:23:02.710
which could take several hours for a single
map at the highest quality,

00:23:02.710 --> 00:23:05.179
and we put a lot of effort into mitigating
the cost of that

00:23:05.179 --> 00:23:08.210
high iteration time for designers on the team.

00:23:08.210 --> 00:23:11.919
Specifically, we implemented a real-time approximation
to the final lighting

00:23:11.919 --> 00:23:15.440
result, which allowed designers to light scenes
in real time with

00:23:15.440 --> 00:23:19.370
confidence that the high-performance lightmaps
would match their intent.

00:23:19.370 --> 00:23:23.399
Most lights in Half-Life: Alyx are baked into
a single term that

00:23:23.399 --> 00:23:26.370
describes distance falloff and shadowing,
allowing us to change the

00:23:26.370 --> 00:23:30.129
brightness and color of lights in real-time
at no cost.

00:23:30.129 --> 00:23:33.789
This encoding also allows us to apply shadows
from dynamic props and

00:23:33.789 --> 00:23:38.110
characters, without having to render any static
geometry into shadow maps.

00:23:38.110 --> 00:23:40.989
Our lightmaps store directional information
using the popular

00:23:40.989 --> 00:23:44.639
Ambient Highlight Direction encoding, an improvement
over Valve's own

00:23:44.639 --> 00:23:48.080
Radiosity Normal Mapping technique pioneered
in Half-Life 2.

00:23:48.080 --> 00:23:52.690
This new lightmap encoding ensures that bumpy
surfaces appear bumpy even when not under

00:23:52.690 --> 00:23:53.729
direct light.

00:23:53.729 --> 00:23:57.259
This blinking lightsource provides a good
illustration of this technique,

00:23:57.259 --> 00:24:02.149
as you can perceive the surface detail even
during times that the light source is off.

00:24:02.149 --> 00:24:05.210
a2_01_barnacles
Barnacles, while an iconic Half-Life creature,

00:24:05.210 --> 00:24:07.289
presented some interesting VR-specific challenges.

00:24:07.289 --> 00:24:12.070
While their tongue is intended to trap hasty
or unobservant players,

00:24:12.070 --> 00:24:14.240
those caught by them often didn't realize
what was happening.

00:24:14.240 --> 00:24:16.570
They would become disoriented, or even motion
sick,

00:24:16.570 --> 00:24:19.220
as the barnacle lifted them up to be eaten.

00:24:19.220 --> 00:24:22.289
These unfortunate players almost universally
died and failed to

00:24:22.289 --> 00:24:24.990
learn what was happening, even after multiple
encounters.

00:24:24.990 --> 00:24:27.929
To address this issue, we recreated the tongue
as a particle system.

00:24:27.929 --> 00:24:30.289
This allowed us to both physically simulate
it as well as

00:24:30.289 --> 00:24:32.730
reliably position it for maximum visibility.

00:24:32.730 --> 00:24:36.169
If a player does get grabbed by a barnacle,
the tongue always wraps right

00:24:36.169 --> 00:24:39.309
in front of their face, no matter which direction
they try to look.

00:24:39.309 --> 00:24:41.850
This makes the tongue almost impossible to
miss and visually guides the

00:24:41.850 --> 00:24:46.039
player's eyes up to the location of the barnacle
so they know where to shoot to free themselves.

00:24:46.039 --> 00:24:49.820
The asphyxiation visuals that kick in as the
player is being lifted not

00:24:49.820 --> 00:24:52.549
only serve to communicate the damage that
the player is taking but they

00:24:52.549 --> 00:24:56.409
also help to reduce motion sickness by narrowing
the player's field of view.

00:24:56.409 --> 00:24:59.769
These changes, combined with sounds effects
and other visuals like seeing

00:24:59.769 --> 00:25:03.830
stars and blacking out, eliminate confusion
for players and enabled us to

00:25:03.830 --> 00:25:06.309
bring this classic enemy into the world of
Half-Life: Alyx.

00:25:06.309 --> 00:25:08.720
a2_01_pistol_training1
We got a lot of positive feedback from our

00:25:08.720 --> 00:25:12.710
initial experiments requiring
players to perform a series physical actions

00:25:12.710 --> 00:25:14.429
to reload the pistol.

00:25:14.429 --> 00:25:19.259
Specifically: ejecting the clip, retrieving
a new clip from their backpack,

00:25:19.259 --> 00:25:22.340
inserting the clip into the gun and chambering
a round.

00:25:22.340 --> 00:25:25.090
Players enjoyed learning and improving at
this skill.

00:25:25.090 --> 00:25:28.979
We experimented with using a single button
for both ejecting a clip and

00:25:28.979 --> 00:25:32.249
chambering a round but we found that assigning
these operations to two

00:25:32.249 --> 00:25:36.700
different buttons was more interesting even
though it was more mentally taxing.

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:41.750
In fact, it was more interesting precisely
because it was more mentally taxing,

00:25:41.750 --> 00:25:45.249
as players would frequently mix up the buttons
under pressure.

00:25:45.249 --> 00:25:48.940
In the early stages of the game, players would
be fumbling for clips,

00:25:48.940 --> 00:25:53.990
ejecting the clips when they meant to be chambering
a round and generally making a mess.

00:25:53.990 --> 00:25:58.190
To those of us observing the playtest, this
looked things had gone horribly

00:25:58.190 --> 00:26:03.559
wrong, but players themselves consistently
cited this as a high point of their playthroughs.

00:26:03.559 --> 00:26:07.600
Because of the physical nature of the reload,
and the skill required,

00:26:07.600 --> 00:26:10.649
players blamed themselves and not the game
for the mistakes made as

00:26:10.649 --> 00:26:12.979
they were learning the reloading sequence.

00:26:12.979 --> 00:26:16.989
In this area, we provide a number of static
targets in the form of barnacles

00:26:16.989 --> 00:26:20.399
for the players to shoot at their own pace,
increasing the likelihood

00:26:20.399 --> 00:26:23.580
that they have at least a few reloads under
their belt before moving on.

00:26:23.580 --> 00:26:26.009
a2_01_locker_hacking
In prior Half-Life games, while playing as

00:26:26.009 --> 00:26:30.110
Gordon, the player saw Alyx use
her multitool to hack into Combine security

00:26:30.110 --> 00:26:35.220
systems in order to unlock doors or perform
other acts of sabotage.

00:26:35.220 --> 00:26:41.649
This presented an opportunity for us to represent
Alyx's hacking skills through VR-centric mini-games.

00:26:41.649 --> 00:26:43.840
All of our hacking mini-game designs were
built to leverage

00:26:43.840 --> 00:26:48.289
the independently-tracked head and hands common
to today's VR systems.

00:26:48.289 --> 00:26:51.909
We wanted to encourage players to use both
hands simultaneously and

00:26:51.909 --> 00:26:53.889
move their head to create parallax to understand
the

00:26:53.889 --> 00:26:57.119
spatial relationships within the puzzles.

00:26:57.119 --> 00:27:00.509
This locker hacking puzzle is based upon one
of our earliest prototypes,

00:27:00.509 --> 00:27:02.979
in which the player was tasked with using
a tool in one hand

00:27:02.979 --> 00:27:06.059
to paint a path on a sphere held by the other
hand.

00:27:06.059 --> 00:27:09.129
There was just something fun and unique about
using all of the

00:27:09.129 --> 00:27:13.309
natural degrees of freedom of both hands to
solve a puzzle in VR.

00:27:13.309 --> 00:27:17.149
In its simplest form, this puzzle's solution
is straightforward,

00:27:17.149 --> 00:27:20.799
but the difficulty gradually increases throughout
the course of the game by

00:27:20.799 --> 00:28:13.830
introducing static or moving obstacles of
various shapes and sizes on 

00:28:13.830 --> 00:28:15.080
the sphere.

00:28:15.080 --> 00:28:20.929
a2_02_art_pacing
This file has no closed captions!

00:28:20.929 --> 00:28:23.879
a2_02_health_injectors
In our early prototypes, players could heal

00:28:23.879 --> 00:28:27.409
themselves by grabbing a
Half-Life 2 health pack and pressing down

00:28:27.409 --> 00:28:30.419
on a controller button,
which obviously wasn't a very interesting

00:28:30.419 --> 00:28:31.520
VR interaction.

00:28:31.520 --> 00:28:34.470
During the development of Half-Life: Alyx,
the Counter-Strike team

00:28:34.470 --> 00:28:38.259
added health injectors into CS:GO as part
of the new Danger Zone game mode

00:28:38.259 --> 00:28:39.849
they were developing.

00:28:39.849 --> 00:28:42.909
When we saw this, we felt that an injector
would work as an intuitive

00:28:42.909 --> 00:28:45.499
healing item in VR as well.

00:28:45.499 --> 00:28:49.249
Players would press a button to arm the injector
as a way of indicating their intent to use

00:28:49.249 --> 00:28:50.249
it.

00:28:50.249 --> 00:28:52.539
This prevented unintentional use of the injector
and gave us an

00:28:52.539 --> 00:28:55.369
opportunity to showcase how physical the interaction
was

00:28:55.369 --> 00:28:58.639
going to be via haptics, animation and sound.

00:28:58.639 --> 00:29:01.309
Some testers even initially responded with
dread over

00:29:01.309 --> 00:29:03.840
the fact they were going to have to use a
needle on themselves

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:07.190
and looked away when performing the injection
action.

00:29:07.190 --> 00:29:10.820
We also had to create approximations of the
player's arms and body,

00:29:10.820 --> 00:29:15.110
as playtesters all had their own preferences
for where they wanted to apply the injection.

00:29:15.110 --> 00:29:17.499
We even added the ability to inject into the
head

00:29:17.499 --> 00:29:20.710
since so many players tried to do it just
to see if it would work.

00:29:20.710 --> 00:29:22.460
a2_02_pistol_training2
We had to ensure that players would become

00:29:22.460 --> 00:29:25.629
proficient at reloading their
pistol during the early levels of the game

00:29:25.629 --> 00:29:28.649
or later encounters in the game would be overly
difficult.

00:29:28.649 --> 00:29:32.630
The prior map contained only static targets
in the form of barnacles.

00:29:32.630 --> 00:29:34.729
Players could engage the barnacles at their
own pace,

00:29:34.729 --> 00:29:39.029
but there were enough of them that players
would need to reload at least a few times.

00:29:39.029 --> 00:29:41.919
This first live zombie is locked behind the
chain-link fence and cannot

00:29:41.919 --> 00:29:45.690
reach the player until the player opts in
to the encounter by shooting the lock on the

00:29:45.690 --> 00:29:46.690
door.

00:29:46.690 --> 00:29:49.600
The placement of zombies in the upcoming section
of the game is designed to

00:29:49.600 --> 00:29:53.279
require plenty of reloading, to ensure that
the player builds up this skill.

00:29:53.279 --> 00:29:55.509
a2_02_train_cars
Virtual reality provides the opportunity to

00:29:55.509 --> 00:29:59.200
add physicality to gameplay,
but this needs to be balanced against player

00:29:59.200 --> 00:30:01.299
fatigue and accessibility.

00:30:01.299 --> 00:30:05.309
For example, we experimented with a low ceiling
in this area,

00:30:05.309 --> 00:30:07.389
requiring players to crouch the whole time
that they made

00:30:07.389 --> 00:30:10.559
their way across the tops of these train cars.

00:30:10.559 --> 00:30:13.440
Playtesters did not enjoy having to crouch
for so long,

00:30:13.440 --> 00:30:14.889
so we raised the ceiling.

00:30:14.889 --> 00:30:17.200
a2_02_breakable_glass
The breakable glass panels in Half-Life: Alyx

00:30:17.200 --> 00:30:20.419
are yet one more system designed
to convince players that they are present

00:30:20.419 --> 00:30:22.220
in a living, breathing world.

00:30:22.220 --> 00:30:26.369
The panels detect the position of an incoming
impact and procedurally generate

00:30:26.369 --> 00:30:30.830
new, smaller glass panels in a radial pattern
around the impact position.

00:30:30.830 --> 00:30:34.590
These new panels can then break into smaller
panels themselves, and so on.

00:30:34.590 --> 00:30:37.779
We found that players would often break glass
panels accidentally then

00:30:37.779 --> 00:30:41.259
spend time 'testing' just how interactive
they really were by trying to

00:30:41.259 --> 00:30:44.460
break them more, eventually realizing that,
yes, the glass really

00:30:44.460 --> 00:30:47.059
did shatter at the appropriate location.

00:30:47.059 --> 00:30:50.070
At one point, we experimented with building
special puzzles out of breakable

00:30:50.070 --> 00:30:53.749
glass, but in the end we found the glass to
be more appropriate as a passive

00:30:53.749 --> 00:30:57.470
interactive element in the world, one that
reacts in a satisfying way to a

00:30:57.470 --> 00:31:01.559
variety of impacts, from zombie melee attacks,
to incoming gun fire

00:31:01.559 --> 00:31:03.529
or any other physical interaction in the game.

00:31:03.529 --> 00:31:05.529
a2_02_weapon_upgrades
Weapon upgrades are a new feature for the

00:31:05.529 --> 00:31:09.039
Half-Life franchise and
fictionally this mechanic paired well with

00:31:09.039 --> 00:31:10.669
Alyx’s tinkering nature.

00:31:10.669 --> 00:31:14.020
We approached this design challenge first
by thinking about what sort of

00:31:14.020 --> 00:31:17.340
upgrades could be meaningful and also provide
additional value to

00:31:17.340 --> 00:31:21.129
players without removing established interactions.

00:31:21.129 --> 00:31:24.869
With limited resin to spend, the upgrade system
also provided an avenue

00:31:24.869 --> 00:31:28.690
for players to personalize and invest in their
favorite weapon,

00:31:28.690 --> 00:31:32.990
or streamline an interaction on another weapon,
increasing its value to them.

00:31:32.990 --> 00:31:37.429
For example, the reflex sight still requires
players to physically aim but

00:31:37.429 --> 00:31:40.659
it improves the feedback on exactly where
their shot will hit

00:31:40.659 --> 00:31:44.009
and exposes previously hidden enemy weak points.

00:31:44.009 --> 00:31:47.759
While some players enjoyed this feedback and
new targeting opportunities,

00:31:47.759 --> 00:31:52.529
others didn't and would instead focus on upgrades
that suited their style of play.

00:31:52.529 --> 00:31:56.210
This allowed players to spend their limited
resin on the upgrades they cared about the

00:31:56.210 --> 00:31:57.640
most.

00:31:57.640 --> 00:32:01.219
Another aspect of weapon upgrades that we
focused on was not necessarily

00:32:01.219 --> 00:32:05.559
obvious when we first started to design and
test combat in VR.

00:32:05.559 --> 00:32:09.599
Due to the more physical nature of combat
in VR it can really push on the

00:32:09.599 --> 00:32:13.249
mental and physical capacity of certain players
as they’re required to

00:32:13.249 --> 00:32:18.729
utilize both their hands and move their head
to take in the environment dealing with enemies.

00:32:18.729 --> 00:32:21.710
While this is a very natural experience there's
also a lot

00:32:21.710 --> 00:32:25.840
going on at any given point with reloading
and combat.

00:32:25.840 --> 00:32:29.919
As we observed players in some of these more
intense combat situations,

00:32:29.919 --> 00:32:33.320
we started finding opportunities to allow
some upgrades to reduce the

00:32:33.320 --> 00:32:36.890
number of times a physical interaction was
required or reduce

00:32:36.890 --> 00:32:39.820
the complexity of a particular interaction.

00:32:39.820 --> 00:32:42.950
The autoloader on the shotgun is a good example.

00:32:42.950 --> 00:32:46.969
When added to the shotgun, the normally slow,
methodical action of feeding

00:32:46.969 --> 00:32:51.519
shells into the gun is replaced with a far
simpler, faster interaction of

00:32:51.519 --> 00:32:56.549
slapping the shells into the autoloader before
they get automatically fed into the gun.

00:32:56.549 --> 00:33:01.659
This allows players to look around, focus
less on the gun while reloading,

00:33:01.659 --> 00:33:03.929
and take in more of what’s going on around
them.

00:33:03.929 --> 00:33:06.480
a2_02_comfort_pacing
At the beginning of the Half-Life: Alyx project,

00:33:06.480 --> 00:33:09.350
we had the expectation
that players would want to take a break after

00:33:09.350 --> 00:33:12.000
30-45 minutes,
since that's what we were seeing with The

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:14.999
Lab and other
smaller-scale VR experiences available at

00:33:14.999 --> 00:33:16.470
the time.

00:33:16.470 --> 00:33:18.279
Because of this, we designed the pacing of
the game's

00:33:18.279 --> 00:33:21.409
levels around play sessions of that duration.

00:33:21.409 --> 00:33:24.279
Over the course of development, however, the
game itself included more

00:33:24.279 --> 00:33:27.179
down time between intense combat encounters
and the

00:33:27.179 --> 00:33:30.769
comfort of the new crop HMDs improved significantly.

00:33:30.769 --> 00:33:34.700
In fact, as the game progressed, we found
that players were happy to play for hours

00:33:34.700 --> 00:33:35.950
on end.

00:33:35.950 --> 00:33:38.710
At the end of a playtest, players would often
remark that this

00:33:38.710 --> 00:33:41.889
was the longest they had ever played VR in
a single session.

00:33:41.889 --> 00:33:43.849
a2_02_headcrab_foreshadowing
Until this point in the game, players have

00:33:43.849 --> 00:33:46.649
only seen
headcrabs attached to the heads of zombies.

00:33:46.649 --> 00:33:48.909
This led playtesters familiar with headcrabs
from prior

00:33:48.909 --> 00:33:51.820
games to anticipate encountering them on their
own.

00:33:51.820 --> 00:33:55.120
We decided to play up this anticipation by
foreshadowing the first headcrab

00:33:55.120 --> 00:33:58.789
encounter with a headcrab that drops out of
a vent before disappearing,

00:33:58.789 --> 00:34:01.249
as well as headcrab sounds in the environment,
including a threatening

00:34:01.249 --> 00:34:03.729
headcrab sound coming from the partially boarded
up doorway ahead.

00:34:03.729 --> 00:34:05.979
a2_03_mural
There are two layers to the markings on the

00:34:05.979 --> 00:34:09.559
walls in this area:
The square and dot patterns that are the functional

00:34:09.559 --> 00:34:12.599
elements of the
environmental puzzle and the mural representing

00:34:12.599 --> 00:34:15.419
the Vortigaunt's
interpretation of the events of the Half-Life

00:34:15.419 --> 00:34:16.419
saga.

00:34:16.419 --> 00:34:19.700
For the first few years of development, this
area only contained the

00:34:19.700 --> 00:34:23.220
dot patterns supporting the perspective puzzle,
but as we fleshed out

00:34:23.220 --> 00:34:26.560
the rest of the level, we found that playtesters
responded especially

00:34:26.560 --> 00:34:30.159
positively to the Vortigaunt's wall markings
leading up to the hideout.

00:34:30.159 --> 00:34:33.470
It was at this point that we realized that
this area could serve as a canvas

00:34:33.470 --> 00:34:36.440
for our Vortigaunt's artistic magnum opus.

00:34:36.440 --> 00:34:39.770
To do this, we organized the dot patterns
into a sort of swirling star field

00:34:39.770 --> 00:34:41.559
behind the Vortigaunt's art.

00:34:41.559 --> 00:34:45.119
The imagery itself, which was inspired by
petroglyphs and other traditional

00:34:45.119 --> 00:34:48.980
rock art, portrays many significant events
from the Combine invasion,

00:34:48.980 --> 00:34:52.060
painted hurriedly by the injured Vortigaunt,
almost as if he was trying

00:34:52.060 --> 00:34:55.860
to capture what fleeting memories he still
had of the Vortessence from which he had been

00:34:55.860 --> 00:34:56.860
severed.

00:34:56.860 --> 00:34:59.960
The fact that Vortigaunts have a complicated
relationship with time gave

00:34:59.960 --> 00:35:04.059
us a lot of leeway in terms of drawing this
as a strict, linear timeline.

00:35:04.059 --> 00:35:07.020
For example, you may notice that the Vortigaunt
uses contemporary Combine

00:35:07.020 --> 00:35:11.059
troops as shorthand for Combine invasions
from the past or that the timeline

00:35:11.059 --> 00:35:14.640
is just generally hazy: a little from the
past, a little from the

00:35:14.640 --> 00:35:18.260
present and a little looking forward to the
future events of Half-Life 2.

00:35:18.260 --> 00:35:21.530
a2_03_lore_music
There are many musical themes and motifs used

00:35:21.530 --> 00:35:26.220
throughout the game
to represent unspoken aspects of the story

00:35:26.220 --> 00:35:27.670
and lore.

00:35:27.670 --> 00:35:33.099
For example, the Vortigaunts play an integral
role in the sub-plot of the

00:35:33.099 --> 00:35:38.680
game and we chose to use music to subconsciously
communicate the repeating

00:35:38.680 --> 00:35:43.619
nature of their role both in the timeline
of Half-Life: Alyx and

00:35:43.619 --> 00:35:48.200
in the larger timeline of the Half-Life saga.

00:35:48.200 --> 00:35:53.140
Within the music in this scene, you'll hear
variations of themes used

00:35:53.140 --> 00:35:57.220
throughout the timeline of the game, from
the very first sounds

00:35:57.220 --> 00:36:01.120
of the game up to the finale in the Vault.

00:36:01.120 --> 00:36:18.760
Variations of these motifs represent the imprisoned
chanting of the Vortigaunts

00:36:18.760 --> 00:36:29.740
The extraction of their energy to confine
the G-Man

00:36:29.740 --> 00:36:42.119
As well as historically familiar sounds representing
the Vortessence itself.

00:36:42.119 --> 00:36:59.319
a2_03_alyx_reacts
During our earliest attempts to write Alyx,

00:36:59.319 --> 00:37:02.450
our instinct was
to have her react to absolutely everything.

00:37:02.450 --> 00:37:05.000
But what we learned from playtesting was that
Alyx reacting

00:37:05.000 --> 00:37:06.520
TOO much could actually be off-putting.

00:37:06.520 --> 00:37:10.230
Say a player is in a really tense, zombie-filled
level, and

00:37:10.230 --> 00:37:12.890
they’re having a great time, but suddenly
Alyx is in their

00:37:12.890 --> 00:37:16.430
head sounding angry or frightened or anxious.

00:37:16.430 --> 00:37:19.640
These Alyx reactions were creating a disconnect
between how the player

00:37:19.640 --> 00:37:22.980
was feeling and how the character they were
roleplaying as was feeling.

00:37:22.980 --> 00:37:24.320
So we took a lot of those out.

00:37:24.320 --> 00:37:26.599
The Alyx reactions we left in tended to be
the ones

00:37:26.599 --> 00:37:28.350
that were practically universal.

00:37:28.350 --> 00:37:31.339
Alyx's reaction here to the surreal entrance
to the Vort hideout

00:37:31.339 --> 00:37:34.160
is a good example, where it was just so different
from everything

00:37:34.160 --> 00:37:37.480
the player had seen up until then, and kind
of takes you by surprise.

00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:39.650
We watched so many playtests where the player
would see it

00:37:39.650 --> 00:37:41.750
and say, 'Whoa, that's weird.'

00:37:41.750 --> 00:37:45.069
So we had Alyx say the same thing a second
later, almost like an echo.

00:37:45.069 --> 00:37:47.829
a2_03_hideout_interactivity
For as long as we've been making choreographed

00:37:47.829 --> 00:37:52.589
scenes like this one,
we've found that there are two types of players:

00:37:52.589 --> 00:37:55.750
those who become
engrossed with the characters and those who

00:37:55.750 --> 00:37:59.050
tinker with everything
around them, paying little attention to the

00:37:59.050 --> 00:38:00.550
scene's action.

00:38:00.550 --> 00:38:03.799
To make these environments entertaining for
the tinkerers,

00:38:03.799 --> 00:38:07.570
we usually add objects for them to find and
play with.

00:38:07.570 --> 00:38:11.109
Since this scene is split into two rooms,
we were able to add a large

00:38:11.109 --> 00:38:14.349
number of interactions into this first room
for players to

00:38:14.349 --> 00:38:18.529
discover after the Vortigaunt has moved to
the second room.

00:38:18.529 --> 00:38:22.390
Some interactive objects are pure physically
driven toys,

00:38:22.390 --> 00:38:26.990
like the dangling headcrab corpses and the
hanging pans.

00:38:26.990 --> 00:38:31.180
Others are animations triggered by the player's
actions, like the way the

00:38:31.180 --> 00:38:35.380
caged headcrabs react to the player approaching
or shooting them.

00:38:35.380 --> 00:38:39.490
One type of object on the table is a squeezable
headcrab heart,

00:38:39.490 --> 00:38:42.730
which is a remnant from an older version of
this scene, where the player

00:38:42.730 --> 00:38:47.520
would revive a wounded Vortigaunt by squeezing
nectar from these hearts.

00:38:47.520 --> 00:38:50.700
While that scene didn't work out, we found
the experience too

00:38:50.700 --> 00:38:55.220
compelling and, well, too gross to cut completely
from the game.

00:38:55.220 --> 00:38:57.600
a2_03_hideout_scene
The fundamental challenge of any choreographed

00:38:57.600 --> 00:39:01.950
dramatic scene like this
one is making sure that we entertain the player

00:39:01.950 --> 00:39:06.210
without undermining
the delivery of critical gameplay information.

00:39:06.210 --> 00:39:09.819
In this case, the player needed to leave this
scene with the knowledge that

00:39:09.819 --> 00:39:15.260
the Vortigaunts have been imprisoned and that
the player should seek the 'Northern Star.'

00:39:15.260 --> 00:39:18.579
This particular scene went through many different
variations during

00:39:18.579 --> 00:39:21.849
development, from a cooking scene with an
extremely traumatized

00:39:21.849 --> 00:39:24.599
Vortigaunt to this humorous version.

00:39:24.599 --> 00:39:27.620
We even experimented with an interaction where
the player was

00:39:27.620 --> 00:39:30.910
required to squeeze the nectar of several
headcrab hearts

00:39:30.910 --> 00:39:33.960
into the Vortigaunt's mouth to revive him.

00:39:33.960 --> 00:39:37.730
While this was a memorable and novel VR interaction
that ensured the

00:39:37.730 --> 00:39:41.260
player was engaged with the scene, the interactivity
on the player's

00:39:41.260 --> 00:39:45.339
part often resulted in playtesters remembering
the actions they took

00:39:45.339 --> 00:39:48.250
but not the critical gameplay information.

00:39:48.250 --> 00:39:52.069
Eventually, we ended up with this more humorous
scene,

00:39:52.069 --> 00:39:56.240
which struck an effective balance between
entertainment and goal delivery.

00:39:56.240 --> 00:40:01.049
Importantly, we also distributed the scene
across two separate rooms,

00:40:01.049 --> 00:40:05.109
breaking the information delivery into two
steps: the first room where the

00:40:05.109 --> 00:40:08.569
player meets and learns about the state of
the Vortigaunt,

00:40:08.569 --> 00:40:12.780
and the second room where the player is given
their new goal.

00:40:12.780 --> 00:40:16.690
Separating these physically into rooms seemed
to help players more reliably

00:40:16.690 --> 00:40:20.760
retain the critical information delivered
in the second room.

00:40:20.760 --> 00:40:24.650
While this scene did undergo a lot of changes,
one story beat remained

00:40:24.650 --> 00:40:28.670
virtually unchanged from the very first roughed-in
version:

00:40:28.670 --> 00:40:34.940
the moment the Vortigaunt tosses a headcrab
carcass down to the player and yells 'Sustenance!'

00:40:34.940 --> 00:40:38.069
Right from the start, this moment always got
a great reaction

00:40:38.069 --> 00:40:41.790
from playtesters and remained, all the way
to the final product.

00:40:41.790 --> 00:40:44.130
a2_04_armored_headcrabs1
We spend time on every game design element

00:40:44.130 --> 00:40:48.109
thinking about how the player
is expected to learn of its existence, and

00:40:48.109 --> 00:40:49.569
understand its details.

00:40:49.569 --> 00:40:52.549
Some design elements are so important that
the game experience will 'break'

00:40:52.549 --> 00:40:56.260
if the player doesn't understand them, while
others may just be fun diversions,

00:40:56.260 --> 00:40:58.820
or rewards for exploring the game's systems.

00:40:58.820 --> 00:41:03.329
Any required element will require work on
our part to teach the player.

00:41:03.329 --> 00:41:06.400
In Half-Life, we try to teach elements in
ways that feel as natural as

00:41:06.400 --> 00:41:09.990
possible, entwined within the core flow of
the game so that you don't feel like

00:41:09.990 --> 00:41:12.130
the game just stopped to teach you something.

00:41:12.130 --> 00:41:16.380
We don't always succeed, but the attempts
usually lead us somewhere interesting.

00:41:16.380 --> 00:41:19.829
In this case, we need to teach the players
about the Armored Headcrab.

00:41:19.829 --> 00:41:22.680
The key elements that we want them to understand
are that the headcrab is

00:41:22.680 --> 00:41:26.279
invulnerable on the top, but has a weak spot
on its belly.

00:41:26.279 --> 00:41:29.250
We would also like the player to learn that
the headcrab exposes its weak

00:41:29.250 --> 00:41:33.289
spot right before it jumps, but this isn't
required, the player can fall back

00:41:33.289 --> 00:41:36.890
to shooting the weak spot as the headcrab
scrambles around after a leap.

00:41:36.890 --> 00:41:39.210
a2_04_armored_headcrabs2
Teaching design elements of enemies is always

00:41:39.210 --> 00:41:40.210
tricky.

00:41:40.210 --> 00:41:41.880
Players have shown us for years that it's
really

00:41:41.880 --> 00:41:44.880
hard to learn something new while under attack.

00:41:44.880 --> 00:41:47.841
We've also seen that invulnerability is a
particularly

00:41:47.841 --> 00:41:49.530
difficult enemy feature to teach.

00:41:49.530 --> 00:41:52.880
If players are blasting away at an enemy,
they're often not

00:41:52.880 --> 00:41:55.890
paying close attention to the impact feedback.

00:41:55.890 --> 00:41:59.380
If the enemy is vulnerable in some areas and
not others, players may

00:41:59.380 --> 00:42:02.510
kill the enemy with a spray of gunfire, not
realizing that some of

00:42:02.510 --> 00:42:06.670
their shots did nothing while others hit the
critical weak spots.

00:42:06.670 --> 00:42:09.530
So when we approached the Armored headcrab
training, we knew we'd

00:42:09.530 --> 00:42:11.450
need to do significant work.

00:42:11.450 --> 00:42:13.920
The first step was to avoid placing the player
under duress

00:42:13.920 --> 00:42:17.269
by containing the enemies inside this wire
fence.

00:42:17.269 --> 00:42:20.970
Next, we placed headcrabs at eye level facing
the player,

00:42:20.970 --> 00:42:23.410
exposing their bright, pulsing weak spot.

00:42:23.410 --> 00:42:25.911
If the player shoots one immediately, they're
rewarded with a

00:42:25.911 --> 00:42:29.250
unique death effect where the headcrab explodes
into chunks.

00:42:29.250 --> 00:42:32.440
This effect is designed to show the player
they did something special to kill

00:42:32.440 --> 00:42:36.250
this headcrab, as opposed to how they've been
killing headcrabs up until now.

00:42:36.250 --> 00:42:39.690
There are a couple of other pieces in the
setup here that are important.

00:42:39.690 --> 00:42:42.950
A second headcrab is placed right behind the
lock the player must shoot

00:42:42.950 --> 00:42:47.170
to enter the cage, encouraging the player
to notice the weak spot and try shooting it.

00:42:47.170 --> 00:42:49.490
Two more headcrabs are loose in the cage,
scripted to

00:42:49.490 --> 00:42:52.779
repeatedly attack invisible points on the
wire fence.

00:42:52.779 --> 00:42:55.450
This ensures they're constantly demonstrating
the way they expose

00:42:55.450 --> 00:42:58.589
their weak points before they leap, and this
allows the player to

00:42:58.589 --> 00:43:01.390
practice hitting the weak spot from behind
the safety of the fence.

00:43:01.390 --> 00:43:04.750
a2_04_armored_headcrabs3
With any section of natural, entwined training,

00:43:04.750 --> 00:43:08.250
we've observed that there's
rarely any one thing that succeeds in teaching

00:43:08.250 --> 00:43:11.910
all players,
nor do 100% of players leave with the perfect

00:43:11.910 --> 00:43:14.099
understanding we'd like.

00:43:14.099 --> 00:43:18.240
If player understanding is absolutely critical,
we have to build a more bulletproof

00:43:18.240 --> 00:43:23.250
gate that ensures the player demonstrates
understanding before moving onwards.

00:43:23.250 --> 00:43:28.400
In fact, we do just that when the player acquires
the shotgun in the next area.

00:43:28.400 --> 00:43:31.680
In the case of the armored headcrab, however,
playtesting showed that we

00:43:31.680 --> 00:43:35.880
could afford to let a few players through
with less than perfect understanding.

00:43:35.880 --> 00:43:39.559
This section gave them enough of a chance
to experiment that they got a sense of

00:43:39.559 --> 00:43:44.359
what was going on, and further encounters
with armored headcrabs solidified it.

00:43:44.359 --> 00:43:46.970
There was still a lot of iteration on the
visual design of the

00:43:46.970 --> 00:43:49.390
headcrab to further drive home its armor and
weak point,

00:43:49.390 --> 00:43:52.619
and we also added a custom response specific
to shotgun fire.

00:43:52.619 --> 00:43:57.500
In that case, the armored headcrab flips over
and wriggles for a second, exposing its weak

00:43:57.500 --> 00:43:58.560
point.

00:43:58.560 --> 00:44:01.170
This was added to reduce the resource loss
we saw happening in

00:44:01.170 --> 00:44:04.030
players who were still struggling to understand
the armored headcrab

00:44:04.030 --> 00:44:06.250
mechanics after they acquired the shotgun.

00:44:06.250 --> 00:44:08.260
a2_04_shotgun_gate1
One of the challenges we always face when

00:44:08.260 --> 00:44:12.010
giving a new weapon or tool
to the player is ensuring that they collect

00:44:12.010 --> 00:44:14.420
it before moving on.

00:44:14.420 --> 00:44:17.609
In these cases, we try to establish some kind
of 'gate' that

00:44:17.609 --> 00:44:21.130
the player can only get past using the new
item.

00:44:21.130 --> 00:44:24.299
This can be especially tricky in cases where
we are giving the player a

00:44:24.299 --> 00:44:28.500
new tool that doesn't fundamentally change
their capabilities, as in the

00:44:28.500 --> 00:44:31.160
case of the shotgun, where almost everything
that can be done

00:44:31.160 --> 00:44:34.099
with the shotgun can also be done with the
pistol.

00:44:34.099 --> 00:44:38.349
In contrast, the pistol gate, where the player
had to acquire the pistol

00:44:38.349 --> 00:44:41.710
before being able to shoot the lock off of
the fence back at Russell's lab,

00:44:41.710 --> 00:44:44.390
was simple to design and execute.

00:44:44.390 --> 00:44:48.309
For this shotgun gate, we not only needed
to ensure that the player collected

00:44:48.309 --> 00:44:52.950
the shotgun, but also that they learned how
to load it before proceeding.

00:44:52.950 --> 00:44:56.140
We playtested a number of approaches to teaching
the reload mechanics

00:44:56.140 --> 00:44:59.650
during combat, and repeatedly found that it
didn't work.

00:44:59.650 --> 00:45:04.039
When faced with learning a new, complex task
under duress, players would

00:45:04.039 --> 00:45:07.770
retreat back to using their pistol, which
was a known quantity to them.

00:45:07.770 --> 00:45:09.630
a2_04_shotgun_gate2
Once the player has collected the shotgun,

00:45:09.630 --> 00:45:13.069
they are only halfway through the shotgun
gate.

00:45:13.069 --> 00:45:15.619
Next, they need to learn how to reload it.

00:45:15.619 --> 00:45:18.460
To drive home the necessity for the player
to learn how to use this

00:45:18.460 --> 00:45:21.900
new weapon, we added an incoming threat, using
a variety

00:45:21.900 --> 00:45:24.319
of audio and visual effects to sell it.

00:45:24.319 --> 00:45:26.670
While our goal is to make the player feel
like they have a

00:45:26.670 --> 00:45:30.119
limited amount of time, the time pressure
is entirely fake,

00:45:30.119 --> 00:45:32.910
and everything proceeds based on the player's
progress in loading

00:45:32.910 --> 00:45:35.599
the shotgun for the first time.

00:45:35.599 --> 00:45:38.940
Once the shotgun has been loaded, a short
timer starts,

00:45:38.940 --> 00:45:41.180
after which zombies burst through the walls.

00:45:41.180 --> 00:45:44.410
If the player happens to look at the walls
before that timer is up,

00:45:44.410 --> 00:45:47.130
we abort the timer and introduce the zombies.

00:45:47.130 --> 00:45:50.380
The combination of a timer and a look trigger
is a common trick used

00:45:50.380 --> 00:45:53.779
in places where we'd like the player to see
a neat visual moment,

00:45:53.779 --> 00:45:57.480
but we can't wait forever or the game simply
won't progress.

00:45:57.480 --> 00:45:59.289
The breaking of the walls by the zombies is
the

00:45:59.289 --> 00:46:02.309
final step in opening the shotgun gate.

00:46:02.309 --> 00:46:04.500
Having new enemies open the way forward when
they enter the

00:46:04.500 --> 00:46:07.640
player's space is a fairly tried and true
method,

00:46:07.640 --> 00:46:09.700
but it's never one we're particularly happy
with.

00:46:09.700 --> 00:46:14.030
It's always a bit too convenient, and lacks
agency on the player's part.

00:46:14.030 --> 00:46:17.210
But in a setup like this, which contains a
novel puzzle,

00:46:17.210 --> 00:46:20.640
new weapon and its associated mechanics, plus
an ambush,

00:46:20.640 --> 00:46:23.109
we're already dealing with a lot of design
complexity

00:46:23.109 --> 00:46:26.140
packed into a small space, and further additions
would

00:46:26.140 --> 00:46:28.260
complicated the scenario even further.

00:46:28.260 --> 00:46:30.549
a2_04_shotgun_design1
The visual design of the shotgun included

00:46:30.549 --> 00:46:32.809
a number of challenges.

00:46:32.809 --> 00:46:38.080
Shotguns in games are generally powerful,
and we communicate that power with bulk.

00:46:38.080 --> 00:46:41.640
As a result, they're big, and held in two
hands.

00:46:41.640 --> 00:46:47.930
But our experiments with two-handed VR weaponry
didn't reach a level we were satisfied with.

00:46:47.930 --> 00:46:51.859
When holding a two handed object in real life,
the object itself provides a

00:46:51.859 --> 00:46:55.790
physical connection between the two hands,
and that constraint allows

00:46:55.790 --> 00:47:01.730
players to stop thinking about it consciously,
in addition to providing physical feedback.

00:47:01.730 --> 00:47:05.420
In VR, without that connection or feedback,
we found that players had to

00:47:05.420 --> 00:47:08.510
keep maintaining their own internal sense
of how they were holding their

00:47:08.510 --> 00:47:15.019
two handed weapon, or they'd drift their hands
into positions that were hard for us to reconcile.

00:47:15.019 --> 00:47:20.109
As a result, our two handed weapons required
players to 'play along' more than we liked.

00:47:20.109 --> 00:47:24.260
Since one of our core goals with Half-Life:
Alyx was to allow players to

00:47:24.260 --> 00:47:27.549
fully immerse themselves by ceasing to think
about what they were doing

00:47:27.549 --> 00:47:32.250
in real life, we decided to focus on single
handed weapons, including the shotgun.

00:47:32.250 --> 00:47:34.150
a2_04_shotgun_design2
Our initial instinct with the shotgun design

00:47:34.150 --> 00:47:38.500
was to use a regular real-world
shotgun, but modified with a sawn-off stock

00:47:38.500 --> 00:47:41.000
and barrel so it
would seem less cumbersome when held with

00:47:41.000 --> 00:47:42.000
one hand.

00:47:42.000 --> 00:47:44.820
This approach always resulted in the handle
being at the rear of the weapon

00:47:44.820 --> 00:47:48.559
and we ran into the problem where the weapon
always felt front heavy.

00:47:48.559 --> 00:47:52.250
Obviously, the VR controller in your hand
wouldn't reflect that, leading

00:47:52.250 --> 00:47:56.380
to a disconnect between what you were feeling
and what you were seeing.

00:47:56.380 --> 00:48:00.750
Placing the handle more centrally, and underneath
the weapon, helped avoid this disconnect.

00:48:00.750 --> 00:48:04.490
This implied center of mass wasn't really
maintained well as the weapon

00:48:04.490 --> 00:48:09.029
accrued upgrades throughout the game, but
the first impression was the most critical.

00:48:09.029 --> 00:48:12.079
Since we were going with an odd form, we were
very keen to use common shotgun

00:48:12.079 --> 00:48:16.070
visual elements wherever we could, to help
with player recognition and to ground the

00:48:16.070 --> 00:48:18.319
design in some reality.

00:48:18.319 --> 00:48:22.059
One element we borrowed was a common shotgun
tube magazine that stores the

00:48:22.059 --> 00:48:25.390
shells horizontally in line with the barrel,
and we were just able to fit

00:48:25.390 --> 00:48:30.109
the gameplay requirement of 6 shells without
the overall length becoming unwieldy.

00:48:30.109 --> 00:48:33.579
You can see the effort we had to go to for
further shell capacity in the design of our

00:48:33.579 --> 00:48:35.369
autoloader upgrade.

00:48:35.369 --> 00:48:40.859
Our first reloading mechanic was based on
the realistic insertion of one shell at a

00:48:40.859 --> 00:48:41.859
time.

00:48:41.859 --> 00:48:44.099
Playtesters found this to be overly tedious,
although we did receive some

00:48:44.099 --> 00:48:47.349
positive comments that it made them feel like
an action movie star.

00:48:47.349 --> 00:48:50.890
The simple addition of allowing players to
insert two shells at a time solved

00:48:50.890 --> 00:48:54.930
this issue, allowing us to keep the good part
of the design whilst losing the bad.

00:48:54.930 --> 00:48:59.069
We were keen to reinforce the whole action
movie star thing and

00:48:59.069 --> 00:49:02.920
experimented with some big gesture the player
could do to finalize reloading.

00:49:02.920 --> 00:49:06.710
The inclusion of a pop-up mechanism allowed
us to also add an additional

00:49:06.710 --> 00:49:10.119
step to the reload that further separated
it from our other weapons

00:49:10.119 --> 00:49:13.380
yet had a satisfying flick of the wrist to
complete the action.

00:49:13.380 --> 00:49:15.829
a2_04_darkness_gate
As with the shotgun and the pistol, we needed

00:49:15.829 --> 00:49:18.940
a gate for the flashlight
to ensure the player couldn't proceed without

00:49:18.940 --> 00:49:19.950
it.

00:49:19.950 --> 00:49:22.759
We tried a number of designs around the moment
where the player collected

00:49:22.759 --> 00:49:25.809
the flashlight, but they all ended up being
about the player picking

00:49:25.809 --> 00:49:30.599
up an object, and not specific to that object
being a flashlight.

00:49:30.599 --> 00:49:33.880
Given that the flashlight's primary function
is to provide light,

00:49:33.880 --> 00:49:36.990
we felt the gate needed to be an area of darkness.

00:49:36.990 --> 00:49:40.569
We also really wanted the player to be excited
to get the flashlight, and

00:49:40.569 --> 00:49:44.910
that meant they had to experience darkness
before collecting the flashlight.

00:49:44.910 --> 00:49:47.670
This turned out to be an interesting challenge,
because we already had many

00:49:47.670 --> 00:49:51.890
poorly lit areas prior to this, and now we
needed an area that was so dark

00:49:51.890 --> 00:49:55.099
that players would correctly interpret it
as a gate.

00:49:55.099 --> 00:49:59.670
Our initial implementation was simply to ensure
there were no lights within the hallway.

00:49:59.670 --> 00:50:03.630
Unfortunately, playtesting immediately showed
that wasn't enough.

00:50:03.630 --> 00:50:07.520
Some VR headsets have extremely low black
levels, and on those headsets,

00:50:07.520 --> 00:50:11.990
enough light bounced in from this lit area
to enable players to move forward.

00:50:11.990 --> 00:50:15.830
In addition, our low ground fog provided just
enough contrast in the dark

00:50:15.830 --> 00:50:19.730
hallway for players to make out the scene
and stumble through it.

00:50:19.730 --> 00:50:22.619
Even once we removed fog and bounce light
in this area,

00:50:22.619 --> 00:50:26.079
we saw yet other ways that players tried to
move forward.

00:50:26.079 --> 00:50:30.660
Some players would fire their pistol repeatedly,
using the muzzle flash as a strobing light

00:50:30.660 --> 00:50:31.859
source.

00:50:31.859 --> 00:50:35.069
Others would use the teleport targeting UI
to probe the room, because at

00:50:35.069 --> 00:50:39.040
that point in the project, it had a small
light source attached to it.

00:50:39.040 --> 00:50:44.359
In the end, we disabled muzzle flashes inside
the hallway, and the teleport UI lost its

00:50:44.359 --> 00:50:45.359
light.

00:50:45.359 --> 00:50:48.740
The final step was the addition of some dialog
from Alyx,

00:50:48.740 --> 00:50:51.839
commenting on the darkness and her need for
a light source.

00:50:51.839 --> 00:50:54.940
This was aimed at solving a perceptual problem.

00:50:54.940 --> 00:50:57.960
Up until now, our game has taught players
that their goal is to continue

00:50:57.960 --> 00:51:02.170
moving forward, and any time that forward
progress is blocked,

00:51:02.170 --> 00:51:05.980
it's because there's something in the local
area that needs solving.

00:51:05.980 --> 00:51:08.970
Given that structure, it's entirely rational
for players to do everything

00:51:08.970 --> 00:51:12.410
they can to find some way to see in the darkness
ahead.

00:51:12.410 --> 00:51:16.049
Alyx's dialog is aimed at telling the player
that the game has recognized

00:51:16.049 --> 00:51:20.869
their attempts to move forward here, but the
solution lies elsewhere.

00:51:20.869 --> 00:51:23.359
Subsequent playtesting showed us that this
dialog, coupled with the

00:51:23.359 --> 00:51:26.730
brightly lit area containing zombies off to
the right,

00:51:26.730 --> 00:51:30.710
helped tug players past the darkness entrance,
and on towards the flashlight.

00:51:30.710 --> 00:51:33.109
a2_04_toner_airlock
Like hacking and environmental puzzles, toner

00:51:33.109 --> 00:51:35.980
puzzles like this one are often
used to relieve tension from combat and engage

00:51:35.980 --> 00:51:38.160
the player's mind in a refreshing way.

00:51:38.160 --> 00:51:40.910
This puzzle introduces a new junction type,
which has two inputs

00:51:40.910 --> 00:51:43.160
and two outputs, as well as branching circuits.

00:51:43.160 --> 00:51:45.720
Functionally, it is just a simple 'airlock.'

00:51:45.720 --> 00:51:49.109
Only one of the main Combine gates can be
powered on at a time,

00:51:49.109 --> 00:51:51.029
with an additional wrinkle that the smaller
side gate

00:51:51.029 --> 00:51:53.710
on the return path must be powered down separately.

00:51:53.710 --> 00:51:56.660
In addition to providing a break between hectic
combat encounters,

00:51:56.660 --> 00:51:59.680
this puzzle is a gameplay mechanic refresher,
since it's been some time since

00:51:59.680 --> 00:52:03.269
the player has seen a toner puzzle and the
next ones start to ramp up in complexity.

00:52:03.269 --> 00:52:05.720
a2_04_flashlight1
In prior Half-Life games, the flashlight was

00:52:05.720 --> 00:52:08.920
attached to the player's
body, which meant that it always illuminated

00:52:08.920 --> 00:52:12.810
the world from the same angle,
relative to the player's point of view.

00:52:12.810 --> 00:52:16.609
With tracked hands in VR, we had the opportunity
to let players manipulate the

00:52:16.609 --> 00:52:21.349
flashlight directly and playtesters reacted
very positively to this new freedom.

00:52:21.349 --> 00:52:25.190
In fact, our earliest implementation of a
flashlight in Half-Life: Alyx

00:52:25.190 --> 00:52:29.080
was an object that could be picked up in either
hand or even set back down.

00:52:29.080 --> 00:52:31.849
This allowed the player to put the flashlight
down somewhere, providing

00:52:31.849 --> 00:52:35.339
illumination for themselves while they accomplished
another task.

00:52:35.339 --> 00:52:38.490
Unfortunately, this presented the possibility
that a player could leave the

00:52:38.490 --> 00:52:43.000
flashlight behind, which would have been a
game-breaking scenario.

00:52:43.000 --> 00:52:46.029
Implementing the flashlight as an object players
had to hold, also prevented

00:52:46.029 --> 00:52:49.671
them from holding something else in that hand,
which was tedious to manage and simply too

00:52:49.671 --> 00:52:51.480
punishing of a trade-off.

00:52:51.480 --> 00:52:54.730
In particular, it meant that weapon reloading
was a nightmare, because the

00:52:54.730 --> 00:52:59.319
player no longer had a free off-hand to perform
the reload actions.

00:52:59.319 --> 00:53:01.150
In the end, we settled on a flashlight that
attaches to the

00:53:01.150 --> 00:53:04.349
off hand and turns on and off 'automatically.'

00:53:04.349 --> 00:53:07.200
This fixes the issues with reloading in the
dark, potentially losing the

00:53:07.200 --> 00:53:10.630
flashlight and has the nice side effect of
not requiring the allocation of a

00:53:10.630 --> 00:53:15.430
button to toggle the light, which is especially
helpful on controllers with fewer buttons.

00:53:15.430 --> 00:53:18.829
Since the automatic on/off behavior is controlled
using level logic,

00:53:18.829 --> 00:53:23.009
designers could make performance and quality
trade-offs in different areas of the game.

00:53:23.009 --> 00:53:25.900
After all, if a player could turn the flashlight
on at any time,

00:53:25.900 --> 00:53:28.589
the rendering overhead of this shadow-casting
light source would

00:53:28.589 --> 00:53:31.559
have to be accounted for throughout the entire
game.

00:53:31.559 --> 00:53:34.910
On the other hand, if the flashlight is known
to be off in a given area,

00:53:34.910 --> 00:53:37.730
a designer can make use of the resulting rendering
headroom by

00:53:37.730 --> 00:53:40.000
increasing the fidelity of the environment
in other ways.

00:53:40.000 --> 00:53:42.210
a2_04_flashlight2
Shortly after adding the flashlight to the

00:53:42.210 --> 00:53:45.299
game, we discovered that
playtesters would frequently brace their gun

00:53:45.299 --> 00:53:47.309
hand with their flashlight hand.

00:53:47.309 --> 00:53:49.690
This meant that players had to orient their
flashlight hand

00:53:49.690 --> 00:53:52.319
awkwardly to align the light with their aim.

00:53:52.319 --> 00:53:55.309
We decided to make this an automatic behavior
of the off hand

00:53:55.309 --> 00:53:58.190
whenever it was brought close to the grip
of the gun.

00:53:58.190 --> 00:54:00.569
If you do this, you'll see that the off hand
grips the gun

00:54:00.569 --> 00:54:04.809
in a natural pose and the flashlight aligns
with the aim.

00:54:04.809 --> 00:54:08.109
In fact, if you look closely at the end of
the flashlight,

00:54:08.109 --> 00:54:10.430
you'll notice that it has a ball joint.

00:54:10.430 --> 00:54:13.089
This allows the flashlight to articulate based
on whether

00:54:13.089 --> 00:54:15.380
the off hand hand is gripping a gun or not.

00:54:15.380 --> 00:54:20.790
The ball joint has four different orientations:
one for each gun and a neutral orientation

00:54:20.790 --> 00:54:24.180
for when the player
is not supporting the gun to aim.

00:54:24.180 --> 00:54:27.460
These four orientations ensure that the flashlight
is always

00:54:27.460 --> 00:54:30.730
pointed in an ideal direction for the player.

00:54:30.730 --> 00:54:33.789
In one-handed mode, the flashlight attaches
to the gun hand

00:54:33.789 --> 00:54:36.319
at an orientation that is ideal for all scenarios.

00:54:36.319 --> 00:54:38.359
a2_04_sandwiches
When the writers create a new character, we

00:54:38.359 --> 00:54:40.990
try to write a variety of
material from that character's perspective

00:54:40.990 --> 00:54:42.619
to help find their voice.

00:54:42.619 --> 00:54:43.829
What motivates them?

00:54:43.829 --> 00:54:44.829
What do they sound like?

00:54:44.829 --> 00:54:46.740
How do they relate to other characters?

00:54:46.740 --> 00:54:50.099
It's helpful to know the answers to questions
like this early in a project,

00:54:50.099 --> 00:54:53.780
particularly when working with a team, where
different people may end up writing for the

00:54:53.780 --> 00:54:54.780
character.

00:54:54.780 --> 00:54:56.420
The 'Club Sandwich' story that Russell tells
in this

00:54:56.420 --> 00:54:59.019
section started out as one of these writing
exercises.

00:54:59.019 --> 00:55:03.009
The writing team wrote pages and pages of
Russell telling Alyx stories

00:55:03.009 --> 00:55:05.849
about life before the Combine, getting irritated
with her;

00:55:05.849 --> 00:55:09.160
telling bad jokes, pretty much the whole emotional
spectrum.

00:55:09.160 --> 00:55:12.730
As we refined Russell's character, almost
all of it fell by the wayside.

00:55:12.730 --> 00:55:16.300
We’d needed to do that work to get to Russell,
but most of that didn't sound like Russell

00:55:16.300 --> 00:55:17.839
anymore.

00:55:17.839 --> 00:55:20.780
Later in the project, we decided to make Alyx
scared of the dark.

00:55:20.780 --> 00:55:24.329
So we needed Russell to talk a lot in this
section to keep Alyx company.

00:55:24.329 --> 00:55:27.220
It didn't matter what he was saying, it just
had to be this voice in

00:55:27.220 --> 00:55:29.680
the darkness that she was grabbing on to.

00:55:29.680 --> 00:55:32.329
And then we remembered:
'Hey, didn't we write a rambling sandwich

00:55:32.329 --> 00:55:34.250
monologue a year ago?'

00:55:34.250 --> 00:55:36.010
And the Club Sandwich speech was resurrected.

00:55:36.010 --> 00:55:38.119
a2_05_budgeting
When building a Half-Life game, we try to

00:55:38.119 --> 00:55:41.859
start playtesting as soon
as we can, even when we only have 10 or 15

00:55:41.859 --> 00:55:43.089
minutes of gameplay built.

00:55:43.089 --> 00:55:47.200
We find it extremely helpful to see how players
are reacting to what we've made as soon as

00:55:47.200 --> 00:55:48.320
possible.

00:55:48.320 --> 00:55:51.779
One of the major ways playtesting impacted
Half-Life: Alyx was in revealing

00:55:51.779 --> 00:55:56.029
how players reacted to the density of interactive
elements in the world.

00:55:56.029 --> 00:55:58.900
Before we began, we were already aware that
players would likely spend

00:55:58.900 --> 00:56:01.980
more time paying attention to the details
of the world around them in VR,

00:56:01.980 --> 00:56:05.049
but we were unprepared for how significant
this would be.

00:56:05.049 --> 00:56:09.230
Seeing some players spend thirty minutes messing
around with a single interactive object really

00:56:09.230 --> 00:56:10.790
opened our eyes.

00:56:10.790 --> 00:56:13.770
As a result of those playtesting observations,
we spent much of our

00:56:13.770 --> 00:56:16.609
development time focusing on the environment
around the player,

00:56:16.609 --> 00:56:19.260
and the level of detail and interactivity
to be found there.

00:56:19.260 --> 00:56:22.109
It might seem surprising that we didn't start
with the assumption that

00:56:22.109 --> 00:56:24.990
we would need to spend that amount of time
on the world, but the reality

00:56:24.990 --> 00:56:27.970
of game development is that we always have
a finite amount of time

00:56:27.970 --> 00:56:30.779
to build the game and we're always making
trade-offs.

00:56:30.779 --> 00:56:33.230
In the ideal case, we spend our finite time
on whatever

00:56:33.230 --> 00:56:36.289
contributes the most to the player's experience.

00:56:36.289 --> 00:56:39.230
So, playtesting early, and thereby getting
a better sense of what elements

00:56:39.230 --> 00:56:43.230
are contributing to the player experience,
helps us understand where to spend the limited

00:56:43.230 --> 00:56:44.450
time we have.

00:56:44.450 --> 00:56:47.700
In this case, those early playtests told us
to significantly increase

00:56:47.700 --> 00:56:50.769
the amount we should budget for creating world
detail and interactivity.

00:56:50.769 --> 00:56:54.039
a2_05_barnacle_sounds
Barnacles are a unique enemy type in Half-Life:

00:56:54.039 --> 00:56:57.290
Alyx in that they are
static and often situated above the player's

00:56:57.290 --> 00:56:58.859
natural view direction.

00:56:58.859 --> 00:57:03.550
During testing in confined or dimly lit spaces,
we often saw players walk into

00:57:03.550 --> 00:57:06.310
Barnacle tongues without noticing them.

00:57:06.310 --> 00:57:08.680
One way we addressed this was with audio.

00:57:08.680 --> 00:57:12.119
In particular, we made idle Barnacles emit
subtle breathing,

00:57:12.119 --> 00:57:23.769
croaking and grumbling sounds like the following.

00:57:23.769 --> 00:57:27.880
These sounds helped alert players to the presence
of Barnacles and also made

00:57:27.880 --> 00:57:31.950
them seem like living creatures that were
always active, not just a mechanical

00:57:31.950 --> 00:57:34.950
piece of gameplay that responded only to the
player's actions.

00:57:34.950 --> 00:57:39.410
The Head Related Transfer Functions available
in Steam Audio were especially

00:57:39.410 --> 00:57:43.180
helpful, as they made it possible to accurately
spatialize Barnacle sounds

00:57:43.180 --> 00:57:46.279
so that the players could tell that the sounds
were coming from above.

00:57:46.279 --> 00:57:48.799
a2_05_manhack_introduction
Early incarnations of Manhacks were frustrating

00:57:48.799 --> 00:57:52.490
to fight in VR, since
their audio was not providing enough information

00:57:52.490 --> 00:57:53.849
about their location.

00:57:53.849 --> 00:57:57.950
They seemed to appear from nowhere and, even
when close to the player,

00:57:57.950 --> 00:58:00.410
they lacked a dramatic presence.

00:58:00.410 --> 00:58:04.869
We tried a variety of layered sounds with
varying distance falloffs but

00:58:04.869 --> 00:58:10.380
this often created more confusion, especially
when multiple Manhacks were nearby.

00:58:10.380 --> 00:58:14.109
Our ultimate solution to the problem consisted
of two parts.

00:58:14.109 --> 00:58:19.279
The first was to make Manhacks emit the following
alarm-like warning sound

00:58:19.279 --> 00:58:26.390
when they first came into range or were deployed
by a Combine soldier.

00:58:26.390 --> 00:58:31.450
The second part of our solution was to add
more aggressive looping sounds with short

00:58:31.450 --> 00:58:33.230
falloff distances.

00:58:33.230 --> 00:58:40.460
The short falloff of these sound loops meant
that this specific component

00:58:40.460 --> 00:58:45.099
of Manhacks' sound treatment would only be
audible quite near the player.

00:58:45.099 --> 00:58:49.499
Thus, when players heard these sounds, they
knew that Manhacks had begun

00:58:49.499 --> 00:58:53.069
hunting them, and one or more was getting
close enough to be of concern.

00:58:53.069 --> 00:58:55.750
a2_05_exterior_pacing
Before final art was added, the cramped, dark

00:58:55.750 --> 00:58:59.569
areas of this level did not take players very
long to traverse.

00:58:59.569 --> 00:59:05.130
But as the fidelity of the game increased,
players spent more and more time getting to

00:59:05.130 --> 00:59:06.130
this point.

00:59:06.130 --> 00:59:10.260
As we've learned over the years, too much
time in dark,

00:59:10.260 --> 00:59:15.210
underground areas can become fatiguing to
players, and this is even more significant

00:59:15.210 --> 00:59:16.299
in VR.

00:59:16.299 --> 00:59:22.410
This outdoor section provides a change of
pace and a brief respite from the dark.

00:59:22.410 --> 00:59:27.650
Nt only does it give players something visually
different and interesting to look at,

00:59:27.650 --> 00:59:32.520
it allows them to clearly see and assess threats
in an open space,

00:59:32.520 --> 00:59:36.240
allowing them to get some easy kills with
little stress.

00:59:36.240 --> 00:59:39.300
It was also important to provide some variety
here,

00:59:39.300 --> 00:59:45.510
as players are about to head into a dark area
that requires precision shooting and flashlight

00:59:45.510 --> 00:59:46.510
usage.

00:59:46.510 --> 00:59:48.800
a2_05_xen_barrels
This section, with a multitude of explosive

00:59:48.800 --> 00:59:52.630
barrels embedded in Xen
overgrowth, was designed to switch up player

00:59:52.630 --> 00:59:56.049
expectations
and change the pacing of the level.

00:59:56.049 --> 00:59:58.100
Prior to this point in the game, explosive
barrels

00:59:58.100 --> 01:00:01.369
and canisters were used as offensive weapons.

01:00:01.369 --> 01:00:06.579
In this space, they are deadly to the player;
if a barrel or canister does explode, it sets

01:00:06.579 --> 01:00:09.160
off a fatal chain reaction.

01:00:09.160 --> 01:00:11.619
This change in priority forces players to
aim

01:00:11.619 --> 01:00:15.309
carefully and adds intensity without combat.

01:00:15.309 --> 01:00:20.970
This has the positive side effect of training
players to utilize the pistol even more effectively.

01:00:20.970 --> 01:00:24.190
While the automatic alignment of the flashlight
with a braced weapon is

01:00:24.190 --> 01:00:28.330
useful in direct combat, in this section we
observed players taking

01:00:28.330 --> 01:00:31.370
advantage of the decoupled weapon and flashlight
as they

01:00:31.370 --> 01:00:35.560
illuminated and targeted Barnacles in the
intricate Xen foliage overhead.

01:00:35.560 --> 01:00:37.700
a2_05_volumetric_lighting
We decided early on that the environments

01:00:37.700 --> 01:00:42.220
we wanted to portray in Half-Life: Alyx
would benefit from an investment in high quality

01:00:42.220 --> 01:00:43.920
volumetric lighting.

01:00:43.920 --> 01:00:47.910
This component of illumination is an indispensable
tool not just for creating

01:00:47.910 --> 01:00:52.320
atmosphere, but for drawing the player's attention
to critical gameplay elements.

01:00:52.320 --> 01:00:54.869
Our system works in three phases.

01:00:54.869 --> 01:00:59.930
In the first phase, illumination from static
light sources is captured in a

01:00:59.930 --> 01:01:03.190
three-level hierarchical volumetric clipmap
surrounding the player.

01:01:03.190 --> 01:01:07.519
The highest-resolution level of the clip map
measures one cubic inch per voxel.

01:01:07.519 --> 01:01:10.900
The clipmap is updated incrementally, less
than once per frame,

01:01:10.900 --> 01:01:12.789
as the player moves throughout the scene.

01:01:12.789 --> 01:01:16.930
In the second phase, a compute shader ray-marches
in view space throughout

01:01:16.930 --> 01:01:21.500
the clipmaps, accumulating static lighting,
dynamic lighting and volumetric extinction.

01:01:21.500 --> 01:01:26.640
The ray marching is performed every frame
and employs temporal anti-aliasing to improve

01:01:26.640 --> 01:01:27.819
quality.

01:01:27.819 --> 01:01:31.279
In the final phase, we re-sample lighting
and volumetric scattering

01:01:31.279 --> 01:01:35.059
from the prior phase for each eye, once per
frame.

01:01:35.059 --> 01:01:39.609
The result is a texture that describes, for
any given view-space depth,

01:01:39.609 --> 01:01:43.070
volumetric scattering and extinction, which
we apply during forward rendering.

01:01:43.070 --> 01:01:45.509
a2_06_combine_sighting
We always introduce new enemies at a distance

01:01:45.509 --> 01:01:47.680
and never
while the player is under duress.

01:01:47.680 --> 01:01:51.230
Here, the player is able to observe the Combine
soldiers killing some

01:01:51.230 --> 01:01:54.539
wandering zombies, giving the player an idea
of how powerful

01:01:54.539 --> 01:01:57.750
the soldiers and how they might operate in
combat.

01:01:57.750 --> 01:02:01.079
We located the Combine far enough away that
it was plausible that they

01:02:01.079 --> 01:02:04.050
would not notice the player, but close enough
that the player could

01:02:04.050 --> 01:02:08.000
choose to engage but remain relatively safe
from return fire.

01:02:08.000 --> 01:02:10.480
a2_06_single_combine
The first encounter with a Combine soldier

01:02:10.480 --> 01:02:13.410
caused a number of issues in playtesting.

01:02:13.410 --> 01:02:16.660
The biggest one was that players were often
surprised

01:02:16.660 --> 01:02:19.060
when the soldier started shooting at them.

01:02:19.060 --> 01:02:21.440
Some players who were new to the Half-Life
series

01:02:21.440 --> 01:02:23.849
had no idea who these soldiers were.

01:02:23.849 --> 01:02:28.329
But it's also been hours of game time since
players saw another human,

01:02:28.329 --> 01:02:32.920
and we found that many players weren't willing
to immediately start shooting.

01:02:32.920 --> 01:02:38.180
Some players also wondered why Alyx moved
so quickly to killing other humans.

01:02:38.180 --> 01:02:42.079
This area was designed to demonstrate that
Combine soldiers are hostile

01:02:42.079 --> 01:02:46.989
and will attack on sight, giving the player
a reason to want to shoot back.

01:02:46.989 --> 01:02:50.999
It starts with the dialog preceding the encounter,
where the player overhears

01:02:50.999 --> 01:02:55.039
the Combine discussing the problem of scavengers
in the Quarantine Zone.

01:02:55.039 --> 01:02:58.289
Then, when the player finally achieves visual
contact with

01:02:58.289 --> 01:03:01.299
their first Combine soldier, they witness
that soldier

01:03:01.299 --> 01:03:04.609
in the process of executing a civilian scavenger.

01:03:04.609 --> 01:03:06.940
a2_06_first_combine_skirmish
Squad soldier AI has been a mainstay of the

01:03:06.940 --> 01:03:10.739
Half-Life series, so we were
excited to see how their design would be impacted

01:03:10.739 --> 01:03:12.289
by the move to VR.

01:03:12.289 --> 01:03:15.089
Our early playtesting against the Half-Life
2 Combine soldier

01:03:15.089 --> 01:03:18.539
AI in VR immediately made a number of things
clear.

01:03:18.539 --> 01:03:24.660
First, players did not like to be shot at,
and often had a visceral negative response.

01:03:24.660 --> 01:03:28.779
The soldiers frequently ran at players at
full speed, firing all the while,

01:03:28.779 --> 01:03:33.470
and we discovered that the speed of Half-Life
2 combat was simply too overwhelming in VR.

01:03:33.470 --> 01:03:37.039
Also, players took longer to do everything
in VR, especially the core

01:03:37.039 --> 01:03:41.059
action of spotting and aiming their weapon
at an enemy while staying in cover.

01:03:41.059 --> 01:03:47.049
As a result, the soldiers tended to live much
longer in VR than they did in Half-Life 2.It

01:03:47.049 --> 01:03:49.980
was clear from these early playtests that
the most common reaction

01:03:49.980 --> 01:03:53.670
among players was to immediately seek cover
from enemy fire.

01:03:53.670 --> 01:03:56.829
So we started experimenting with changes to
the soldier AI, aimed at

01:03:56.829 --> 01:04:00.440
building a combat experience around players'
tendency to seek cover.

01:04:00.440 --> 01:04:03.339
Early experiments were focused on keeping
Combine soldiers at a comfortable

01:04:03.339 --> 01:04:05.990
distance from the player, while keeping them
moving around enough

01:04:05.990 --> 01:04:08.390
to not have combat become too static.

01:04:08.390 --> 01:04:12.000
To create lulls in firing for players to peek
out from cover,

01:04:12.000 --> 01:04:16.150
we prevented soldiers from shooting while
on the run in most cases.

01:04:16.150 --> 01:04:19.960
To keep players from remaining stationary,
we created a concept of 'flushing',

01:04:19.960 --> 01:04:23.400
where squads will attempt to flush the player
out of cover,

01:04:23.400 --> 01:04:27.670
and we gave each class of soldier a different
method of flushing out the player.

01:04:27.670 --> 01:04:31.220
But even in these early tests, it was also
clear that players weren't capable

01:04:31.220 --> 01:04:34.920
of fighting soldiers until they'd gotten skilled
in a number of basic combat

01:04:34.920 --> 01:04:38.369
actions, like reloading their gun without
having to focus all of their attention on

01:04:38.369 --> 01:04:39.589
it.

01:04:39.589 --> 01:04:43.279
So we were careful to slowly introduce the
various types of soldiers,

01:04:43.279 --> 01:04:46.420
and even the individual features within the
soldier classes.

01:04:46.420 --> 01:04:50.060
For example, these early fights against Combine
soldiers only feature the

01:04:50.060 --> 01:04:54.009
basic Grunts, and these particular Grunts
don't carry grenades.

01:04:54.009 --> 01:04:57.819
This means they don't actually have the capability
to flush a player out of cover.

01:04:57.819 --> 01:05:00.970
We found it was important in these first fights
to give players all the

01:05:00.970 --> 01:05:04.130
time they need to learn to use cover effectively,
before we start

01:05:04.130 --> 01:05:06.420
introducing enemies that could try and flush
them out.

01:05:06.420 --> 01:05:08.739
a2_06_train_crash
At this point in the game, we knew we needed

01:05:08.739 --> 01:05:12.789
a big set piece; a non-combat
moment to reward players for fighting their

01:05:12.789 --> 01:05:15.700
way through the dark, scary tunnels.

01:05:15.700 --> 01:05:19.369
Not only that, but the pace of the story was
leading up to its most

01:05:19.369 --> 01:05:24.670
important moment thus far: the rescue of Eli
Vance.

01:05:24.670 --> 01:05:27.860
Crashing the Combine prison train that was
transporting Eli felt like the

01:05:27.860 --> 01:05:32.510
exact right thing to accomplish these goals
and it gave us the opportunity to create some

01:05:32.510 --> 01:05:34.839
VR spectacle.

01:05:34.839 --> 01:05:38.519
We wanted to achieve the cinematic chaos of
causing a train wreck,

01:05:38.519 --> 01:05:41.960
while giving the players an active role in
the event.

01:05:41.960 --> 01:05:45.859
Our first attempts to design the scenario
were a challenge.

01:05:45.859 --> 01:05:49.420
It turned out that directing the player's
attention wasn't as simple as

01:05:49.420 --> 01:05:53.049
ensuring they were in the right position through
gameplay.

01:05:53.049 --> 01:05:58.710
In our initial tests, players frequently failed
to see some or all of the spectacular train

01:05:58.710 --> 01:05:59.869
wreck!

01:05:59.869 --> 01:06:03.690
The crash was one of the first destruction
sequences we put into the game,

01:06:03.690 --> 01:06:07.499
so we had a lot to learn about player behavior
in VR and how to

01:06:07.499 --> 01:06:11.269
direct the player naturally to immerse them
in the chaos.

01:06:11.269 --> 01:06:14.900
On top of that, to continually hit our target
frame rate, we needed to create a

01:06:14.900 --> 01:06:20.450
cascading series of events, so that only some
of the destruction was happening at any given

01:06:20.450 --> 01:06:21.450
time.

01:06:21.450 --> 01:06:24.999
Sound design, level design, effects and animation
had to be carefully

01:06:24.999 --> 01:06:29.599
coordinated to lead the player's attention
through each stage of the wreck.

01:06:29.599 --> 01:06:33.760
For example, at the start of the crash, we
used the sounds of train cars

01:06:33.760 --> 01:06:37.849
piling up in the distance, encouraging players
to look in that direction

01:06:37.849 --> 01:06:42.869
before one of the train cars lifts up and
blasts through the concrete connector.

01:06:42.869 --> 01:06:46.400
At the end of the sequence, the smokestack
collapse is used direct the

01:06:46.400 --> 01:06:49.520
player's attention to their eventual path
forward and we wanted

01:06:49.520 --> 01:06:52.180
players to witness its destruction.

01:06:52.180 --> 01:06:55.480
Before bringing down the smokestack, we gave
players a brief moment of

01:06:55.480 --> 01:07:00.940
rest and, again, use sound to draw their attention
to the smokestack.

01:07:00.940 --> 01:07:05.369
Initially, we used a rumbling sound preceding
its collapse, but we found

01:07:05.369 --> 01:07:09.450
this to be too subtle in practice and instead
added the transient explosion

01:07:09.450 --> 01:07:13.150
sound which drew player attention towards
the smokestack more reliably.

01:07:13.150 --> 01:07:15.829
a2_06_eli_rescue
For the rescue of Eli, we wanted to create

01:07:15.829 --> 01:07:20.240
a tense 'just out of reach'
moment where the player physically saves him.

01:07:20.240 --> 01:07:23.200
But, for the rest of the story to work, Alyx
and Eli had to

01:07:23.200 --> 01:07:26.180
take different paths at the end of the scene.

01:07:26.180 --> 01:07:29.560
We tried a few different approaches, including
a version where Eli was hanging

01:07:29.560 --> 01:07:32.720
from a ledge just in front of the player and
the player

01:07:32.720 --> 01:07:37.060
could reach down to attempt to grab Eli’s
hand to pull him up.

01:07:37.060 --> 01:07:41.299
In practice, however, players' hands would
collide with the real world floor.

01:07:41.299 --> 01:07:43.859
Not only did this make it impossible to reach
Eli,

01:07:43.859 --> 01:07:47.910
it took players out of the moment, wrecking
the emotion of the scene.

01:07:47.910 --> 01:07:51.270
Since Eli and Alyx had to take different paths,
we tried an approach

01:07:51.270 --> 01:07:55.730
where Alyx rescued Eli, but he was injured
and couldn't proceed.

01:07:55.730 --> 01:08:00.250
Unfortunately, many players would stick around,
not wanting to abandon him.

01:08:00.250 --> 01:08:04.859
Eventually, we arrived at the solution that
shipped in the final product.

01:08:04.859 --> 01:08:08.220
Separating Eli from the player with the Vortigaunt’s
rescue made for a much

01:08:08.220 --> 01:08:13.609
more meaningful return and fulfillment of
the Vortigaunt's promise to help Alyx.

01:08:13.609 --> 01:08:17.870
The datapod that Eli found was an addition
that not only let us integrate

01:08:17.870 --> 01:08:22.040
Eli back into the story later but also gave
him something to do so there

01:08:22.040 --> 01:08:26.480
was a much stronger reason why he would not
accompany the player on their journey going

01:08:26.480 --> 01:08:27.480
forward.

01:08:27.480 --> 01:08:28.790
a3_01_melee
Early on in the project, we spent some time

01:08:28.790 --> 01:08:32.010
experimenting
with the classic Half-Life crowbar.

01:08:32.010 --> 01:08:34.930
Playtests showed us that it was an interesting
tool for our player to use in

01:08:34.930 --> 01:08:39.400
puzzles and world interactions, but we weren't
very happy with the how our melee combat efforts

01:08:39.400 --> 01:08:40.400
turned out.

01:08:40.400 --> 01:08:43.160
We felt it was really important that the crowbar
was solid to the

01:08:43.160 --> 01:08:47.560
world and enemies, and that resulted in some
tricky physics challenges.

01:08:47.560 --> 01:08:50.430
Players could easily push the crowbar into
an enemy in front of them,

01:08:50.430 --> 01:08:52.740
because nothing in the real world would stop
it.

01:08:52.740 --> 01:08:57.870
Indeed, without that real-world physical feedback,
we found playtesters exhibited a wide variety

01:08:57.870 --> 01:08:59.790
of swing behaviors.

01:08:59.790 --> 01:09:04.180
Some would waggle the controller rapidly,
others would put their whole body into it.

01:09:04.180 --> 01:09:08.300
At the same time, playtest response to our
pistol prototype was very positive,

01:09:08.300 --> 01:09:11.630
especially around the more complex reloading
mechanics.

01:09:11.630 --> 01:09:15.310
Faced with a potentially significant R&amp;D problem
in melee combat,

01:09:15.310 --> 01:09:18.090
we chose to refocus our efforts on ranged
combat.

01:09:18.090 --> 01:09:22.370
It also didn't help that players all associated
the crowbar with Gordon Freeman, not Alyx

01:09:22.370 --> 01:09:23.370
Vance.

01:09:23.370 --> 01:09:24.931
a3_01_vault_view
This entire section of the map, from the start

01:09:24.931 --> 01:09:28.400
of the keycard puzzle up to
this room, was added specifically to show

01:09:28.400 --> 01:09:30.990
the player the vault on its own.

01:09:30.990 --> 01:09:33.640
Without this little detour, players would
otherwise have their first view

01:09:33.640 --> 01:09:36.490
of the vault at the entrance to the Northern
Star Hotel,

01:09:36.490 --> 01:09:40.480
which was also when they would see their first
Combine substation.

01:09:40.480 --> 01:09:44.190
So much new information all at once led many
players to confuse the vault

01:09:44.190 --> 01:09:47.580
with the substation or just miss it entirely,
so we created this area

01:09:47.580 --> 01:09:51.370
of the train station to highlight the vault
on its own.

01:09:51.370 --> 01:09:53.600
Even though players are required to enter
this room to get the

01:09:53.600 --> 01:09:57.770
second keycard, they still wouldn't necessarily
notice the vault outside,

01:09:57.770 --> 01:10:02.260
so we added some dialog with Russell about
looking out the window to see it.

01:10:02.260 --> 01:10:05.230
This may be a bit heavy-handed, but it was
necessary to solve the problem

01:10:05.230 --> 01:10:08.520
of players not recognizing the vault when
they saw it later in the game.

01:10:08.520 --> 01:10:10.850
a3_01_throwing
Although it may seem obvious that throwing

01:10:10.850 --> 01:10:15.740
would be a trivial interaction to
support in VR, a lot of nuanced work is required

01:10:15.740 --> 01:10:17.540
to get it to feel right.

01:10:17.540 --> 01:10:21.180
And if we were going to build puzzles like
this one, where the player must

01:10:21.180 --> 01:10:25.380
throw an armed grenade through a high duct,
throwing needed to work well.

01:10:25.380 --> 01:10:29.710
A typical VR game running at 90 frames-per-second
will read the

01:10:29.710 --> 01:10:33.060
sensor data from the tracked controllers once
per frame.

01:10:33.060 --> 01:10:36.700
This means the data is read every 11 milliseconds.

01:10:36.700 --> 01:10:40.720
That may seem like a short time step, but
when throwing, a user can easily

01:10:40.720 --> 01:10:44.810
move their hand 15 cm or more in that amount
of time.

01:10:44.810 --> 01:10:48.370
Additionally, the player may release the object
by opening their hand or

01:10:48.370 --> 01:10:51.950
releasing the trigger at any point during
their throwing motion.

01:10:51.950 --> 01:10:54.690
This makes reconstructing the act of throwing
a virtual object

01:10:54.690 --> 01:10:57.170
an interesting signal processing problem.

01:10:57.170 --> 01:11:01.330
It's up to any given piece of VR software
to derive the player's intent

01:11:01.330 --> 01:11:04.630
from the discretely sampled controller data
and we've learned a lot

01:11:04.630 --> 01:11:07.180
over the years about what works and what doesn't.

01:11:07.180 --> 01:11:11.920
In The Lab, which we shipped in 2016, we sampled
the controller's

01:11:11.920 --> 01:11:15.500
positional data every 11ms and used the sampled
positions to

01:11:15.500 --> 01:11:18.560
compute the controller velocity at each time
step.

01:11:18.560 --> 01:11:21.870
We then used an average of the computed velocity
and direction over several

01:11:21.870 --> 01:11:25.200
frames before and after the object release
to determine the

01:11:25.200 --> 01:11:28.700
velocity and direction to apply to the thrown
object.

01:11:28.700 --> 01:11:32.230
Although this was functional enough to ship,
it didn't always feel consistent,

01:11:32.230 --> 01:11:36.330
especially if the game's frame rate ever fell
below 90 frames per second.

01:11:36.330 --> 01:11:40.160
Later, in the Index Controller Tech Demo Moondust,
we expanded on

01:11:40.160 --> 01:11:44.030
this algorithm by biasing towards higher velocity
data.

01:11:44.030 --> 01:11:47.850
We also extended the SteamVR software interface
to provide the controller's

01:11:47.850 --> 01:11:51.550
instantaneous velocity and acceleration data
from the hardware

01:11:51.550 --> 01:11:56.590
Inertial Measurement Unit, which is computed
on the controller every 2 milliseconds.

01:11:56.590 --> 01:11:59.520
This meant that we did not have to calculate
an estimate from the positions

01:11:59.520 --> 01:12:04.130
sampled by the engine only every 11 milliseconds,
as we had done on the The Lab.

01:12:04.130 --> 01:12:07.530
This was crucial to providing a more responsive
feel, as it meant the

01:12:07.530 --> 01:12:10.890
throwing calculations were no longer tied
to the rendering frame rate.

01:12:10.890 --> 01:12:15.470
After further experimentation, we arrived
at the solution used in Half-Life: Alyx,

01:12:15.470 --> 01:12:18.800
which takes ten frames that preceded the release
of the held object and

01:12:18.800 --> 01:12:23.600
averages the three frames bracketing the peak
controller velocity in that time window.

01:12:23.600 --> 01:12:26.190
We have found that this tends to lead to a
better approximation of

01:12:26.190 --> 01:12:29.680
precisely when the player intended to release
a thrown object.

01:12:29.680 --> 01:12:33.440
This approach resulted in consistent, comfortable
throwing, enabling us to

01:12:33.440 --> 01:12:38.250
rely on players being able to accurately throw
grenades, or any other objects, with confidence.

01:12:38.250 --> 01:12:41.220
a3_01_optional_toner
This optional toner puzzle is intended to

01:12:41.220 --> 01:12:45.300
train players not to
blindly click on each junction without first

01:12:45.300 --> 01:12:47.890
understanding the implications.

01:12:47.890 --> 01:12:51.460
This particular puzzle is designed to have
the side effect of tripping a

01:12:51.460 --> 01:12:55.460
fire alarm if the player merely clicks on
each toner junction

01:12:55.460 --> 01:12:57.570
they encounter without looking ahead.

01:12:57.570 --> 01:13:02.260
An observant player can easily solve the puzzle
without tripping the alarm,

01:13:02.260 --> 01:13:05.680
but a careless player will trip the alarm,
causing a Combine soldier

01:13:05.680 --> 01:13:09.300
to come storming around the corner to investigate
the noise.

01:13:09.300 --> 01:13:12.570
a3_01_substation_music
Typical players spend up to two hours progressing

01:13:12.570 --> 01:13:17.340
through the interior
spaces of the Northern Star Hotel and we found

01:13:17.340 --> 01:13:21.920
in playtests that they
often had trouble remembering their next goal

01:13:21.920 --> 01:13:25.990
of disrupting
the Combine substation above.

01:13:25.990 --> 01:13:29.940
In earlier tests, the Vault constantly reminded
players of its

01:13:29.940 --> 01:13:51.900
presence by emitting a repeating abstract
musical pattern.

01:13:51.900 --> 01:13:56.980
This pattern worked so well at drawing and
keeping the player's attention

01:13:56.980 --> 01:14:01.430
that we expanded and fictionalized it as a
semi-musical sound design

01:14:01.430 --> 01:14:06.650
pattern which we changed to emit from the
Combine substation representing

01:14:06.650 --> 01:14:29.560
it collecting and transmitting Vortigaunt
energy to the Vault.

01:14:29.560 --> 01:14:34.560
While in the hotel, the player does not have
line of sight to the substation,

01:14:34.560 --> 01:14:39.270
so we repeat this sonic pattern at varying
volumes within the hotel

01:14:39.270 --> 01:14:47.370
as a reminder of both the Combine substation
and the Vault outside.

01:14:47.370 --> 01:14:59.120
In fact, if you look closely at the Xen foliage
when you are in the Hotel,

01:14:59.120 --> 01:15:05.750
you will notice that it reacts visibly to
the overspill of Vortigaunt energy as it flows

01:15:05.750 --> 01:15:06.920
from the substation.

01:15:06.920 --> 01:15:09.090
a3_02_liquid_bottles
Given the large number of bottles throughout

01:15:09.090 --> 01:15:12.690
the game and the fact that they
feature so significantly in the distillery

01:15:12.690 --> 01:15:17.000
gameplay, we decided to make
interacting with them as interesting as possible

01:15:17.000 --> 01:15:19.310
using a procedural liquid effect.

01:15:19.310 --> 01:15:22.870
Each bottle is assigned a random fill level,
and values for the amount of

01:15:22.870 --> 01:15:27.250
liquid agitation and foaming are calculated
based on the bottle's movement.

01:15:27.250 --> 01:15:31.370
This is stored in the object's tint color
which is otherwise unused.

01:15:31.370 --> 01:15:36.210
The shader calculates the liquid level based
on the bottle's orientation in game,

01:15:36.210 --> 01:15:39.810
since the amount of liquid visible in a bottle
as a proportion of its height,

01:15:39.810 --> 01:15:44.140
is different when oriented upright, sideways
or upside-down, different values

01:15:44.140 --> 01:15:48.750
are used for each in order to preserve the
apparent volume of liquid inside.

01:15:48.750 --> 01:15:52.250
The liquid's surface is then displaced by
a number of sine waves,

01:15:52.250 --> 01:15:56.060
modulated with the agitation value to create
rippling waves.

01:15:56.060 --> 01:15:59.400
The areas determined to contain liquid, as
opposed to air,

01:15:59.400 --> 01:16:03.200
take on the color and refraction index values
of that liquid.

01:16:03.200 --> 01:16:07.170
Bubbles and foam effects are added based on
the agitation and foaming values.

01:16:07.170 --> 01:16:12.300
These are procedural and can be modified to
create a variety of different behaviors.

01:16:12.300 --> 01:16:15.350
Several layers of bubbles are created in a
local view space relative to

01:16:15.350 --> 01:16:18.500
the object, so that they're stable from frame
to frame and

01:16:18.500 --> 01:16:21.880
can always move upwards relative to gravity.

01:16:21.880 --> 01:16:25.920
Artists can set a variety of parameters per
liquid, such as color, glow,

01:16:25.920 --> 01:16:30.030
foaming potential, wave properties and a meniscus
at the surface of the liqui

01:16:30.030 --> 01:16:32.550
which curls up around the edges of the volume,
helping to

01:16:32.550 --> 01:16:35.370
communicate the viscosity of the liquid.

01:16:35.370 --> 01:16:38.820
The refracted environment seen through the
glass and liquid is sampled from

01:16:38.820 --> 01:16:43.300
the same cubic environment map of the local
area that's used for reflections,

01:16:43.300 --> 01:16:46.520
and so the bottles are effectively opaque
and do not have to blend with the

01:16:46.520 --> 01:16:50.400
frame buffer in the way that transparent objects
normally do.

01:16:50.400 --> 01:16:54.010
This was a strategic design choice, as along
with storing the physics data

01:16:54.010 --> 01:16:57.680
in the tint color it means that the bottles
can be rendered together as a batch,

01:16:57.680 --> 01:17:01.770
in arbitrary order, which is critical for
graphics throughput.

01:17:01.770 --> 01:17:05.660
In the end, the refractive liquid effect allows
for a variety of interactions

01:17:05.660 --> 01:17:10.530
to take place at a fidelity that is especially
compelling with tracked hand-controllers in

01:17:10.530 --> 01:17:11.530
VR.

01:17:11.530 --> 01:17:13.670
a3_02_physics_sounds
We knew from previous VR projects that players

01:17:13.670 --> 01:17:16.870
would expect a high level
of fidelity from their interactions with objects

01:17:16.870 --> 01:17:19.940
in the world,
and during early testing of Half-Life: Alyx

01:17:19.940 --> 01:17:23.080
we received comments
that objects were not making the sounds that

01:17:23.080 --> 01:17:26.520
players expected,
given an object's material, size and assumed

01:17:26.520 --> 01:17:27.520
weight.

01:17:27.520 --> 01:17:30.050
This led us to a more granular approach to
physics sounds

01:17:30.050 --> 01:17:32.470
than than we had used in prior products.

01:17:32.470 --> 01:17:35.280
For example, in the past, we would have used
a single physics

01:17:35.280 --> 01:17:38.990
sound for all objects of a certain size and
material.

01:17:38.990 --> 01:17:43.660
So we would have had one sound to represent
collisions of small metal objects,

01:17:43.660 --> 01:17:46.800
another sound for large wooden objects and
so on.

01:17:46.800 --> 01:17:50.620
For Half-Life: Alyx, we built up a new library
of object-specific

01:17:50.620 --> 01:17:53.330
sounds so that we could distinguish between
similar objects

01:17:53.330 --> 01:17:56.600
like a piece of silverware and a small metal
can.

01:17:56.600 --> 01:18:00.890
Not only do we now have a library of almost
two thousand object-specific

01:18:00.890 --> 01:18:04.280
physics sounds covering over 160 different
objects,

01:18:04.280 --> 01:18:08.930
but we can further alter the sound at run
time based on an object's

01:18:08.930 --> 01:18:11.990
impact velocity and the hardness of the material
it collides with.

01:18:11.990 --> 01:18:16.550
In some cases, we even layer in sounds representing
an object's contents,

01:18:16.550 --> 01:18:20.820
such as matches in a matchbox or fuel in a
jerrycan, which can be heard when

01:18:20.820 --> 01:18:24.480
those objects are shaken by the player or
collide with something at high velocity.

01:18:24.480 --> 01:18:26.671
a3_02_xen_art
Breaching the quarantine zone revealed a new

01:18:26.671 --> 01:18:31.140
hidden secret of the city:
alien plant life from Xen has taken root on

01:18:31.140 --> 01:18:35.040
Earth and has been thriving in the shadows
of City 17.

01:18:35.040 --> 01:18:37.980
On the development team, the Northern Star
Hotel was referred to

01:18:37.980 --> 01:18:41.830
as 'Hotel Xen' before it had a proper name
in the story, as it was the first

01:18:41.830 --> 01:18:45.230
location we used to explore what Xen foliage
could look like and

01:18:45.230 --> 01:18:48.170
how it might consume any given environment.

01:18:48.170 --> 01:18:52.080
One of the challenges the environment team
faced when designing the Xen foliage,

01:18:52.080 --> 01:18:54.940
was layering a new look onto an existing environment
without

01:18:54.940 --> 01:18:58.380
having to rebuild Xen infested versions of
every prop.

01:18:58.380 --> 01:19:02.710
We relied heavily on texture blending and
kit bashing of Xen foliage shapes

01:19:02.710 --> 01:19:07.320
to handle most of the big environmental changes
when Xen-ifying a scene.

01:19:07.320 --> 01:19:10.810
We also added some new features to our level
editor, Hammer, which allowed

01:19:10.810 --> 01:19:14.190
for more creative manipulation of assets to
reduce repetition.

01:19:14.190 --> 01:19:18.780
For example, we could pose certain types of
meshes such as the Xen tentacles.

01:19:18.780 --> 01:19:22.880
These could be placed, scaled, stretched and
manipulated to snake through an

01:19:22.880 --> 01:19:27.320
environment, giving a handcrafted look without
every asset having to be built for a specific

01:19:27.320 --> 01:19:28.320
location.

01:19:28.320 --> 01:19:31.800
These elements were editable in Hammer so
they could be adjusted at any time,

01:19:31.800 --> 01:19:36.000
but were baked into static meshes when the
map was compiled to improve their run-time

01:19:36.000 --> 01:19:37.000
performance.

01:19:37.000 --> 01:19:39.410
a3_02_xen_animation
The Xen flora in this area animate in response

01:19:39.410 --> 01:19:42.520
to the Vortigaunt
energy pulsing from the Combine substation

01:19:42.520 --> 01:19:44.010
outside.

01:19:44.010 --> 01:19:47.480
Since the substation itself is not visible
from inside the hotel,

01:19:47.480 --> 01:19:50.810
it was important that we continue to remind
players of its presence using audio

01:19:50.810 --> 01:19:55.680
and visual cues, and the Xen flora provided
a perfect opportunity to do this.

01:19:55.680 --> 01:19:58.900
The shaders used on the Xen flora help illustrate
the

01:19:58.900 --> 01:20:02.500
plants' response to the energy pulses in a
few different ways.

01:20:02.500 --> 01:20:06.170
For example, the vili and cilia elongate and
vibrate,

01:20:06.170 --> 01:20:09.380
while many of the other plants pulsate or
glow.

01:20:09.380 --> 01:20:13.840
The intensity and timing of these effects
are driven directly by the sound system,

01:20:13.840 --> 01:20:18.390
meaning that the audio and visual reinforcement
of the Combine substation pulses are always

01:20:18.390 --> 01:20:19.390
in synch.

01:20:19.390 --> 01:20:21.350
a3_02_reviver_intro
The Reviver, one of the new enemy types introduced

01:20:21.350 --> 01:20:25.330
in Half-Life: Alyx,
arose from our observation that playtesters

01:20:25.330 --> 01:20:28.340
enjoyed searching their
environment in VR much more than they had

01:20:28.340 --> 01:20:30.210
in traditional games.

01:20:30.210 --> 01:20:34.000
In addition to rewarding that desire to search
with ammunition and resin,

01:20:34.000 --> 01:20:37.030
we experimented with how it could be part
of fighting an enemy.

01:20:37.030 --> 01:20:41.500
We began prototyping with a fast headcrab
variant we called the Runner,

01:20:41.500 --> 01:20:45.310
modifying its behavior to run away and hide
from the player after it attacked.

01:20:45.310 --> 01:20:48.130
We found players really enjoyed looking for
a small,

01:20:48.130 --> 01:20:51.570
hidden enemy they knew was somewhere within
a dense environment.

01:20:51.570 --> 01:20:54.080
They also liked shooting at a fast moving
enemy that was able

01:20:54.080 --> 01:20:58.600
to run under and behind everyday objects,
like tables and chairs.

01:20:58.600 --> 01:21:01.910
Lastly, players talked about liking the way
they got to be the aggressor in combat

01:21:01.910 --> 01:21:06.250
for once, since everywhere else in the game
they felt like they were always on the defensive.

01:21:06.250 --> 01:21:11.030
While feedback was overall very positive,
there was one specific problem:

01:21:11.030 --> 01:21:14.200
how to encourage players to search for the
Runner.

01:21:14.200 --> 01:21:18.130
After a few Runner encounters, we found players
would start to realize they didn't have to

01:21:18.130 --> 01:21:21.060
look for the Runner at all,
they could simply wait for it to run away,

01:21:21.060 --> 01:21:22.990
and then leave the room.

01:21:22.990 --> 01:21:26.840
So we started prototyping various ways in
which the Runner could become more

01:21:26.840 --> 01:21:31.100
dangerous if not found, setting up a time
pressure where players had to find

01:21:31.100 --> 01:21:35.050
the Runner quickly, or the situation would
get worse.

01:21:35.050 --> 01:21:38.500
After some experimentation, we landed on the
prototype where the Runner could

01:21:38.500 --> 01:21:41.520
revive a fallen zombie, and become a new kind
of foe,

01:21:41.520 --> 01:21:44.640
one that was more dangerous than a Runner
or Zombie.

01:21:44.640 --> 01:21:48.600
After this change, the Runner was renamed
internally to the Reviver,

01:21:48.600 --> 01:21:51.880
and we began assembling the various encounters
around it.

01:21:51.880 --> 01:21:56.210
It took a lot of playtesting to balance out
all the various elements of the

01:21:56.210 --> 01:22:00.240
Reviver fights, because they involve multiple,
separate stages:

01:22:00.240 --> 01:22:05.410
Combat versus the Reviver, searching for a
hidden Reviver, and combat versus a Revived

01:22:05.410 --> 01:22:06.750
Zombie.

01:22:06.750 --> 01:22:10.440
In the end, we still didn't feel like we'd
created enough of a foolproof reason

01:22:10.440 --> 01:22:13.850
for players to need to search for hidden Revivers,
so we turned the

01:22:13.850 --> 01:22:17.380
Reviver's heart into a key, requiring players
to collect it.

01:22:17.380 --> 01:22:19.520
a3_02_reviver_animation
An enemy's animation can elicit a powerful

01:22:19.520 --> 01:22:22.110
emotional response from players, especially
in VR.

01:22:22.110 --> 01:22:25.430
While developing the Reviver, we learned that
sometimes a character

01:22:25.430 --> 01:22:28.900
performance can have unintended consequences.

01:22:28.900 --> 01:22:32.370
In our initial Reviver prototypes, we simply
used a blue-tinted

01:22:32.370 --> 01:22:35.620
headcrab model with rudimentary electrical
effects.

01:22:35.620 --> 01:22:39.540
The prototype was successful enough in playtesting
that we decided to invest in a

01:22:39.540 --> 01:22:45.250
new purpose-built model for the Reviver, with
electrical skin and improved effects.

01:22:45.250 --> 01:22:50.080
Players identified the new Reviver successfully
in the game, but we ran into a new problem:

01:22:50.080 --> 01:22:51.950
players didn’t want to fight it any more.

01:22:51.950 --> 01:22:55.310
While the blue headcrab was easily identified
as something dangerous,

01:22:55.310 --> 01:22:58.190
the new model was something that players had
to evaluate from scratch

01:22:58.190 --> 01:23:00.850
and they didn't necessarily see it as threatening.

01:23:00.850 --> 01:23:03.870
In fact, the animations authored for the new
Reviver model were

01:23:03.870 --> 01:23:08.090
interpreted by many players as friendly or
even cute.

01:23:08.090 --> 01:23:11.440
Players would often approach the creature
to try and pet it or, worse,

01:23:11.440 --> 01:23:15.850
refuse to kill it to retrieve the required
puzzle piece, blocking their progression in

01:23:15.850 --> 01:23:16.850
the game.

01:23:16.850 --> 01:23:19.190
If they did then realize that they were going
to have to kill the cute

01:23:19.190 --> 01:23:23.160
creature to proceed, they felt horrible having
to go through with it.

01:23:23.160 --> 01:23:26.960
To fix this, we re-authored most of the Reviver’s
animations,

01:23:26.960 --> 01:23:30.640
making it significantly more unsettling and
menacing.

01:23:30.640 --> 01:23:34.751
We also made the Reviver more aggressive by
adding a jump attack if the player got too

01:23:34.751 --> 01:23:36.030
close.

01:23:36.030 --> 01:23:39.570
With these changes to animation and behavior,
players once again were fighting

01:23:39.570 --> 01:23:43.650
the Reviver as intended and they no longer
wanted to take it home as a pet.

01:23:43.650 --> 01:23:46.140
a3_02_hlpr
This is the room featured in the very first

01:23:46.140 --> 01:23:49.360
shot of the Half-Life: Alyx announcement trailer.

01:23:49.360 --> 01:23:54.750
It's not an obvious choice, for that use - the
room is dark, not particularly colorful,

01:23:54.750 --> 01:23:56.980
and it's a pretty pedestrian corridor.

01:23:56.980 --> 01:24:01.370
Cleaning supplies and laundry baskets are
not in themselves attention grabbers.

01:24:01.370 --> 01:24:04.560
But we chose this scenario because it very
effectively demonstrates

01:24:04.560 --> 01:24:08.690
the excitement and tension that we thought
was typical of the game.

01:24:08.690 --> 01:24:11.600
All game trailers require careful design.

01:24:11.600 --> 01:24:16.840
Conveying a game’s core experience in just
a few moments is tricky even at the best of

01:24:16.840 --> 01:24:17.840
times.

01:24:17.840 --> 01:24:21.200
But we believe the Half-Life: Alyx trailer
had to work harder than most,

01:24:21.200 --> 01:24:24.920
to meet a particularly difficult and uncommon
set of challenges.

01:24:24.920 --> 01:24:28.860
We had kept the existence of the game under
wraps, so shipping the trailer was

01:24:28.860 --> 01:24:34.420
going to be the first time in 13 years that
Valve had shown any work on Half-Life.

01:24:34.420 --> 01:24:37.770
On top of that, the trailer had to quickly
communicate that Valve had

01:24:37.770 --> 01:24:42.490
chosen to make the next installment of Half-Life
a VR game.

01:24:42.490 --> 01:24:46.000
And although we were very confident in the
decision to make a VR game

01:24:46.000 --> 01:24:49.760
and in the quality of what we had built, we
still worried that revealing

01:24:49.760 --> 01:24:53.310
those decisions to the world might cause even
our most loyal fans

01:24:53.310 --> 01:24:57.830
to make their own decision: that we had truly
lost our minds.

01:24:57.830 --> 01:25:00.290
Those of us working on the trailer knew it
had to make people

01:25:00.290 --> 01:25:03.930
so excited that they could put those worries
aside.

01:25:03.930 --> 01:25:08.270
The trailer also had to clearly convey that
the game was not Half-Life 3.

01:25:08.270 --> 01:25:12.240
Meaning that it did not take place after the
events of Episode 2.

01:25:12.240 --> 01:25:15.440
Half-Life: Alyx was, instead, a prequel of
sorts,

01:25:15.440 --> 01:25:18.580
occurring between the events of Half-Life
and Half-Life 2.

01:25:18.580 --> 01:25:23.400
In addition, the trailer had to show depth,
variety, familiar characters,

01:25:23.400 --> 01:25:27.350
and enough novelty and surprise to get across
to the viewer that this

01:25:27.350 --> 01:25:33.940
was really and truly a full-fledged installment
in the Half-Life series, not a small VR experiment

01:25:33.940 --> 01:25:35.560
or a spinoff.

01:25:35.560 --> 01:25:38.680
This was a tall order, but the entire game
team rallied around

01:25:38.680 --> 01:25:42.060
the effort because everyone understood the
value of the work.

01:25:42.060 --> 01:25:45.180
We’re just as proud of the result we achieved
in the trailer

01:25:45.180 --> 01:25:47.790
as we are of any other part of Half-Life:
Alyx.

01:25:47.790 --> 01:25:50.720
a3_03_teleport_immersion
Half-Life: Alyx supports three types of player

01:25:50.720 --> 01:25:51.720
movement.

01:25:51.720 --> 01:25:54.670
Continuous movement, shift teleport, and blink
teleport.

01:25:54.670 --> 01:25:58.440
Early on, one of the properties of blink teleport,
and to a lesser degree,

01:25:58.440 --> 01:26:01.680
shift teleport, was that players would often
refer to the actual mechanic

01:26:01.680 --> 01:26:04.940
of teleporting when describing their movement
through the environment.

01:26:04.940 --> 01:26:09.100
'I teleported to the desk' or 'I teleported
into cover' was often how

01:26:09.100 --> 01:26:12.000
players would describe their actions and it
served as an unconscious

01:26:12.000 --> 01:26:15.950
reminder that they were interacting with a
game in a specific way.

01:26:15.950 --> 01:26:19.710
To address this, we made a couple of key changes
early on that significantly

01:26:19.710 --> 01:26:23.370
altered the way players recounted their movement,
and as a result,

01:26:23.370 --> 01:26:26.600
pulled them further into a more convincing
virtual reality.

01:26:26.600 --> 01:26:30.760
One of these changes was the visual indicator
that represented the teleport destination.

01:26:30.760 --> 01:26:35.720
We swapped the abstract cylindrical indicator
used in The Lab for a pair of holographic

01:26:35.720 --> 01:26:36.720
feet.

01:26:36.720 --> 01:26:39.790
The feet help provide a sense of physical
presence and indicated

01:26:39.790 --> 01:26:42.830
the direction the player would face upon finishing
a teleport.

01:26:42.830 --> 01:26:47.570
We also added animated feet between the player's
current location and the teleport target.

01:26:47.570 --> 01:26:50.350
These feet illustrated the path the player
would traverse,

01:26:50.350 --> 01:26:54.000
further helping provide a fiction for their
movement through the environment.

01:26:54.000 --> 01:26:57.690
Along with the visual interface changes, we
also added audio of footsteps

01:26:57.690 --> 01:27:01.030
and body movements that would play after a
teleport to effectively

01:27:01.030 --> 01:27:03.760
summarize the player's movement through the
space.

01:27:03.760 --> 01:27:06.690
This isn’t something everyone necessarily
notices at first,

01:27:06.690 --> 01:27:09.160
but it is quite obvious when it’s missing
or incorrect.

01:27:09.160 --> 01:27:12.150
The footstep audio transformed how players
described their movement through

01:27:12.150 --> 01:27:15.630
the world and players would often refer back
to movement in the environment

01:27:15.630 --> 01:27:19.020
as walking or running as opposed to referring
to game terminology.

01:27:19.020 --> 01:27:22.220
a3_03_reviver_foreshadowing
Compared to our traditional first person experiences,

01:27:22.220 --> 01:27:25.420
in VR we found
it was sometimes all too easy to create an

01:27:25.420 --> 01:27:26.420
intense atmosphere.

01:27:26.420 --> 01:27:29.970
This can be used to the game’s advantage
but it can also be detrimental in

01:27:29.970 --> 01:27:34.260
cases where we our focus is on training players
in some new mechanic.

01:27:34.260 --> 01:27:39.230
In intense situations, players frequently
fail notice or retain critical information.

01:27:39.230 --> 01:27:41.750
And as a result, we often had to intentionally
reduce tension in

01:27:41.750 --> 01:27:45.860
areas of the game where we were introducing
players to something new.

01:27:45.860 --> 01:27:49.710
The audio of the Reviver here, as the player
descends beneath the hotel,

01:27:49.710 --> 01:27:53.340
is designed to ease players into the idea
that they will be encountering

01:27:53.340 --> 01:27:57.130
the creature that they had briefly seen as
they entered the hotel earlier.

01:27:57.130 --> 01:28:01.570
The Reviver's scream and its shadow on the
wall are designed to reduce

01:28:01.570 --> 01:28:07.270
tension by indicating the position of the
Reviver around the corner, removing any potential

01:28:07.270 --> 01:28:08.270
surprise.

01:28:08.270 --> 01:28:12.490
Telegraphing the Reviver's position and giving
players the upper hand

01:28:12.490 --> 01:28:16.210
helps set up the resurrection scene, where
players want to observe

01:28:16.210 --> 01:28:19.230
the Reviver instead of shooting at it immediately.

01:28:19.230 --> 01:28:22.760
This is critical, because this is the first
time the player sees the

01:28:22.760 --> 01:28:26.860
Reviver reanimating a zombie and if players
are too distracted to take it in,

01:28:26.860 --> 01:28:28.730
they won't be successful in the upcoming fight.

01:28:28.730 --> 01:28:30.970
a3_03_reviver_training
One of the challenges with the Reviver’s

01:28:30.970 --> 01:28:35.650
resurrection mechanic was training
players that the Reviver was the target and

01:28:35.650 --> 01:28:38.970
the zombie was
effectively just an inert vehicle that could

01:28:38.970 --> 01:28:40.610
not be damaged.

01:28:40.610 --> 01:28:45.270
This was a significant departure from what
we had been training players to expect.

01:28:45.270 --> 01:28:48.460
Up until this point in the game, players would
see a zombie,

01:28:48.460 --> 01:28:52.000
shoot at it and as long as they hit it enough
times it would die.

01:28:52.000 --> 01:28:56.690
The Reviver breaks that expectation and, in
early tests, players thought the

01:28:56.690 --> 01:29:01.280
game was broken when the zombie wouldn’t
die or even react to being shot.

01:29:01.280 --> 01:29:05.920
We had to make a number of changes to revived
zombies to train players that this behavior

01:29:05.920 --> 01:29:07.500
was intentional.

01:29:07.500 --> 01:29:12.530
First, we designed the Revived zombie's introduction
animation to use particle

01:29:12.530 --> 01:29:18.620
effects and audio to draw attention to the
changes that result in this new type of combat.

01:29:18.620 --> 01:29:23.140
Second, we substituted in dull particle and
impact sounds to make shooting

01:29:23.140 --> 01:29:28.860
the zombie feel less impactful than it did
in previous encounters with ordinary non-revived

01:29:28.860 --> 01:29:29.860
zombies.

01:29:29.860 --> 01:29:33.860
And, finally, we modified the revived zombie
so that it doesn’t play any

01:29:33.860 --> 01:29:37.770
flinch animation or respond to bullet impacts,
further communicating to

01:29:37.770 --> 01:29:40.960
players that its behavior is different.

01:29:40.960 --> 01:29:46.010
We also used the Reviver itself to illustrate
the new combat experience.

01:29:46.010 --> 01:29:49.210
Electrical effects were added to indicate
the Reviver’s movement inside

01:29:49.210 --> 01:29:55.510
the zombie, with a bright pop and glow as
it emerged at a new location on the zombie's

01:29:55.510 --> 01:29:56.510
body.

01:29:56.510 --> 01:30:01.260
With these new visuals, and satisfying impact
sounds and screeches from the Reviver when

01:30:01.260 --> 01:30:04.940
hit,
playtesters were no longer confused, and consistently

01:30:04.940 --> 01:30:06.630
enjoyed the new combat mechanic.

01:30:06.630 --> 01:30:09.760
a3_03_reviver_ground_attack
In our early designs, a revived zombie was

01:30:09.760 --> 01:30:13.420
just an invincible
zombie with a moving vulnerable spot.

01:30:13.420 --> 01:30:16.420
Playtesting revealed this to be less tense
and threatening than the reviver

01:30:16.420 --> 01:30:20.790
on its own and since we wanted the revived
zombie to be a higher-stakes

01:30:20.790 --> 01:30:24.560
adversary, we needed to enhance its offensive
capabilities.

01:30:24.560 --> 01:30:27.900
As a natural extension of the electrified
nature of the reviver,

01:30:27.900 --> 01:30:31.450
we chose to create an electrical field around
the revived zombie

01:30:31.450 --> 01:30:35.460
so that players who got too close would take
heavy electrical damage.

01:30:35.460 --> 01:30:38.280
This worked well for many playtests, until
we observed a

01:30:38.280 --> 01:30:41.460
player who was a proficient real-world marksman.

01:30:41.460 --> 01:30:44.400
This player was able to easily defeat the
revived zombie

01:30:44.400 --> 01:30:46.240
without ever being threatened by it.

01:30:46.240 --> 01:30:50.900
In response, we gave the revived zombie its
electrified ground counterattack,

01:30:50.900 --> 01:30:54.880
which forces players to change positions to
avoid damage.

01:30:54.880 --> 01:30:59.730
This created a cadence of attack and retreat
that was more tense and satisfying for all

01:30:59.730 --> 01:31:00.730
players.

01:31:00.730 --> 01:31:02.880
a3_03_elevator_comfort
Since a VR game must always be rendered from

01:31:02.880 --> 01:31:06.870
a virtual camera that matches
the player's own point of view, we have limited

01:31:06.870 --> 01:31:10.540
opportunities to control what the player sees.

01:31:10.540 --> 01:31:14.740
Elevators are one of the few tools we have
in VR to direct the player's view.

01:31:14.740 --> 01:31:18.680
A rising elevator like this one can be used
similar to a crane shot that

01:31:18.680 --> 01:31:22.660
you might see in film, going from one elevation
to another,

01:31:22.660 --> 01:31:26.060
revealing a beautiful vista or dramatic story
beat.

01:31:26.060 --> 01:31:30.460
In VR, elevator rides can be challenging to
make comfortable, as the motion

01:31:30.460 --> 01:31:36.300
of the virtual elevator is inconsistent with
the player's own vestibular system.

01:31:36.300 --> 01:31:39.420
We found that we could make the elevator rides
in Half-Life: Alyx comfortable

01:31:39.420 --> 01:31:43.080
enough for most players by moving the elevator
slowly and limiting

01:31:43.080 --> 01:31:46.320
how much the player could see out of the elevator.

01:31:46.320 --> 01:31:50.520
This particular elevator was initially far
more open but we gradually

01:31:50.520 --> 01:31:54.530
introduced more and more visual obstruction
to the design so that the player

01:31:54.530 --> 01:31:59.470
always had some significant portion of the
elevator in their field of view.

01:31:59.470 --> 01:32:02.420
This meant that we were able to get the dramatic
reveal of the hotel

01:32:02.420 --> 01:32:07.040
exterior and Combine substation we desired,
but players were comfortable

01:32:07.040 --> 01:32:10.810
since they could always see the elevator,
which was in their own frame of reference.

01:32:10.810 --> 01:32:13.600
a3_03_substation
After exiting the elevator, the player gets

01:32:13.600 --> 01:32:18.050
their closest view yet of
the Combine substation attached to the exterior

01:32:18.050 --> 01:32:19.180
of the hotel.

01:32:19.180 --> 01:32:23.500
While the Combine architecture can veer into
the abstract, it's often

01:32:23.500 --> 01:32:28.350
at its most successful when contrasted with
a human structure.

01:32:28.350 --> 01:32:31.410
Beyond this, we always try to drive the visual
design with an invented

01:32:31.410 --> 01:32:36.450
function, ideally to support the gameplay
or story; in this case, the routing

01:32:36.450 --> 01:32:41.450
of some unusual power source to the vault
from various parts of the city.

01:32:41.450 --> 01:32:45.070
The player doesn't know it at this point,
but this substation is designed

01:32:45.070 --> 01:32:49.880
to essentially torture the song out of a group
of Vortigaunts who are housed in the attached

01:32:49.880 --> 01:32:51.880
pod farms.

01:32:51.880 --> 01:32:55.760
The output is collected from the Vortigaunts
before being 're-harmonized' by

01:32:55.760 --> 01:32:59.660
a single 'conductor' Vortigaunt via the cable
tuner apparatus seen at

01:32:59.660 --> 01:33:04.030
the root of the transmission cables that run
up to the vault itself.

01:33:04.030 --> 01:33:08.460
This design gave us the opportunity to subsequently
meet another Vortigaunt,

01:33:08.460 --> 01:33:12.000
and acted as enough of a constraint to allow
us to design an interaction

01:33:12.000 --> 01:33:16.690
that would physically compromise the substation
as the player exits the hotel in the next

01:33:16.690 --> 01:33:17.690
map.

01:33:17.690 --> 01:33:19.400
a3_04_xen_response
Xen foliage has been designed to respond to

01:33:19.400 --> 01:33:21.170
the player in a number of ways.

01:33:21.170 --> 01:33:24.620
In dark areas, where the flashlight is active,
some Xen

01:33:24.620 --> 01:33:27.400
flora will animate or expand in response to
the light.

01:33:27.400 --> 01:33:31.640
These Xen plants respond to the proximity
of the player's hand by glowing and pulsing.

01:33:31.640 --> 01:33:34.900
The glow effect in particular is rendered
with an offset using the correct

01:33:34.900 --> 01:33:37.620
binocular perspective for each eye, which
gives the impression

01:33:37.620 --> 01:33:40.300
that the glow originates in the interior of
the organism.

01:33:40.300 --> 01:33:43.820
The Xen flora's shader effects which rely
on hand proximity take their

01:33:43.820 --> 01:33:48.210
input from a dynamic voxel field which follows
the player at all times.

01:33:48.210 --> 01:33:51.400
The player's hands effectively draw motion
trails into this voxel field,

01:33:51.400 --> 01:33:56.730
which can be sampled by the foliage's shaders
to drive deformations and other shading effects.

01:33:56.730 --> 01:34:00.670
If the player moves their hands quickly, they
produce motion trails in the voxel field.

01:34:00.670 --> 01:34:03.780
If they hold their hands still, they produce
a radiating force.

01:34:03.780 --> 01:34:06.580
These different types of motion characteristics
allow us to create

01:34:06.580 --> 01:34:09.970
different behaviors in the foliage to give
it a bit of extra personality.

01:34:09.970 --> 01:34:12.220
a3_04_particle_effects
Particle effects present an interesting challenge

01:34:12.220 --> 01:34:16.700
in VR due to stereopsis,
which is our ability to discern the depth

01:34:16.700 --> 01:34:20.300
of objects from two slightly
different views of the world we see from our

01:34:20.300 --> 01:34:22.120
two eyes.

01:34:22.120 --> 01:34:25.020
Traditional particle systems, as seen on the
left, were typically

01:34:25.020 --> 01:34:28.790
implemented as a series of flipbook images
displayed on relatively

01:34:28.790 --> 01:34:32.470
large polygons which orient to face the player.

01:34:32.470 --> 01:34:36.720
Each series of images would contain effectively
a little movie of a more

01:34:36.720 --> 01:34:42.010
complex phenomenon like a wafting puff of
smoke, or a flickering flame and so on.

01:34:42.010 --> 01:34:46.340
For the monoscopic rendering used in non-VR
systems, this approach holds

01:34:46.340 --> 01:34:50.360
up well and is widely used across the industry.

01:34:50.360 --> 01:34:53.780
Because of the stereo rendering used in VR,
however, players can easily

01:34:53.780 --> 01:34:58.950
see the flat cards, resulting in the unconvincing
effect seen on the left.

01:34:58.950 --> 01:35:03.450
To address this, we had to update our particle
rendering techniques for VR.

01:35:03.450 --> 01:35:08.631
The particle system on the right is an updated
VR-friendly version of the older effect seen

01:35:08.631 --> 01:35:10.090
on the left.

01:35:10.090 --> 01:35:14.940
One technique we used on large cards like
the ones used in this smoke effect

01:35:14.940 --> 01:35:19.320
was to create some drag on the cards' reaction
to the player's head movement.

01:35:19.320 --> 01:35:23.230
To do this, we created another particle system
which lagged the player's

01:35:23.230 --> 01:35:30.030
position and was used as the player camera
for the facing direction of the smoke cards.

01:35:30.030 --> 01:35:33.570
By removing this one-to-one relationship of
head motion to sprite

01:35:33.570 --> 01:35:39.690
rotation we were able to give the impression
that the smoke was volumetric.

01:35:39.690 --> 01:35:43.500
In addition to using more 3D elements in the
creation of our effects,

01:35:43.500 --> 01:35:47.420
we also often used a post-processing bloom
on particles to help visually

01:35:47.420 --> 01:35:51.990
merge elements that otherwise would appear
as a set of flat planes.

01:35:51.990 --> 01:35:56.990
This made the cards harder to stereo fuse,
which gave them a more diffuse, volumetric

01:35:56.990 --> 01:35:57.990
feel.

01:35:57.990 --> 01:35:59.800
a3_04_darkness
One of the tenets of Half-Life game design

01:35:59.800 --> 01:36:02.930
is creating crafted
moment-to-moment experiences that players

01:36:02.930 --> 01:36:06.020
participate in as they progress through the
game.

01:36:06.020 --> 01:36:08.540
Sometimes, this means playing with emotional
responses to turn

01:36:08.540 --> 01:36:11.930
something undesirable into a memorable highlight.

01:36:11.930 --> 01:36:16.030
This collapsed section of the hotel features
this type of experience.

01:36:16.030 --> 01:36:18.900
Players are often instinctively pulled to
the safest looking location

01:36:18.900 --> 01:36:22.510
in a given area, and here, that would be the
well lit corridor above.

01:36:22.510 --> 01:36:27.470
But when they get there, they find their way
blocked by a combine power shield.

01:36:27.470 --> 01:36:30.390
The power cable to shut down the Combine forcefield
leads them

01:36:30.390 --> 01:36:33.990
back down into the poison headcrab-infested
darkness below.

01:36:33.990 --> 01:36:36.780
A place they really didn’t want to go.

01:36:36.780 --> 01:36:40.650
These larger gameplay spaces also need to
be resilient to player choice.

01:36:40.650 --> 01:36:43.720
So even if a player chooses not to head up
to the well lit corridor,

01:36:43.720 --> 01:36:50.030
they likely will still have the same trepidation
about heading down into the darkness below.

01:36:50.030 --> 01:36:53.050
After the player has braved the darkness,
and sheltered themselves in the

01:36:53.050 --> 01:36:56.230
lit room where they're safe from the wandering
headcrabs, they're faced

01:36:56.230 --> 01:37:00.170
with the daunting task of cutting the power
to the Combine forcefield,

01:37:00.170 --> 01:37:04.620
because this has the side effect of plunging
the area into darkness.

01:37:04.620 --> 01:37:07.730
Not only does that make the situation worse,
since the player must make

01:37:07.730 --> 01:37:11.020
their way out of the pitch blackness with
only a flashlight,

01:37:11.020 --> 01:37:15.210
but the path to the exit is back through all
the poison headcrabs.

01:37:15.210 --> 01:37:19.440
We spent a lot of time tuning this experience
through careful playtesting and evaluation.

01:37:19.440 --> 01:37:24.010
And, often, when recounting their travels
through the hotel in post-playtest

01:37:24.010 --> 01:37:27.920
discussions, players would recall this moment
of being plunged into darkness

01:37:27.920 --> 01:37:33.010
as a high point, managing to combine a feeling
of amusement with the overall sense of dread.

01:37:33.010 --> 01:37:35.570
a3_04_ammo_balancing_1
A common game design problem is that of resource

01:37:35.570 --> 01:37:39.310
placement,
and this spot in the game is a good example.

01:37:39.310 --> 01:37:43.300
Players have just left an area where they
acquired the Combine Submachine Gun.

01:37:43.300 --> 01:37:45.360
Whenever they get a new weapon, we try to
give the player the

01:37:45.360 --> 01:37:48.290
opportunity to experiment with it right away.

01:37:48.290 --> 01:37:52.711
So, right after the SMG, we made sure to give
the player a bunch of ammo for it,

01:37:52.711 --> 01:37:56.650
and a supply of not-too-threatening enemies,
in the form of confined

01:37:56.650 --> 01:37:59.950
zombies and wandering headcrabs, to experiment
on.

01:37:59.950 --> 01:38:03.250
But playtesting has shown us that there's
a wide range of ways

01:38:03.250 --> 01:38:05.400
that players respond to that setup.

01:38:05.400 --> 01:38:08.940
Some shoot a headcrab or two and move on,
while others take their time

01:38:08.940 --> 01:38:12.630
exploring the weapon, even using up all the
ammunition we gave them.

01:38:12.630 --> 01:38:16.570
Now, after the headcrabs, the player is heading
toward a new area,

01:38:16.570 --> 01:38:19.350
which features one of the hardest combat arenas
in the game,

01:38:19.350 --> 01:38:23.210
where they must fight two Revivers at the
same time.

01:38:23.210 --> 01:38:26.080
How much ammunition can we expect the player
to have here?

01:38:26.080 --> 01:38:29.800
How much should we put in this area between
the two arenas to ensure all

01:38:29.800 --> 01:38:32.040
players will have enough to fight the two
Revivers?

01:38:32.040 --> 01:38:36.110
If we don't put in enough,
a player may be in a position where they cannot

01:38:36.110 --> 01:38:40.640
succeed at the upcoming encounter,
and reloading their savegames won't help them.

01:38:40.640 --> 01:38:44.300
They would need to go back and re-play the
previous area,

01:38:44.300 --> 01:38:46.550
this time conserving more ammo.

01:38:46.550 --> 01:38:49.670
That's not something we want to happen, nor
is it something

01:38:49.670 --> 01:38:52.680
we can even expect all players to realize.

01:38:52.680 --> 01:38:57.370
Instead, many will repeatedly try and fail
to beat the two Revivers.

01:38:57.370 --> 01:39:02.530
So at its core, the problem with resource
placement is that the dynamic

01:39:02.530 --> 01:39:07.730
range of resources carried by players can
be significant, and it gets even wider as

01:39:07.730 --> 01:39:09.300
the game progresses.

01:39:09.300 --> 01:39:13.440
In Half-Life: Alyx, we would see some players
end the game with barely

01:39:13.440 --> 01:39:17.910
any ammunition, while other players had over
1000 unused rounds.

01:39:17.910 --> 01:39:20.780
a3_04_ammo_balancing_2
So how can we handle resource placement when

01:39:20.780 --> 01:39:24.250
there's such a wide
dynamic range in player collection and usage

01:39:24.250 --> 01:39:25.250
patterns?

01:39:25.250 --> 01:39:29.230
Like many games, we opted for a dynamic system
that looks at the

01:39:29.230 --> 01:39:33.050
player's current resource counts, and makes
decisions based on them.

01:39:33.050 --> 01:39:35.740
But the design of these kinds of systems can
be tricky,

01:39:35.740 --> 01:39:37.720
because they tend to affect player behavior.

01:39:37.720 --> 01:39:41.450
For instance, in Half-Life 2, we placed breakable
crates throughout the

01:39:41.450 --> 01:39:45.540
game which dynamically spawned resources,
based on the player's current health and ammo

01:39:45.540 --> 01:39:46.570
levels.

01:39:46.570 --> 01:39:49.690
This had the advantage of allowing us to not
need a huge amount of

01:39:49.690 --> 01:39:52.220
playtesting for resource balancing, because
as long as there were

01:39:52.220 --> 01:39:56.410
enough crates around, the player would always
have enough resources.

01:39:56.410 --> 01:39:59.900
But because that system exists, we didn't
have to spend much time on

01:39:59.900 --> 01:40:02.610
resource placement, and as a result, we didn't
really craft

01:40:02.610 --> 01:40:05.190
challenges around resource collection.

01:40:05.190 --> 01:40:07.590
Players also learned that they didn't need
to really care about their

01:40:07.590 --> 01:40:11.850
resource levels, because the crates were always
going to top them up on whatever they needed

01:40:11.850 --> 01:40:12.930
most.

01:40:12.930 --> 01:40:17.100
For Half-Life: Alyx, we wanted to revisit
those assumptions.

01:40:17.100 --> 01:40:21.590
We knew that searching for resources in a
VR environment was something players really

01:40:21.590 --> 01:40:22.590
enjoyed.

01:40:22.590 --> 01:40:26.350
So we wanted resource collection to be something
players needed to do throughout the entire

01:40:26.350 --> 01:40:27.360
game.

01:40:27.360 --> 01:40:30.040
And that meant we didn't want them to ever
reach the point where

01:40:30.040 --> 01:40:32.840
they had so much ammo and health that they
didn't need to explore.

01:40:32.840 --> 01:40:36.320
We were also able to start playtesting earlier
in the product development

01:40:36.320 --> 01:40:40.010
cycle than we had been able to when making
Half-Life 2, which gave us the

01:40:40.010 --> 01:40:44.810
confidence that we'd be able to spend the
time we needed to carefully place every resource.

01:40:44.810 --> 01:40:47.140
a3_04_ammo_balancing_3
For Half-Life: Alyx, we moved to a system

01:40:47.140 --> 01:40:50.690
that allowed us to craft a
more specific experience, using a dynamic

01:40:50.690 --> 01:40:53.610
layer that attempts to
reduce the dynamic range of resource gathering

01:40:53.610 --> 01:40:54.850
between players.

01:40:54.850 --> 01:40:58.570
Instead of placing generic resources that
switch types, level designers

01:40:58.570 --> 01:41:01.850
carefully placed each resource type throughout
the world.

01:41:01.850 --> 01:41:04.370
Some are easy to find and some are more difficult.

01:41:04.370 --> 01:41:07.970
Then, as the player moves forward in the world,
the system opportunistically

01:41:07.970 --> 01:41:13.530
removes resources in the path ahead, based
on the player's current resource levels.

01:41:13.530 --> 01:41:16.730
This had the benefit of allowing level designers
to have a lot of

01:41:16.730 --> 01:41:20.590
control over the player's resource amounts
in different areas of the game.

01:41:20.590 --> 01:41:23.210
They were able to craft areas where they wanted
the player to

01:41:23.210 --> 01:41:26.410
be starved of one ammo type, or encouraged
to use another.

01:41:26.410 --> 01:41:30.260
For example, in this area, designers could
provide enough ammo to ensure

01:41:30.260 --> 01:41:33.210
that the player would have enough to fight
two Revivers, with the knowledge that

01:41:33.210 --> 01:41:37.550
the ammo balancing system would remove much
of it if the player was already loaded up.

01:41:37.550 --> 01:41:41.330
We could safely give the players a large amount
of Combine SMG ammo in the

01:41:41.330 --> 01:41:45.440
previous area and know that players who ran
past the poison headcrabs without firing a

01:41:45.440 --> 01:41:48.690
shot wouldn't find more here.

01:41:48.690 --> 01:41:51.320
There are a number of additional features
to address complexities that

01:41:51.320 --> 01:41:54.930
result from this system, such as ensuring
players always find something

01:41:54.930 --> 01:41:58.300
inside a Combine Locker, or ensuring that
players are still rewarded for

01:41:58.300 --> 01:42:02.630
searching regularly and carefully, even if
they're carrying a lot of ammo.

01:42:02.630 --> 01:42:05.880
Ultimately, we want a player who is paying
more attention to the environment to come

01:42:05.880 --> 01:42:07.610
out ahead resource-wise.

01:42:07.610 --> 01:42:10.630
We just needed some dials to be able to control
how far ahead they got.

01:42:10.630 --> 01:42:13.190
a3_04_double_reviver
Up until this point, each Reviver encounter

01:42:13.190 --> 01:42:17.100
has been focused on teaching
players something new about the Reviver so

01:42:17.100 --> 01:42:18.590
that they can defeat it.

01:42:18.590 --> 01:42:22.180
This arena represents the culmination of that
training.

01:42:22.180 --> 01:42:23.580
There are no new rules.

01:42:23.580 --> 01:42:27.010
There’s nothing to learn about the Reviver
itself.

01:42:27.010 --> 01:42:31.690
By this point, players are armed with the
knowledge required to progress.

01:42:31.690 --> 01:42:37.360
The challenge then shifts into a combat problem
where strategy and execution are required.

01:42:37.360 --> 01:42:41.730
Players have to juggle and prioritize multiple
targets, utilize all of their

01:42:41.730 --> 01:42:46.500
weapons and manage a constrained supply of
ammunition in order to prevail and

01:42:46.500 --> 01:42:52.610
make their way back out to the exterior of
the hotel so they can deactivate the Combine

01:42:52.610 --> 01:42:53.610
Substation.

01:42:53.610 --> 01:42:56.190
a3_05_vort_energy
One of the core sub-plots of the story line

01:42:56.190 --> 01:43:00.460
is regarding the combine
imprisonment and use of the Vortigaunts to

01:43:00.460 --> 01:43:02.520
trap the G-Man.

01:43:02.520 --> 01:43:06.460
The multi-dimensional power of the Vortigaunts
to detain the seemingly

01:43:06.460 --> 01:43:11.050
invincible G-Man is established in previous
Half-Life lore.

01:43:11.050 --> 01:43:14.960
Through the use of their prisoner pods and
sub-station technology,

01:43:14.960 --> 01:43:19.360
the Combine extract the power of the Vortigaunt's
Vortessence chant,

01:43:19.360 --> 01:43:25.010
tune that energy and then transmit it to the
Vault to contain the G-Man.

01:43:25.010 --> 01:43:29.030
To communicate this Combine process to the
player without explaining

01:43:29.030 --> 01:43:33.320
it through lengthy exposition, we used musical
sound patterns

01:43:33.320 --> 01:43:36.670
to represent the process implicitly.

01:43:36.670 --> 01:43:42.860
If you stand near the pods holding the Vortigaunts,
you can hear them groaning their chant in

01:43:42.860 --> 01:43:54.780
a slow but semi-musical pattern.

01:43:54.780 --> 01:43:58.230
Just past the pods is the 'resonator' within
which you can

01:43:58.230 --> 01:44:10.010
hear a distilled version of the chant energy.

01:44:10.010 --> 01:44:23.990
Finally, the cables are activated with the
concentrated Vortigaunt energy,

01:44:23.990 --> 01:44:28.490
which is harmonized in varying patterns and
intermittently

01:44:28.490 --> 01:44:34.940
transmitted to the vault once the energy is
sufficiently tuned.

01:44:34.940 --> 01:44:38.500
If you stand near to the cables while they're
tuning, you'll

01:44:38.500 --> 01:44:42.770
notice the sheer size and height of the sound.

01:44:42.770 --> 01:44:47.630
This was achieved by placing the sound emitters
for each cable at

01:44:47.630 --> 01:44:51.900
the locations of the actual cables themselves.

01:44:51.900 --> 01:44:56.170
The sound of the cables covers a broad frequency
range which

01:44:56.170 --> 01:45:01.960
emphasizes Steam Audio's 'head related transfer
function' processing.

01:45:01.960 --> 01:45:07.060
That simulation combined with the feedback
of the observers inner ear as

01:45:07.060 --> 01:45:23.200
she looks upwards gives the same impression
of scale that VR is known to give visual objects.

01:45:23.200 --> 01:45:34.260
a3_05_substation_vort
Character performance within a VR game has

01:45:34.260 --> 01:45:36.810
a unique set of opportunities and challenges.

01:45:36.810 --> 01:45:39.711
In our efforts to keep control in the hands
of the player, we must create a

01:45:39.711 --> 01:45:42.800
performance that is interesting enough to
maintain players' attention

01:45:42.800 --> 01:45:45.460
but flexible enough to allow for player freedom.

01:45:45.460 --> 01:45:48.940
This scene has the most face-to-face interaction
in the game and there is no

01:45:48.940 --> 01:45:52.780
physical barrier between the player and the
Vortigaunt when he first appears.

01:45:52.780 --> 01:45:56.200
At one point, we played with the idea of a
jump scare when the Vortigaunt is

01:45:56.200 --> 01:45:58.910
released, upending the player's expectations
of what had been

01:45:58.910 --> 01:46:03.170
referred to only as a 'battery' up until that
point.

01:46:03.170 --> 01:46:05.970
Not only was it a bit ham-fisted, but the
Vortigaunt came flailing

01:46:05.970 --> 01:46:09.990
out of the pod, frequently into the same space
occupied by the player.

01:46:09.990 --> 01:46:13.350
To solve this problem, we cut the jump scare
and slid the pod back out of

01:46:13.350 --> 01:46:16.320
the player's space as it opened, to allow
for the Vortigaunt to

01:46:16.320 --> 01:46:17.320
emerge at a comfortable distance.

01:46:17.320 --> 01:46:20.880
We faced a similar challenge when determining
how to get both the player

01:46:20.880 --> 01:46:23.980
and Vortigaunt to their next locations without
intersecting.

01:46:23.980 --> 01:46:26.520
We wanted to get the player and Vortigaunt
side-by-side overlooking the

01:46:26.520 --> 01:46:29.570
vista while the Vortigaunt delivered critical
exposition.

01:46:29.570 --> 01:46:32.600
It would have been awkward for the player
and the Vortigaunt to take the

01:46:32.600 --> 01:46:36.120
same route along the catwalk, so we looked
at other options, including

01:46:36.120 --> 01:46:40.170
exploring ways that the Vortigaunt may have
once moved in its native environment.

01:46:40.170 --> 01:46:43.630
With long forearms, it's feasible that the
Vortigaunts may have moved

01:46:43.630 --> 01:46:47.540
around as quadrupeds, so we chose an agile
four-legged scramble up to the

01:46:47.540 --> 01:46:52.060
higher platform where he can deliver exposition
without contending with the player for space.

01:46:52.060 --> 01:46:54.290
a3_05_prefabs
While developing Half-Life: Alyx, we had a

01:46:54.290 --> 01:46:57.550
large number of people
in many disciplines contributing to a relatively

01:46:57.550 --> 01:46:59.440
small number of maps.

01:46:59.440 --> 01:47:03.200
This meant that we frequently had multiple
people working on the same map at the same

01:47:03.200 --> 01:47:04.290
time.

01:47:04.290 --> 01:47:06.520
One way that we managed this complexity was
to break our

01:47:06.520 --> 01:47:10.120
maps into smaller pieces referred to as 'prefabs.'

01:47:10.120 --> 01:47:13.840
A prefab could be built out of the same types
of geometry, logic,

01:47:13.840 --> 01:47:17.860
enemies, particle systems or any other elements
as a regular map.

01:47:17.860 --> 01:47:20.540
As long as people were working in separate
prefabs, they could

01:47:20.540 --> 01:47:23.640
make changes or additions without stepping
on each other's work.

01:47:23.640 --> 01:47:26.350
A prefab could contain a large physical space
like this whole

01:47:26.350 --> 01:47:30.210
street scene or it could contain only a handful
of elements.

01:47:30.210 --> 01:47:33.050
Large prefabs that partitioned a map to allow
for collaboration

01:47:33.050 --> 01:47:36.420
were essential for a project of this scope,
but many small,

01:47:36.420 --> 01:47:39.610
reusable prefabs were created for Half-Life:
Alyx as well.

01:47:39.610 --> 01:47:44.070
A good example of this was the set of reusable
vehicle prefabs used here and throughout the

01:47:44.070 --> 01:47:45.330
game.

01:47:45.330 --> 01:47:48.120
Each vehicle was authored as a configurable
prefab,

01:47:48.120 --> 01:47:50.830
enabling designers to quickly place them around
the game,

01:47:50.830 --> 01:47:54.990
setting a few parameters to customize the
vehicle for the context.

01:47:54.990 --> 01:47:57.970
Parameters include things like the vehicle's
paint color, the amount of

01:47:57.970 --> 01:48:01.850
pre-existing damage, whether the doors were
open or simply missing and

01:48:01.850 --> 01:48:05.860
the type of ammunition or other resources
the vehicle might contain.

01:48:05.860 --> 01:48:08.740
This sped up our workflow and also meant that
updates to a prefab

01:48:08.740 --> 01:48:11.960
were automatically propagated out to all of
the maps that used it.

01:48:11.960 --> 01:48:14.190
a3_05_procedural_displacement
The tarps used by the Combine to cover buildings

01:48:14.190 --> 01:48:17.380
in the
quarantine zone are animated procedurally.

01:48:17.380 --> 01:48:20.630
This animation was added relatively late in
development, so it needed to

01:48:20.630 --> 01:48:24.390
be both computationally inexpensive and technologically
conservative.

01:48:24.390 --> 01:48:26.830
So, no new systems.

01:48:26.830 --> 01:48:29.870
Simple overlapping waveforms are used to displace
the tarp geometry

01:48:29.870 --> 01:48:32.350
where it hangs loosely away from the building.

01:48:32.350 --> 01:48:35.180
The displacements are oriented along the wind
direction,

01:48:35.180 --> 01:48:37.420
and their propagation direction is further
modified by the

01:48:37.420 --> 01:48:41.210
gradients of a texture we referred to as a
'freedom of motion' map.

01:48:41.210 --> 01:48:44.550
This map is derived from the shape of the
building geometry under the tarp

01:48:44.550 --> 01:48:47.870
and is used to attenuate the displacement
so that the tarp won't animate in

01:48:47.870 --> 01:48:50.610
places where it is meant to be pulled tight
against the building.

01:48:50.610 --> 01:48:53.980
The result is a flapping tarp that seems to
conform to its surroundings

01:48:53.980 --> 01:48:55.800
and responds appropriately to the wind.

01:48:55.800 --> 01:48:58.010
a3_05_character_movement
The Combine soldier you see here won't fight

01:48:58.010 --> 01:48:59.010
you.

01:48:59.010 --> 01:49:02.230
It's been programmed to run back and forth
along the path shown on the ground,

01:49:02.230 --> 01:49:07.580
in order to illustrate the stride retargeting
system developed for Half-Life: Alyx.

01:49:07.580 --> 01:49:10.930
One of the challenges of animating digital
characters is making sure

01:49:10.930 --> 01:49:13.980
that their feet don't slide along the ground
as they move.

01:49:13.980 --> 01:49:18.960
Humans are very good at picking out irregularities
in the movements of virtual characters,

01:49:18.960 --> 01:49:23.110
especially in VR, and Combine soldiers in
particular need to look like their

01:49:23.110 --> 01:49:27.350
feet are planting and their weight is shifting
as they change direction,

01:49:27.350 --> 01:49:31.080
otherwise they appear weightless and unconvincing.

01:49:31.080 --> 01:49:36.000
In a preprocessing phase, our stride retargeting
system analyzes the steps

01:49:36.000 --> 01:49:40.820
of each authored animation and stores them
as a direction-independent trajectory.

01:49:40.820 --> 01:49:45.350
At run time, our system predicts where each
foot will land on the next

01:49:45.350 --> 01:49:51.080
step based on the motion of the animation,
the character's path and the height of the

01:49:51.080 --> 01:49:52.080
ground.

01:49:52.080 --> 01:49:55.670
The pairs of colored boxes on the ground illustrate
this process.

01:49:55.670 --> 01:49:58.730
The red boxes show the previous foot positions.

01:49:58.730 --> 01:50:02.550
The green boxes indicate the soldier's current
foot position and the

01:50:02.550 --> 01:50:07.480
blue boxes are drawn where each foot is predicted
to land next.

01:50:07.480 --> 01:50:10.920
Once the predicted foot positions are calculated,
the system guides the

01:50:10.920 --> 01:50:14.750
feet to the predicted locations using the
trajectory information to

01:50:14.750 --> 01:50:18.120
preserve the style of the original animation.

01:50:18.120 --> 01:50:21.840
Once the foot lands, it is locked in place
until the next step.

01:50:21.840 --> 01:50:24.890
In the end, this system was so effective that
we could essentially

01:50:24.890 --> 01:50:29.590
make any animation walk in any direction,
without foot sliding.

01:50:29.590 --> 01:50:32.730
You can experiment with the system by moving
around the area,

01:50:32.730 --> 01:50:36.900
since this soldier has been set up to always
face you while running the specified path.

01:50:36.900 --> 01:50:38.690
a3_05_trip_mines
Trip mines have appeared in prior Half-Life

01:50:38.690 --> 01:50:42.840
games and the act of defusing
them seemed like a natural fit for a VR game,

01:50:42.840 --> 01:50:45.760
where players can perform complex operations
with their hands.

01:50:45.760 --> 01:50:49.190
By this point in the game, players have already
learned how to hack Combine

01:50:49.190 --> 01:50:54.240
technology with their multitool, but we wanted
defusing a trip mine to be more tense than

01:50:54.240 --> 01:50:55.550
the hacking puzzles.

01:50:55.550 --> 01:50:59.260
To defuse a trip mine, not only does the player
have to place their hand near

01:50:59.260 --> 01:51:02.940
the laser that could trigger the mine, but
they have to perform the task within just

01:51:02.940 --> 01:51:03.940
a few seconds.

01:51:03.940 --> 01:51:07.520
Early versions of this puzzle we inspired
by lock picking,

01:51:07.520 --> 01:51:11.240
resembling a series of tumblers that the player
had to quickly align.

01:51:11.240 --> 01:51:14.440
Unfortunately, the tumbler design required
players to rotate their

01:51:14.440 --> 01:51:17.550
wrists in an uncomfortable manner, particularly
given that they

01:51:17.550 --> 01:51:19.800
had to also avoid touching the laser beam.

01:51:19.800 --> 01:51:23.320
We removed the rotational component but kept
the timing element,

01:51:23.320 --> 01:51:26.340
leading to the mechanic you see in the final
game.

01:51:26.340 --> 01:51:29.410
We also experimented with causing the trip
mines to actually explode if the

01:51:29.410 --> 01:51:33.420
player failed to defuse them, but most players
found this too punishing,

01:51:33.420 --> 01:51:36.830
especially in cases where a chain reaction
of explosions could occur.

01:51:36.830 --> 01:51:41.590
Instead, the mines just stay active and the
player can attempt to defuse them again.

01:51:41.590 --> 01:51:44.780
We did end up keeping this behavior for the
hard difficulty setting

01:51:44.780 --> 01:51:48.170
but added a warning sound a few seconds before
the mine actually explodes,

01:51:48.170 --> 01:51:50.630
to give players some time to move away.

01:51:50.630 --> 01:51:53.850
We also experimented with allowing defused
mines to be removed and

01:51:53.850 --> 01:51:57.530
redeployed by the player, but we hadn't designed
enough combat scenarios around

01:51:57.530 --> 01:52:02.400
this mechanic for it to be satisfying in practice,
so this was left out of the final game.

01:52:02.400 --> 01:52:04.820
a3_05_combat_pacing
This combat arena that loops back over itself

01:52:04.820 --> 01:52:07.320
as the player winds through
the tenement buildings was part of one of

01:52:07.320 --> 01:52:10.920
the earliest test maps built
when we began adding Combine soldiers to Half-Life:

01:52:10.920 --> 01:52:12.670
Alyx.

01:52:12.670 --> 01:52:15.630
One of the lessons we learned from this series
of combat encounters was

01:52:15.630 --> 01:52:18.860
that fighting Combine soldiers was physically
taxing on players as

01:52:18.860 --> 01:52:22.090
we needed to pay careful attention to pacing.

01:52:22.090 --> 01:52:25.950
Taking cover, reloading, switching weapons
and acquiring targets really

01:52:25.950 --> 01:52:29.560
pushed on all of the skills that players had
learned up until this point.

01:52:29.560 --> 01:52:33.100
To help cut down on fatigue, we added gaps
between the Combine encounters

01:52:33.100 --> 01:52:37.450
and removed some of the combat altogether
to better pace the experience.

01:52:37.450 --> 01:52:40.540
The trip mines in the stairwell following
this battle are a good example

01:52:40.540 --> 01:52:43.530
of one of the pacing elements we use to slow
the player down

01:52:43.530 --> 01:52:46.390
and let them recuperate before the combat
ramps back up again.

01:52:46.390 --> 01:52:48.750
a3_05_xen_world_building
It was an interesting challenge for artists

01:52:48.750 --> 01:52:52.750
and level designers to employ
environmental storytelling to paint a picture

01:52:52.750 --> 01:52:55.611
of what may
have happened inside the walls of the quarantine

01:52:55.611 --> 01:52:56.730
zone.

01:52:56.730 --> 01:53:00.280
Just like the hotel, these apartments have
been overrun by Xen foliage

01:53:00.280 --> 01:53:04.100
and are filled with peoples' belongings, abandoned
in haste.

01:53:04.100 --> 01:53:07.540
In this area, the walls have been torn down,
either by destructive

01:53:07.540 --> 01:53:11.980
portal storms or perhaps the Combine's clean
up effort.

01:53:11.980 --> 01:53:16.350
Barrels of fluid used by workers to try and
treat or contain the Xen growth

01:53:16.350 --> 01:53:20.340
have been left behind and it would appear
that the cleanup efforts have either failed

01:53:20.340 --> 01:53:21.530
or been abandoned.

01:53:21.530 --> 01:53:25.200
By not explaining every facet of the world,
we leave players with the

01:53:25.200 --> 01:53:30.010
opportunity to imagine for themselves the
bigger picture of life in City 17.

01:53:30.010 --> 01:53:32.640
a3_05_mannequin_headcrab
Once we had the spine of the game complete,

01:53:32.640 --> 01:53:35.010
teams went through the maps
looking for additional opportunities to add

01:53:35.010 --> 01:53:38.820
the character and tone
that players associate with Half-Life games.

01:53:38.820 --> 01:53:41.040
This mannequin headcrab was a fun opportunity
to hit on

01:53:41.040 --> 01:53:43.470
the sci-fi / horror / B-movie vibe.

01:53:43.470 --> 01:53:46.100
We had a few conversations about how long
to leave the headcrab atop

01:53:46.100 --> 01:53:48.780
the mannequin, and decided the experience
should be analogous

01:53:48.780 --> 01:53:51.530
to catching a small dog in the middle of some
mischief.

01:53:51.530 --> 01:53:53.520
We wanted players to feel like they had walked
in on something they

01:53:53.520 --> 01:53:56.380
weren't supposed to see, and for them to go
away feeling like every inch

01:53:56.380 --> 01:54:00.460
of the game contained something for them to
find, if they chose to explore it.

01:54:00.460 --> 01:54:03.170
After an early update to the headcrab sounds,
where details like breathing

01:54:03.170 --> 01:54:06.380
and grunting were added for the first time,
we received feedback that the

01:54:06.380 --> 01:54:09.660
new sounds were too cute and familiar, moving
the headcrab too far in the

01:54:09.660 --> 01:54:15.080
direction of a cuddly creature rather than
an alien threat.

01:54:15.080 --> 01:54:18.010
We updated the sounds to be more threatening,
but for this headcrab

01:54:18.010 --> 01:54:20.140
we brought back some of the cuter sounds to
sell the

01:54:20.140 --> 01:54:21.860
headcrab's frustration with the mannequin.

01:54:21.860 --> 01:54:24.130
a3_06_pistol_hopper
In order to reduce the amount of reloading

01:54:24.130 --> 01:54:27.410
required in the heat of battle,
we wanted players to be able to upgrade the

01:54:27.410 --> 01:54:30.270
pistol by increasing its ammo capacity.

01:54:30.270 --> 01:54:33.270
That may seem like a small change, but implementing
it in a way that was

01:54:33.270 --> 01:54:36.420
intuitive to players and that didn't have
a lot of negative side

01:54:36.420 --> 01:54:39.210
effects was surprisingly tricky to get right.

01:54:39.210 --> 01:54:44.420
Our initial mechanic for increasing pistol
ammo capacity was to use double-sized clips.

01:54:44.420 --> 01:54:47.740
Once the pistol was upgraded, the player's
backpack held clips with twice

01:54:47.740 --> 01:54:52.250
as many bullets, and those were represented
by a clip model that was twice as long.

01:54:52.250 --> 01:54:57.530
But, unfortunately, the longer clips presented
a lot of distracting fictional problems.

01:54:57.530 --> 01:55:01.830
For one, they looked ridiculous protruding
out of the bottom of the pistol.

01:55:01.830 --> 01:55:05.520
Players were also left wondering what was
going on in their backpack to.

01:55:05.520 --> 01:55:08.870
transform the single-capacity clips that they
put in there into the.

01:55:08.870 --> 01:55:11.580
double-capacity versions that they were pulling
out.

01:55:11.580 --> 01:55:15.330
We were also left with the sort of absurd
implication that the pistol clips found.

01:55:15.330 --> 01:55:19.530
in the world now magically all had to be the
upgraded double-sized versions.

01:55:19.530 --> 01:55:23.110
While we did scrap the double-sized clips,
the concept was still enticing.

01:55:23.110 --> 01:55:25.370
We just needed to find a better metaphor.

01:55:25.370 --> 01:55:28.710
So then we started looking at the hoppers
were used on paintball guns

01:55:28.710 --> 01:55:30.920
that can store large amounts of paint balls.

01:55:30.920 --> 01:55:34.340
In fact, the final upgrade that resulted from
this whole line of

01:55:34.340 --> 01:55:37.390
thinking is still internally referred to as
the bullet hopper.

01:55:37.390 --> 01:55:42.290
So, with this in mind, we next needed to design
an intuitive interaction model.

01:55:42.290 --> 01:55:46.860
Players were already satisfied with our existing
pistol interaction loop which was:

01:55:46.860 --> 01:55:51.120
eject clip, insert the next one, chamber and
then shoot,

01:55:51.120 --> 01:55:55.310
so we wanted to preserve these skills that
players were already beginning to master.

01:55:55.310 --> 01:55:59.360
So, players would still reload the gun as
before, but small mechanical prongs

01:55:59.360 --> 01:56:02.760
would steal bullets from the inserted clip,
pull them in to the hopper and

01:56:02.760 --> 01:56:05.920
then place them into the firing chamber as
needed.

01:56:05.920 --> 01:56:10.860
Audio cues were also added to clearly communicate
the state of the hopper loading process.

01:56:10.860 --> 01:56:15.810
The hopper also impacted the visual design
that we used to convey the state of the pistol.

01:56:15.810 --> 01:56:19.330
Originally, the clips themselves had a numerical
counter indicating the number

01:56:19.330 --> 01:56:23.910
of bullets remaining and, on its own, this
was was fine and players understood it easily.

01:56:23.910 --> 01:56:27.610
But the readout on the bullet hopper was on
the opposite side of the pistol

01:56:27.610 --> 01:56:31.480
and had a pattern of blue dots indicating
the amount of bullets remaining.

01:56:31.480 --> 01:56:35.080
So these two representations in two different
locations made it difficult

01:56:35.080 --> 01:56:39.520
for players to quickly determine the total
number of bullets in the upgraded gun.

01:56:39.520 --> 01:56:43.820
To solve this, we designed a single visual
readout on the side of the pistol grip.

01:56:43.820 --> 01:56:46.340
It structurally matched the state of a pistol
clip,

01:56:46.340 --> 01:56:49.970
the pistol's bullet chamber and the bullet
hopper if the upgrade was acquired.

01:56:49.970 --> 01:56:52.820
a3_06_soundscapes
For Half-Life: Alyx, we wanted to expand the

01:56:52.820 --> 01:56:56.720
functionality of our
soundscape system, which is used to play ambient

01:56:56.720 --> 01:56:57.760
sounds.

01:56:57.760 --> 01:57:02.490
In prior games, we could generally only play
one soundscape at a time.

01:57:02.490 --> 01:57:06.770
The player would be hearing either soundscape
'A' or soundscape 'B'

01:57:06.770 --> 01:57:09.670
with a brief crossfade from one to the other.

01:57:09.670 --> 01:57:12.840
In this game, the soundscapes are much more
flexible and can be

01:57:12.840 --> 01:57:16.980
overlapped in ways that enable more realistic
transitions.

01:57:16.980 --> 01:57:21.040
For example, the volume of different soundscapes
can be controlled by the

01:57:21.040 --> 01:57:25.870
player's position and the open or closed state
of particular doors in the world.

01:57:25.870 --> 01:57:30.910
We frequently use this functionality in areas
like this transition between indoor and outdoor

01:57:30.910 --> 01:57:32.150
spaces.

01:57:32.150 --> 01:57:36.180
If you pause outside for a moment, you'll
notice the crickets, birds,

01:57:36.180 --> 01:57:39.310
distant dog barks and other environmental
sounds that help paint the

01:57:39.310 --> 01:57:42.680
picture of the quarantine zone late in the
day.

01:57:42.680 --> 01:57:46.180
As you move into the indoor area, you'll notice
the outdoor soundscape

01:57:46.180 --> 01:57:48.620
diminish as you move away from the door.

01:57:48.620 --> 01:57:53.690
In fact, if you close the door, the volume
of the outdoor soundscape is reduced even

01:57:53.690 --> 01:57:55.010
further.

01:57:55.010 --> 01:57:57.620
And as you progress into the building, you'll
begin to notice the

01:57:57.620 --> 01:58:02.860
indoor soundscape, consisting of the indistinct
hum of machinery and buzz of electrical fixtures.

01:58:02.860 --> 01:58:04.780
a3_06_suppressor_intro
It's generally a requirement that all new

01:58:04.780 --> 01:58:07.490
enemy types have a
section of the game designed to introduce

01:58:07.490 --> 01:58:08.490
them.

01:58:08.490 --> 01:58:11.370
We rarely build the introduction early on
in the process though,

01:58:11.370 --> 01:58:14.300
because it's often through playtesting that
we learn exactly what aspects

01:58:14.300 --> 01:58:17.480
of the new enemy need to be highlighted in
the introduction.

01:58:17.480 --> 01:58:20.000
In addition to understanding the capabilities
of a new enemy,

01:58:20.000 --> 01:58:22.850
we also find introductions help solidify the
very existence of

01:58:22.850 --> 01:58:25.350
the new enemy type in the player's mind.

01:58:25.350 --> 01:58:27.990
Without an explicit introduction, we sometimes
find that playtesters

01:58:27.990 --> 01:58:32.280
confuse multiple enemy types, conflating them
in their minds.

01:58:32.280 --> 01:58:34.730
This was the case with the Suppressor, the
class of soldier that's

01:58:34.730 --> 01:58:38.990
designed to pin a player behind cover while
other soldiers advance on them.

01:58:38.990 --> 01:58:41.760
The Suppressor is an inversion of the previous
soldier classes,

01:58:41.760 --> 01:58:44.620
who've all focused on trying to push players
out of cover.

01:58:44.620 --> 01:58:47.600
The Suppressor introduction stretches across
the next two rooms.

01:58:47.600 --> 01:58:51.140
In the first, players see the Suppressor firing
at zombies,

01:58:51.140 --> 01:58:53.990
giving the player a moment to safely observe
its firing behavior.

01:58:53.990 --> 01:58:58.800
They also get to hear its associated sounds,
which are important to learn for future encounters.

01:58:58.800 --> 01:59:02.680
n the second room, players learn to fight
the Suppressor themselves.

01:59:02.680 --> 01:59:05.750
This is relatively straightforward when the
Suppressor is alone,

01:59:05.750 --> 01:59:09.310
but will become more involved in later arenas,
where we combine the Suppressor with other

01:59:09.310 --> 01:59:10.310
soldier types.

01:59:10.310 --> 01:59:12.190
a3_06_environment_art
It isn't practical to spend development time

01:59:12.190 --> 01:59:15.990
or art resources evenly
across every part of the game, so we rely

01:59:15.990 --> 01:59:19.740
up on strategic re-use of
resources to maintain fidelity and specificity

01:59:19.740 --> 01:59:21.470
across a game's environments.

01:59:21.470 --> 01:59:24.490
This space was initially constructed using
industrial models and

01:59:24.490 --> 01:59:27.470
textures seen throughout many previous environments.

01:59:27.470 --> 01:59:30.880
To set it apart, we added the large cables
and Vortigaunt pods to imply a

01:59:30.880 --> 01:59:35.610
make-shift Combine power plant whose purpose
was to transmit energy to the substations.

01:59:35.610 --> 01:59:38.900
The juxtaposition of this abandoned industrial
space with the large Combine

01:59:38.900 --> 01:59:43.150
cables created enough visual interest to make
the space feel meaningful at relatively low

01:59:43.150 --> 01:59:44.150
cost.

01:59:44.150 --> 01:59:46.870
The cable motif was subsequently added to
other parts of the game to

01:59:46.870 --> 01:59:51.100
increase visual interest and give the player
a sense of being lead toward the Vault.

01:59:51.100 --> 01:59:53.770
It may not be obvious, but the cables exiting
through the ceiling here

01:59:53.770 --> 01:59:57.090
continue on to the exterior of the building,
across the large construction

01:59:57.090 --> 01:59:59.560
courtyard, and over the roof of the Distillery
building,

01:59:59.560 --> 02:00:03.660
presumably continuing onto a substation or
other Combine infrastructure.

02:00:03.660 --> 02:00:06.470
Players may not notice that continuation,
but such details help guide

02:00:06.470 --> 02:00:09.260
us in building a world that feels connected
and consistent.

02:00:09.260 --> 02:00:11.611
a3_06_combine_ai_in_vr
One problem we faced with the VR movement

02:00:11.611 --> 02:00:15.340
styles that Alyx supported was
keeping the combat experience from diverging

02:00:15.340 --> 02:00:18.150
based upon whether
players were using teleport or continuous

02:00:18.150 --> 02:00:19.400
movement.

02:00:19.400 --> 02:00:22.030
If the combat was significantly different
depending on the player's

02:00:22.030 --> 02:00:26.610
movement setting, our playtesting requirements
could explode combinatorially.

02:00:26.610 --> 02:00:30.300
One way that we prevented this combinatorial
explosion was by ensuring that AIs

02:00:30.300 --> 02:00:34.840
could sense player movement no matter which
locomotion style the player was using.

02:00:34.840 --> 02:00:39.650
To do this, we created a visual proxy for
players using teleport locomotion.

02:00:39.650 --> 02:00:43.870
Imagine a scenario where the player is behind
cover, hidden from a soldier's sight.

02:00:43.870 --> 02:00:46.500
If the player used continuous movement to
run to another piece of

02:00:46.500 --> 02:00:50.070
nearby cover, the soldier would see them while
they were out in the open.

02:00:50.070 --> 02:00:53.760
But a player who instantly teleported from
one piece of cover to the next would not be

02:00:53.760 --> 02:00:54.900
seen.

02:00:54.900 --> 02:00:58.220
To solve this discrepancy, when a player teleports,
we leave a breadcrumb

02:00:58.220 --> 02:01:02.900
trail of invisible visual proxies, which the
enemy AI can see.

02:01:02.900 --> 02:01:06.040
These proxies pass information to the AI,
similar to what it would have

02:01:06.040 --> 02:01:09.450
gathered from seeing the player perform the
movement directly.

02:01:09.450 --> 02:01:12.710
In this case, the soldier would know the teleporting
player had left the

02:01:12.710 --> 02:01:16.890
original cover, and run across open ground
to the new cover, just like a player using

02:01:16.890 --> 02:01:18.750
continuous motion.

02:01:18.750 --> 02:01:22.350
While obviously not identical, in that the
soldier didn't have a chance to

02:01:22.350 --> 02:01:26.230
take a shot at the teleporting player, these
kinds of features did allow us to

02:01:26.230 --> 02:01:30.960
reduce the number of ways our AI logic needed
to take player movement options into consideration.

02:01:30.960 --> 02:01:33.750
a3_06_ladders
For our teleport and continuous locomotion

02:01:33.750 --> 02:01:37.420
modes, we provide corresponding ladder climbing
methods.

02:01:37.420 --> 02:01:42.580
By default, players using teleport locomotion
can target a ladder and,

02:01:42.580 --> 02:01:48.320
after a short timer, be teleported to the
top or the bottom, depending on where they

02:01:48.320 --> 02:01:49.320
started.

02:01:49.320 --> 02:01:53.720
The timer was necessary to ensure that players
were intentionally using the ladder.

02:01:53.720 --> 02:01:57.730
We didn't want them to unintentionally target
a ladder and become disoriented when they

02:01:57.730 --> 02:02:00.690
found themselves at the other end.

02:02:00.690 --> 02:02:05.100
Players using continuous locomotion can directly
grab ladders to climb up or down.

02:02:05.100 --> 02:02:10.890
In this mode, they can grab a ladder rung
and move their hand down to raise their body

02:02:10.890 --> 02:02:13.370
or move their hand up to lower it.

02:02:13.370 --> 02:02:17.690
This allows players to move up or down the
ladder, rung by rung in a natural way.

02:02:17.690 --> 02:02:22.100
This mode turned out to be so popular that
we added it as an option even for players

02:02:22.100 --> 02:02:24.610
using teleport locomotion.

02:02:24.610 --> 02:02:28.930
As it happens, most of the ladders in our
game do not extend very far beyond the upper

02:02:28.930 --> 02:02:30.090
landing area.

02:02:30.090 --> 02:02:34.410
This made dismounting the top of a ladder
challenging for players using continuous ladder

02:02:34.410 --> 02:02:39.430
climbing since there is very little ladder
to grab onto above the upper landing area.

02:02:39.430 --> 02:02:43.960
To solve this, we detect when the players
stop holding the ladder with both hands and

02:02:43.960 --> 02:02:48.610
automatically teleport them to either the
top or bottom, based on the direction they

02:02:48.610 --> 02:02:49.610
were climbing.

02:02:49.610 --> 02:02:53.650
Unfortunately, we don’t have a lot of explicit
ladder training in the game, so some players

02:02:53.650 --> 02:02:58.320
are surprised by this teleport behavior if
they accidentally let go with both hands while

02:02:58.320 --> 02:02:59.320
climbing.

02:02:59.320 --> 02:03:03.150
Nevertheless, this was a better alternative
to leaving the players hanging or forcing

02:03:03.150 --> 02:03:08.340
them to fall down if they let go with both
hands, especially since some of our ladders

02:03:08.340 --> 02:03:13.400
are rather long and it would be tedious to
have to re-climb just because a player happened

02:03:13.400 --> 02:03:16.030
to let go shortly before reaching the top.

02:03:16.030 --> 02:03:18.840
a3_07_distillery_intro
Some of our earliest experiments on the project

02:03:18.840 --> 02:03:22.570
involved placing old
Half-Life 2 models in a level and walking

02:03:22.570 --> 02:03:24.330
around them in VR.

02:03:24.330 --> 02:03:29.560
These included static models of enemies like
Combine soldiers or zombies.

02:03:29.560 --> 02:03:33.250
We found that players really enjoyed being
able to inspect these models,

02:03:33.250 --> 02:03:37.350
because VR let them get much closer than they'd
been able to in the past.

02:03:37.350 --> 02:03:40.500
Players reported getting a sense of the true
size of the characters and

02:03:40.500 --> 02:03:44.010
would often point out details that they had
never noticed before, even

02:03:44.010 --> 02:03:47.640
though they had seen the models many times
throughout Half-Life 2.

02:03:47.640 --> 02:03:51.080
These reactions led us to the idea of creating
an enemy that took advantage

02:03:51.080 --> 02:03:54.430
of that experience, one that required players
to spend long

02:03:54.430 --> 02:03:57.860
periods of time in close physical proximity
to it.

02:03:57.860 --> 02:04:01.580
But enemies typically don't let the player
get near them without attacking,

02:04:01.580 --> 02:04:03.810
so we felt it made fictional sense for this
enemy to

02:04:03.810 --> 02:04:08.520
be blind, and to feature non-combat gameplay
as its focus.

02:04:08.520 --> 02:04:12.160
Early experiments with guiding a blind creature
using sounds were immediately

02:04:12.160 --> 02:04:16.310
interesting and gave us a way to create scenarios
unique to the product.

02:04:16.310 --> 02:04:20.970
Thus, the concept for Jeff⁠, known as the
'blind zombie' internally, was born.

02:04:20.970 --> 02:04:23.450
a3_07_xen_ears
The Xen flora you see on the wall here, which

02:04:23.450 --> 02:04:27.190
we refer to as 'Xen Ears'
for obvious reasons, animate in reaction to

02:04:27.190 --> 02:04:28.920
the same sounds as Jeff.

02:04:28.920 --> 02:04:32.200
Early in development, we would observe situations
where playtesters

02:04:32.200 --> 02:04:35.550
would fail to notice Jeff becoming enraged
by a sound.

02:04:35.550 --> 02:04:39.020
If the player died as a result, they felt
frustrated and wronged.

02:04:39.020 --> 02:04:41.990
We worked to make Jeff's animations and sounds
communicate his level

02:04:41.990 --> 02:04:44.980
of aggression to the player as clearly as
possible, but if

02:04:44.980 --> 02:04:48.080
players weren't looking at Jeff, they could
still be caught unaware.

02:04:48.080 --> 02:04:51.190
Adding these Xen Ear growths to the environment
was a way for

02:04:51.190 --> 02:04:54.360
us to help players be more aware of Jeff's
reactions to sound.

02:04:54.360 --> 02:04:59.360
The Xen Ears work as an extra layer of visual
feedback for Jeff's behavioral state;

02:04:59.360 --> 02:05:01.720
one that lives in the environment instead
of on Jeff himself.

02:05:01.720 --> 02:05:03.880
a3_07_xen_clams
Right from the beginning of development, we

02:05:03.880 --> 02:05:07.460
intended to feature a
variety of visually distinct Xen flora and

02:05:07.460 --> 02:05:09.690
fauna in Half Life: Alyx.

02:05:09.690 --> 02:05:11.910
But we didn't yet know how those visual elements
would

02:05:11.910 --> 02:05:14.770
be used to craft interesting gameplay.

02:05:14.770 --> 02:05:19.100
Referred to internally as 'Xen Clams,' the
Xen plants you see here were originally

02:05:19.100 --> 02:05:22.530
built to add visual flavor to the world and
were only lightly interactive.

02:05:22.530 --> 02:05:26.771
They could track the player's head and hands,
and would close tight if the player got too

02:05:26.771 --> 02:05:28.090
close.

02:05:28.090 --> 02:05:30.480
When we began working on Jeff, we wanted to
find surprising

02:05:30.480 --> 02:05:33.840
new ways to use sound as a core gameplay mechanic.

02:05:33.840 --> 02:05:38.310
In particular, we wanted to make players wary
of the environment around them.

02:05:38.310 --> 02:05:42.050
We realized we could use the Xen Clams as
a type of noise hazard by having

02:05:42.050 --> 02:05:45.970
them repeatedly snap open and shut when approached
by the player.

02:05:45.970 --> 02:05:49.390
We had already placed these clams in earlier
parts of the game,

02:05:49.390 --> 02:05:53.640
which meant that players would reach the distillery
with an understanding of how they functioned.

02:05:53.640 --> 02:05:57.440
The introduction of Jeff changed the player's
relationship with the Xen Clams,

02:05:57.440 --> 02:06:00.670
as they went from being harmless but annoying
to a dangerous hazard

02:06:00.670 --> 02:06:02.940
that players would need to take care to avoid.

02:06:02.940 --> 02:06:05.030
a3_07_larry
Early versions of the distillery explored

02:06:05.030 --> 02:06:08.470
a variety of methods for subtly
teaching players how Jeff works without any

02:06:08.470 --> 02:06:09.900
explicit exposition.

02:06:09.900 --> 02:06:13.360
Though some of the training techniques were
quite successful, anything less

02:06:13.360 --> 02:06:16.830
than a complete understanding of Jeff's behavior
resulted in players bungling

02:06:16.830 --> 02:06:20.580
their way through this section of the game
and having a terrible time doing it.

02:06:20.580 --> 02:06:23.430
Eventually, we determined that explicit training
was necessary,

02:06:23.430 --> 02:06:26.340
and that's where the character of Larry came
in.

02:06:26.340 --> 02:06:29.290
Larry acts as a gate, delivering critical
information about

02:06:29.290 --> 02:06:32.790
Jeff's behavior before allowing the player
to proceed.

02:06:32.790 --> 02:06:36.240
It's a dense learning environment and a delicate
balancing act, ensuring that we

02:06:36.240 --> 02:06:40.450
deliver the rules of Jeff while still making
Larry an interesting character.

02:06:40.450 --> 02:06:43.890
To spread out the exposition, you'll notice
that it's distributed over a few

02:06:43.890 --> 02:06:48.040
different story beats as Larry first introduces
Jeff, then demonstrates Jeff's

02:06:48.040 --> 02:06:51.170
predictable reaction to a breaking bottle
and finally explains that the

02:06:51.170 --> 02:06:54.260
player can prevent their own coughing on Xen
spores by covering their mouth.

02:06:54.260 --> 02:06:56.420
a3_07_coughing
A core element of both the environment and

02:06:56.420 --> 02:06:59.930
Jeff himself are the Xen
plants seen here spewing toxic gas that makes

02:06:59.930 --> 02:07:01.400
the player cough.

02:07:01.400 --> 02:07:04.070
These were not created to be merely ornamental
or to support the

02:07:04.070 --> 02:07:07.360
novel VR interaction of players covering their
mouth.

02:07:07.360 --> 02:07:09.810
They were added specifically to solve a long-standing
challenge

02:07:09.810 --> 02:07:12.940
that we had with illustrating Jeff's personal
space.

02:07:12.940 --> 02:07:15.410
One of the trickier aspects of designing Jeff
was striking the

02:07:15.410 --> 02:07:19.710
right balance between perceived threat and
actual threat.

02:07:19.710 --> 02:07:21.940
Many scenarios in the level force the player
into close

02:07:21.940 --> 02:07:24.800
proximity with Jeff in order to create tension.

02:07:24.800 --> 02:07:27.540
We found that these moments could be exciting,
but they could also become

02:07:27.540 --> 02:07:32.400
frustrating when players accidentally got
too close to Jeff and were killed unexpectedly.

02:07:32.400 --> 02:07:36.080
Players reported not understanding 'how close
is too close.'

02:07:36.080 --> 02:07:39.140
We tried a number of solutions, such as adding
glowing tentacles to Jeff

02:07:39.140 --> 02:07:43.320
that acted as a visual representation of the
perimeter of his personal space.

02:07:43.320 --> 02:07:46.020
Touch the tentacles, and you die.

02:07:46.020 --> 02:07:48.590
Getting the look and feel of these tentacles
to a satisfactory place

02:07:48.590 --> 02:07:52.260
proved challenging, however, and we backed
away from the idea.

02:07:52.260 --> 02:07:54.820
Another problem we encountered was that a
subset of our players quickly

02:07:54.820 --> 02:07:58.130
became comfortable around Jeff once they became
adept at keeping their

02:07:58.130 --> 02:08:00.660
distance from him and avoiding sources of
noise.

02:08:00.660 --> 02:08:03.620
For these players, dealing with Jeff felt
trivial.

02:08:03.620 --> 02:08:05.740
He simply didn't feel like a threat to them.

02:08:05.740 --> 02:08:08.670
We discussed a variety of potential solutions
to these problems,

02:08:08.670 --> 02:08:11.590
even wacky concepts such as giving Jeff the
ability to teleport

02:08:11.590 --> 02:08:14.470
to sources of noise, but it was difficulty
to come up with ideas

02:08:14.470 --> 02:08:16.781
that didn't feel like we were giving Jeff
the ability to cheat.

02:08:16.781 --> 02:08:21.390
Eventually, the we came up with the idea of
having the player cough.

02:08:21.390 --> 02:08:24.010
This was appealing, as it gave us a way to
make the player themselves

02:08:24.010 --> 02:08:26.390
a source of noise, thus causing Jeff to pursue
them.

02:08:26.390 --> 02:08:29.280
We were also interested in making the environment
more dangerous

02:08:29.280 --> 02:08:31.900
generally, and so our first experiments with
coughing involved

02:08:31.900 --> 02:08:35.470
littering the level with Xen plants spewing
toxic gas.

02:08:35.470 --> 02:08:37.510
Initial tests showed a lot of promise.

02:08:37.510 --> 02:08:39.780
Players now felt like Jeff was a dangerous
pursuer and they

02:08:39.780 --> 02:08:43.370
must carefully observe the environment to
avoid the gas.

02:08:43.370 --> 02:08:45.800
We observed that players had a natural instinct
to cover their

02:08:45.800 --> 02:08:48.280
mouth with their hand to suppress their cough.

02:08:48.280 --> 02:08:50.740
This was an affordance that VR with tracked
hand controllers

02:08:50.740 --> 02:08:54.920
supported very naturally, so we implemented
this mechanic right away.

02:08:54.920 --> 02:08:57.240
This still left us with the problem of players
being unsure

02:08:57.240 --> 02:08:59.820
of how close they could safely get to Jeff.

02:08:59.820 --> 02:09:02.860
We realized that the earlier experiment of
adding tentacles to Jeff could be

02:09:02.860 --> 02:09:07.500
replaced with the much simpler approach of
attaching a toxic cloud directly to him.

02:09:07.500 --> 02:09:09.920
There were multiple benefits to this.

02:09:09.920 --> 02:09:12.730
First, it gave players visual feedback for
how far they

02:09:12.730 --> 02:09:15.020
needed to stay away from Jeff to be safe.

02:09:15.020 --> 02:09:19.620
Second, it made those times when Jeff did
get close all the more tense,

02:09:19.620 --> 02:09:22.780
with players now needing to commit a hand
to covering their mouth.

02:09:22.780 --> 02:09:25.800
Finally, we didn't need to teach the players
anything new.

02:09:25.800 --> 02:09:27.860
They could carry forward what they'd already
learned about

02:09:27.860 --> 02:09:31.230
toxic gas in the environment, and apply those
lessons to Jeff himself.

02:09:31.230 --> 02:09:33.670
a3_07_single_controller
One of our major goals in the design of Half-Life:

02:09:33.670 --> 02:09:36.810
Alyx was
to reach the widest possible audience.

02:09:36.810 --> 02:09:40.340
This meant developing for all of the major
VR hardware in the market as well

02:09:40.340 --> 02:09:45.070
as accommodating different sized play spaces,
traversal preferences and so on.

02:09:45.070 --> 02:09:48.351
The accessibility option that had the most
knock-on effects into the design

02:09:48.351 --> 02:09:52.470
of the game, from controller bindings to game
logic to level design

02:09:52.470 --> 02:09:56.840
was the support of a single controller mode,
for players who don’t use two controllers.

02:09:56.840 --> 02:09:59.900
In this mode, reloading is performed by moving
the weapon to the shoulder

02:09:59.900 --> 02:10:05.890
and pressing a button, much like arcade games
which

02:10:05.890 --> 02:10:07.269
require the player to fire their weapon off
screen to reload.

02:10:07.269 --> 02:10:10.550
The flashlight was modified to attach to the
primary hand and gas masks

02:10:10.550 --> 02:10:14.450
were added so that players could muffle their
coughs hands free.

02:10:14.450 --> 02:10:17.380
Even the gun used in the final Strider battle,
with its two-handed

02:10:17.380 --> 02:10:21.470
aiming and reloading controls was modified
to be operable with one hand.

02:10:21.470 --> 02:10:24.190
a3_07_jeff_attack
As we refined Jeff's behavior and visual design,

02:10:24.190 --> 02:10:26.970
we thought
lot about how we wanted players to feel about

02:10:26.970 --> 02:10:27.970
him.

02:10:27.970 --> 02:10:30.630
The goal was to have playtesters describe
Jeff with words

02:10:30.630 --> 02:10:33.340
such as 'menacing,' 'threatening' and 'deadly.'

02:10:33.340 --> 02:10:35.630
And for that reason, it became important that
players not

02:10:35.630 --> 02:10:39.030
survive if they got too close to Jeff, so
we decided that his

02:10:39.030 --> 02:10:40.970
attack should result in instant death.

02:10:40.970 --> 02:10:44.820
This would teach players unambiguously that
Jeff was too dangerous to

02:10:44.820 --> 02:10:47.650
trifle with, but it left us with the challenge
of teaching this

02:10:47.650 --> 02:10:49.870
to players without necessarily killing them.

02:10:49.870 --> 02:10:53.390
The approach we took was to give the player
opportunities to

02:10:53.390 --> 02:10:56.760
witness Jeff attack and kill other characters.

02:10:56.760 --> 02:10:59.530
Early versions of the level included scenarios
where Jeff and

02:10:59.530 --> 02:11:02.670
the player would encounter zombies and Combine
soldiers.

02:11:02.670 --> 02:11:05.850
The player could attack these enemies, or
use sound to lure

02:11:05.850 --> 02:11:09.540
Jeff into place so that he could pummel them
with his massive arms.

02:11:09.540 --> 02:11:12.210
This gave players an opportunity to witness
Jeff's power and his

02:11:12.210 --> 02:11:15.640
method of attack, without dying at his hands
themselves.

02:11:15.640 --> 02:11:19.260
Unfortunately, this outcome wasn't guaranteed,
as some players

02:11:19.260 --> 02:11:21.090
would opt to kill those enemies themselves.

02:11:21.090 --> 02:11:24.090
And having these other characters present
was also quite expensive

02:11:24.090 --> 02:11:27.450
in terms of animation, sound and level content.

02:11:27.450 --> 02:11:29.570
We were already grappling with the challenge
of keeping this

02:11:29.570 --> 02:11:34.050
level well-paced and within scope, and so
we cut these sections.

02:11:34.050 --> 02:11:36.510
But this still left us with the original problem
of how to

02:11:36.510 --> 02:11:40.110
demonstrate Jeff's ability to kill, without
killing the player.

02:11:40.110 --> 02:11:44.590
We chose to address this by adding beats to
existing animated sequences.

02:11:44.590 --> 02:11:48.180
For example, when the player opens this two-handed
roller door,

02:11:48.180 --> 02:11:52.260
we added a headcrab for Jeff to munch on in
full view of the player.

02:11:52.260 --> 02:11:55.700
We also added a similar beat to the later
elevator ride, with Jeff

02:11:55.700 --> 02:11:58.740
smashing a headcrab against the wall before
melting it down with acid.

02:11:58.740 --> 02:12:00.550
a3_07_training_alcove
Even though Larry instructs the player on

02:12:00.550 --> 02:12:04.371
the basics of how Jeff works,
we found that we needed to let players put

02:12:04.371 --> 02:12:06.610
those rules to
test without having Jeff in their immediate

02:12:06.610 --> 02:12:07.680
personal space.

02:12:07.680 --> 02:12:11.090
If we made players first interact with Jeff
face-to-face,

02:12:11.090 --> 02:12:14.790
they often fell to pieces and forgot the rules.

02:12:14.790 --> 02:12:18.470
They would die over and over again, and their
fear of Jeff

02:12:18.470 --> 02:12:22.030
quickly turned into frustration and annoyance.

02:12:22.030 --> 02:12:25.970
This area was created to serve as a training
playground, where players can

02:12:25.970 --> 02:12:30.550
observe Jeff reacting to various sounds while
remaining safe from his attacks.

02:12:30.550 --> 02:12:33.900
It contains many of the elements that are
encountered throughout the rest

02:12:33.900 --> 02:12:37.450
of the level including falling bottles, a
padlock that

02:12:37.450 --> 02:12:41.420
needs to be shot, and various types of Xen
plants.

02:12:41.420 --> 02:12:45.830
Jeff remains close enough to feel threatening,
but he cannot attack the player.

02:12:45.830 --> 02:12:48.440
Players can spend as much time as they need
here,

02:12:48.440 --> 02:12:51.920
and move on when they feel like they have
a good grip on how Jeff works.

02:12:51.920 --> 02:12:53.970
a3_07_jeff_design
Jeff's character design went through several

02:12:53.970 --> 02:12:57.200
significant
revisions over the course of development.

02:12:57.200 --> 02:12:59.290
Just by looking at him, we wanted players
to understand

02:12:59.290 --> 02:13:01.580
certain things about the character.

02:13:01.580 --> 02:13:04.510
Our first priority was to communicate his
blindness.

02:13:04.510 --> 02:13:08.130
Second, we wanted the character to feel physically
imposing.

02:13:08.130 --> 02:13:11.740
After all, the entire concept of Jeff had
grown out of our observation that

02:13:11.740 --> 02:13:15.920
just being close to a character in VR could
make for a powerful experience.

02:13:15.920 --> 02:13:20.930
Finally, we wanted players to understand the
blind zombie's behavioral state at a glance.

02:13:20.930 --> 02:13:22.430
Is he feeling angry?

02:13:22.430 --> 02:13:24.750
Did he just hear something and is feeling
curious?

02:13:24.750 --> 02:13:25.880
And so on.

02:13:25.880 --> 02:13:30.570
The first design that we modeled and animated
was a sort of Combine worker-robot.

02:13:30.570 --> 02:13:33.220
The robot's face featured a holographic display
that could flip

02:13:33.220 --> 02:13:36.340
between different icons that represented its
state.

02:13:36.340 --> 02:13:39.910
A large piece of rebar jutted out the back
of the robot's head,

02:13:39.910 --> 02:13:42.150
sparking electricity and spewing smoke.

02:13:42.150 --> 02:13:47.060
This was meant to demonstrate that its visual
sensors were impaired, making it blind.

02:13:47.060 --> 02:13:49.170
We made the robot tall and hulking.

02:13:49.170 --> 02:13:51.920
It looked heavy, unpredictable and dangerous.

02:13:51.920 --> 02:13:56.840
However, player reactions to this robotic
design showed that it had problems.

02:13:56.840 --> 02:14:00.070
Players would initially focus on the head's
holographic display.

02:14:00.070 --> 02:14:03.400
Parsing the face and understanding the meaning
of the icons proved too

02:14:03.400 --> 02:14:07.390
challenging for players, and even worse, this
element distracted from the

02:14:07.390 --> 02:14:12.060
rebar poking through the robot's head and
their understanding that it was blind.

02:14:12.060 --> 02:14:14.490
Players found the robot imposing, but we weren't
getting the

02:14:14.490 --> 02:14:17.780
deeply visceral reaction of fear that we desired.

02:14:17.780 --> 02:14:20.170
A trip back to the drawing board landed us
with the more

02:14:20.170 --> 02:14:22.770
organic character you see in front of you.

02:14:22.770 --> 02:14:26.550
Drawing inspiration from the idea of coal
miners succumbing to the black lung,

02:14:26.550 --> 02:14:29.670
we focused on the concept of a member of the
Combine's Xen

02:14:29.670 --> 02:14:32.740
cleanup crew that had succumbed to Xen infection.

02:14:32.740 --> 02:14:36.090
This gave us the visceral reaction from players
which we were looking for.

02:14:36.090 --> 02:14:41.670
A human face is still visible, but the eyes
are clearly decayed, communicating blindness.

02:14:41.670 --> 02:14:45.130
The organic nature of this design allowed
us to lean more heavily on audio

02:14:45.130 --> 02:14:49.580
to feed back the behavioral state, with animalistic
grunts and roars

02:14:49.580 --> 02:14:52.500
that came to include more human-sounding elements
over time.

02:14:52.500 --> 02:14:54.910
a3_07_freezer_toner
Toner puzzles are a useful design tool since

02:14:54.910 --> 02:14:58.590
they force players to move around
the environment and also occupy their primary

02:14:58.590 --> 02:15:00.810
hand via use of the multitool.

02:15:00.810 --> 02:15:04.110
In this area, the player has to aim their
flashlight to navigate,

02:15:04.110 --> 02:15:07.590
point their off hand to teleport, cover their
mouth to prevent coughing,

02:15:07.590 --> 02:15:11.800
follow wires with the multitool and potentially
pull bottles with their gravity gloves.

02:15:11.800 --> 02:15:16.110
The player has to make tradeoffs as they proceed,
since their two hands are over committed by

02:15:16.110 --> 02:15:17.110
design.

02:15:17.110 --> 02:15:20.290
One-handed players are even more overwhelmed
by these many tasks.

02:15:20.290 --> 02:15:23.200
This is why we littered the distillery with
gas masks the player can

02:15:23.200 --> 02:15:26.820
attach to their mouth so they won't have to
use a hand to muffle their cough.

02:15:26.820 --> 02:15:29.660
This toner puzzle went through several revisions,
many of which

02:15:29.660 --> 02:15:32.850
left Jeff free to roam while players worked
on the puzzle.

02:15:32.850 --> 02:15:35.990
But this resulted in players having to juggle
too many elements at once,

02:15:35.990 --> 02:15:39.180
and their fear of Jeff quickly turned into
frustration.

02:15:39.180 --> 02:15:42.710
The solution was to keep Jeff locked up while
players solved the puzzle,

02:15:42.710 --> 02:15:45.840
with his distant roars maintaining a level
of tension.

02:15:45.840 --> 02:15:47.700
We were then left with the problem of how
to release

02:15:47.700 --> 02:15:50.480
Jeff so that the level could continue.

02:15:50.480 --> 02:15:52.980
Initial ideas involved having Jeff break out
from the freezer on his

02:15:52.980 --> 02:15:57.030
own when the puzzle was complete, but this
felt contrived and scripted,

02:15:57.030 --> 02:15:59.840
compared to having the player free Jeff from
the freezer.

02:15:59.840 --> 02:16:03.050
The toner puzzle mechanic gave us a logical
solution to this problem,

02:16:03.050 --> 02:16:06.120
with the added benefit of creating a moment
of high tension for the

02:16:06.120 --> 02:16:08.090
player when they realize they're going to
have to open

02:16:08.090 --> 02:16:10.710
the freezer door and release Jeff in order
to proceed.

02:16:10.710 --> 02:16:13.130
a3_07_elevator
Many of our ideas for Jeff, involved coming

02:16:13.130 --> 02:16:17.140
up with plausible ways to force
Jeff and the player to occupy an enclosed

02:16:17.140 --> 02:16:19.410
space at the same time.

02:16:19.410 --> 02:16:22.140
An elevator ride was on the table from the
beginning, but we

02:16:22.140 --> 02:16:25.420
were unsure of how successful it would be
in execution.

02:16:25.420 --> 02:16:30.110
There was also a concern that such a sequence
could be technically expensive to pull off.

02:16:30.110 --> 02:16:33.120
Our first prototype was extremely rudimentary.

02:16:33.120 --> 02:16:35.760
Players would get into an elevator with Jeff,
the ride would last

02:16:35.760 --> 02:16:39.301
around thirty seconds, and then Jeff and the
player would exit.

02:16:39.301 --> 02:16:43.570
There was no complicated level scripting,
no bespoke animations for Jeff,

02:16:43.570 --> 02:16:46.240
and no special audio or lighting treatment.

02:16:46.240 --> 02:16:51.340
Despite the relative lack of polish, players
had a strong positive reaction to the sequence,

02:16:51.340 --> 02:16:55.570
even naming it a highlight of the game when
we ran company-wide playtests.

02:16:55.570 --> 02:16:58.400
This convinced us that it was worth putting
more work into the

02:16:58.400 --> 02:17:01.700
elevator ride to make it something really
special.

02:17:01.700 --> 02:17:04.740
In the end, a lot of different disciplines
came together to create

02:17:04.740 --> 02:17:09.310
the final sequence, which combines sound,
animation, scripted lighting,

02:17:09.310 --> 02:17:13.099
and careful choreography of Jeff's movements
and the player's actions.

02:17:13.099 --> 02:17:15.150
a3_07_cheeky_headcrab
Most of the time, the noises that attract

02:17:15.150 --> 02:17:18.510
Jeff are created by the player,
who is either actively trying to lure him

02:17:18.510 --> 02:17:22.059
to a new location,
or has perhaps made a noisy mistake that could

02:17:22.059 --> 02:17:23.090
have been avoided.

02:17:23.090 --> 02:17:26.490
The key point being that the player feels
some amount of responsibility for the noises

02:17:26.490 --> 02:17:27.719
being made.

02:17:27.719 --> 02:17:32.300
The goal of the upcoming hallway was to flip
that idea on its head, if only for a moment.

02:17:32.300 --> 02:17:36.830
Like a misbehaving pet, this cheeky headcrab
appears to surprise and infuriate the player,

02:17:36.830 --> 02:17:40.550
clumsily knocking over a series of bottles
before escaping into a vent.

02:17:40.550 --> 02:17:42.040
Classic headcrab nonsense!

02:17:42.040 --> 02:17:44.309
We were surprised to find that many players
wanted to check

02:17:44.309 --> 02:17:46.940
and see if the headcrab had indeed escaped.

02:17:46.940 --> 02:17:51.040
They would climb up onto the boxes and crane
their head up into the vents.

02:17:51.040 --> 02:17:54.719
This prompted the idea of adding a small easter
egg to reward those players.

02:17:54.719 --> 02:17:58.270
Try it, and you'll find that the cheeky crab
has indeed escaped to be annoying another

02:17:58.270 --> 02:17:59.270
day.

02:17:59.270 --> 02:18:01.400
The cost of adding these sorts of secrets
need to be weighed

02:18:01.400 --> 02:18:04.059
against the potential for players to actual
discover them.

02:18:04.059 --> 02:18:07.690
In this case, our playtest observations made
the cost appear worth it,

02:18:07.690 --> 02:18:11.190
in particular for the impact it would have
in making the world feel more real:

02:18:11.190 --> 02:18:14.160
a full reality where living creatures go about
their lives

02:18:14.160 --> 02:18:15.830
even if the player isn't there to witness
them.

02:18:15.830 --> 02:18:18.240
a3_07_combine_forcefields
Since the entire premise of Jeff hinged on

02:18:18.240 --> 02:18:21.851
keeping Jeff and the player
in close proximity to each other, the distillery

02:18:21.851 --> 02:18:25.300
map was constructed
as a series of chambers connected by one-way

02:18:25.300 --> 02:18:26.760
transitions.

02:18:26.760 --> 02:18:29.519
Early on, we used two techniques in concert
to keep Jeff and

02:18:29.519 --> 02:18:31.710
the player together throughout the level.

02:18:31.710 --> 02:18:35.330
First, each new area was gated with a special
locked door.

02:18:35.330 --> 02:18:39.551
These took the form of impenetrable 'Xen Membranes'
that only Jeff could tear through.

02:18:39.551 --> 02:18:42.430
The player would lure Jeff to the membrane
using sound, and Jeff

02:18:42.430 --> 02:18:45.639
would break it open so that both Jeff and
the player could proceed.

02:18:45.639 --> 02:18:49.760
We had always had a goal of having players
describe Jeff in adversarial terms,

02:18:49.760 --> 02:18:52.849
as a constant pursuer or 'thorn in their side.'

02:18:52.849 --> 02:18:55.920
The problem with the Xen Membrane concept
was that it lead to players

02:18:55.920 --> 02:19:01.179
describing Jeff as a useful tool, or worse,
'angry co-op partner,' rather than something

02:19:01.179 --> 02:19:02.179
they feared.

02:19:02.179 --> 02:19:04.519
Another way that we would keep Jeff and the
player together

02:19:04.519 --> 02:19:06.889
was via the use of one-way drops.

02:19:06.889 --> 02:19:10.770
Jeff would fall off of catwalks, platforms
and ladders into each new area.

02:19:10.770 --> 02:19:14.559
These drops functioned as intended, but just
like the Xen Membranes,

02:19:14.559 --> 02:19:19.710
elicited an undesirable reaction from players,
especially when repeated multiple times.

02:19:19.710 --> 02:19:23.610
Watching Jeff do these pratfalls left players
feeling like he was less dangerous.

02:19:23.610 --> 02:19:26.139
They felt sorry for him and even laughed at
him.

02:19:26.139 --> 02:19:29.430
Eventually, we realized that we could use
Combine force-fields,

02:19:29.430 --> 02:19:33.240
which the player cannot pass through, as a
way to create 'one-way doors.'

02:19:33.240 --> 02:19:35.671
These force-fields allowed Jeff to move through
them,

02:19:35.671 --> 02:19:39.139
but required the player to follow via another
one-way path.

02:19:39.139 --> 02:19:41.639
This allowed us to build thresholds where
Jeff and the player could

02:19:41.639 --> 02:19:45.950
both move forward while eliminating the awkwardness
of the Xen Membranes

02:19:45.950 --> 02:19:50.570
and avoiding the repeated falls that had previously
made Jeff look so clumsy.

02:19:50.570 --> 02:19:53.070
Combine force-fields also had a few other
benefits.

02:19:53.070 --> 02:19:56.570
For one, they gave Jeff a convenient way to
take a 'backstage' route

02:19:56.570 --> 02:20:00.590
that is inaccessible to the player, like the
one at the bottom of this ladder.

02:20:00.590 --> 02:20:05.130
They also tied in nicely with Jeff's fictional
background as a former member of the Combine

02:20:05.130 --> 02:20:06.130
Xen cleanup crew.

02:20:06.130 --> 02:20:08.330
a3_07_sounds
Creating an experience with such a heavy focus

02:20:08.330 --> 02:20:11.021
on audio
presented some unique design problems.

02:20:11.021 --> 02:20:14.090
The primary challenge was determining which
sounds Jeff should be able

02:20:14.090 --> 02:20:17.080
to hear and making sure that players understood
the rules.

02:20:17.080 --> 02:20:20.771
Half Life: Alyx contains a vast array of physics
objects.

02:20:20.771 --> 02:20:23.500
However, through playtesting, we found it
best to use

02:20:23.500 --> 02:20:26.260
only a small set of them in the distillery.

02:20:26.260 --> 02:20:29.800
We needed to pay close attention to each object
that we did include,

02:20:29.800 --> 02:20:32.721
particularly to audio design for collisions
between the object and

02:20:32.721 --> 02:20:35.641
the various surface types used in the distillery.

02:20:35.641 --> 02:20:37.641
It was important that what players heard matched
their

02:20:37.641 --> 02:20:40.670
expectations for how Jeff would react.

02:20:40.670 --> 02:20:43.880
In early testing, we would find individual
cases where players would say

02:20:43.880 --> 02:20:46.430
things like 'I don't know why Jeff got angry'
or

02:20:46.430 --> 02:20:49.470
'I would have expected that noise to attract
Jeff.'

02:20:49.470 --> 02:20:53.550
Over time, we identified these cases of confusion,
and addressed them one by one.

02:20:53.550 --> 02:20:56.561
It was vital that players always be able to
identify the cause

02:20:56.561 --> 02:20:59.780
and effect of a noise that might attract Jeff.

02:20:59.780 --> 02:21:01.850
Failing this meant players were left feeling
like Jeff

02:21:01.850 --> 02:21:05.170
acted randomly, and they'd grow frustrated.

02:21:05.170 --> 02:21:07.320
Some experiments failed in this area.

02:21:07.320 --> 02:21:10.400
At one point, parts of the level were littered
with piles of broken glass

02:21:10.400 --> 02:21:14.510
that would make noise if the player walked
or teleported over them.

02:21:14.510 --> 02:21:17.290
Few players would notice the broken glass
and were then surprised

02:21:17.290 --> 02:21:21.580
when Jeff attacked, so this concept was cut
to avoid confusion.

02:21:21.580 --> 02:21:24.180
We had to be wary of the opposite case too,
where players would

02:21:24.180 --> 02:21:27.530
expect purely aesthetic elements to present
a noise hazard.

02:21:27.530 --> 02:21:31.730
For example, at one point we had placed shallow
pools of water seeping in among the floor

02:21:31.730 --> 02:21:32.730
tiles.

02:21:32.730 --> 02:21:36.271
It looked really cool, but it confused some
of our playtesters who thought

02:21:36.271 --> 02:21:39.040
that the sound of their footsteps splashing
through these puddles would

02:21:39.040 --> 02:21:42.600
be audible to Jeff, even though we never intended
that to be the case.

02:21:42.600 --> 02:21:44.380
a3_07_jeff_death
Figuring out how to end the player's journey

02:21:44.380 --> 02:21:47.590
through the distillery involved a
lot of discussion about how players would

02:21:47.590 --> 02:21:50.700
ultimately end up feeling about Jeff.

02:21:50.700 --> 02:21:53.010
Observing playtests told us that most players
simply wanted

02:21:53.010 --> 02:21:55.520
him dead, usually from the moment they met
him.

02:21:55.520 --> 02:21:57.590
But there were some who felt kind of sorry
for him and

02:21:57.590 --> 02:22:00.380
didn't necessarily want to see him killed.

02:22:00.380 --> 02:22:03.570
Our first version of the ending involved the
player's actions unintentionally

02:22:03.570 --> 02:22:06.610
resulting in Jeff being attacked and dragged
away by antlions.

02:22:06.610 --> 02:22:09.620
Part of the goal was to demonstrate that the
antlions are even more

02:22:09.620 --> 02:22:12.780
powerful than Jeff, to amp up the tension
just before players have

02:22:12.780 --> 02:22:14.820
to square off against antlions themselves.

02:22:14.820 --> 02:22:18.250
However, this ending left players feeling
a little empty, they felt

02:22:18.250 --> 02:22:21.590
detached from Jeff's demise and wanted to
be more directly involved.

02:22:21.590 --> 02:22:23.370
Dark stuff, to be sure.

02:22:23.370 --> 02:22:26.490
Plus, we weren't really servicing those players
who felt sorry

02:22:26.490 --> 02:22:30.110
for Jeff and didn't necessarily want to see
him killed.

02:22:30.110 --> 02:22:31.400
There were other problems too.

02:22:31.400 --> 02:22:35.200
The antlion attack felt random to many players,
which reduced its impact.

02:22:35.200 --> 02:22:40.171
And because Jeff presumably died off-screen,
players were left without a sense of closure.

02:22:40.171 --> 02:22:43.030
This prevented players from being able to
switch gears and

02:22:43.030 --> 02:22:45.471
settle in for their encounter with the antlions.

02:22:45.471 --> 02:22:47.790
The trash compactor was the solution to these
problems.

02:22:47.790 --> 02:22:50.740
It gave players the opportunity to decide
Jeff's fate and be

02:22:50.740 --> 02:22:55.800
left with a sense of closure, whether they
decide to squash him or just leave him imprisoned.

02:22:55.800 --> 02:23:00.310
Even so, it has to be admitted that most players
seem to crush Jeff with zero hesitation.

02:23:00.310 --> 02:23:01.310
Monsters.

02:23:01.310 --> 02:23:03.300
a4_01_antlion_combat
In some of our early experiments bringing

02:23:03.300 --> 02:23:07.230
Half-Life 2 enemies to VR,
we found that antlions in particular showed

02:23:07.230 --> 02:23:11.050
promise and were interesting
to fight, but tended to quickly overwhelm

02:23:11.050 --> 02:23:12.050
playtesters.

02:23:12.050 --> 02:23:15.120
They were able to get into the player's physical
space too quickly and

02:23:15.120 --> 02:23:17.510
applied so much pressure that players just
ended up endlessly

02:23:17.510 --> 02:23:19.990
teleporting around, trying to get away.

02:23:19.990 --> 02:23:22.660
Around the same time, we were discovering
that all players,

02:23:22.660 --> 02:23:25.240
even relative novices, have surprisingly good
aim in VR.

02:23:25.240 --> 02:23:28.980
The variability of aiming ability amongst
players is simply

02:23:28.980 --> 02:23:32.561
much lower in VR than it is in mouse-and-keyboard
games.

02:23:32.561 --> 02:23:35.650
To take advantage of the idea that players
would consistently be able to

02:23:35.650 --> 02:23:38.970
make precise shots, we started testing an
antlion design which

02:23:38.970 --> 02:23:42.490
players could slow down by shooting off the
legs.

02:23:42.490 --> 02:23:45.720
Playtesters reacted positively to both the
need for accuracy and to the

02:23:45.720 --> 02:23:48.350
organic feeling that this gave the antlions
when their legs flew off

02:23:48.350 --> 02:23:51.471
and the injured antlion continued to limp
toward them.

02:23:51.471 --> 02:23:54.890
The removal of the legs was clear to players,
but presenting the fact that the

02:23:54.890 --> 02:23:59.440
abdomen was invulnerable until the legs were
removed was a longstanding challenge.

02:23:59.440 --> 02:24:01.990
There was no single solution to communicating
this effectively to

02:24:01.990 --> 02:24:06.460
playtesters but, rather, a series of small
additions that communicated the behavior.

02:24:06.460 --> 02:24:10.290
For example, we made the abdomen dull and
black when invulnerable,

02:24:10.290 --> 02:24:13.690
and changed it to match the bright orange
legs as it became vulnerable.

02:24:13.690 --> 02:24:16.050
Shooting armored parts of the antlion would
generate sparks and hard

02:24:16.050 --> 02:24:19.061
ricochet sounds, while shooting the vulnerable
body parts resulted

02:24:19.061 --> 02:24:21.990
in orange blood spurts and soft impact sounds.

02:24:21.990 --> 02:24:25.521
We also designed the death animations to emphasize
the abdomen exploding,

02:24:25.521 --> 02:24:29.950
and ensured that an exploded abdomen chunk
was left behind after the antlion was dead.

02:24:29.950 --> 02:24:31.760
a4_01_foliage
All of the foliage in the game animates in

02:24:31.760 --> 02:24:35.131
response to player touch and environmental
wind conditions.

02:24:35.131 --> 02:24:39.040
The foliage animation is entirely procedural
and is computed on the graphics processor

02:24:39.040 --> 02:24:40.740
using vertex shader deformation.

02:24:40.740 --> 02:24:45.030
A voxel field that travels with the player's
body is used to track the location of the

02:24:45.030 --> 02:24:46.460
player's hands in space.

02:24:46.460 --> 02:24:50.070
The hands effectively draw motion trails into
this voxel field, which can be

02:24:50.070 --> 02:24:54.340
sampled by the foliage's vertex shader and
used to drive deformations,

02:24:54.340 --> 02:24:57.230
allowing the player to bat the foliage around
with their hands.

02:24:57.230 --> 02:25:01.840
The foliage also interacts with wind, allowing
us to give ambient motion to the plants.

02:25:01.840 --> 02:25:05.080
In order to accommodate a range of environmental
design, we tuned the wind

02:25:05.080 --> 02:25:09.360
deformations at different speeds, from slight
drafts to hurricane force winds.

02:25:09.360 --> 02:25:12.120
It turned out, though, that the ambient wind
was never required to be more

02:25:12.120 --> 02:25:16.940
than a moderate breeze, and for most of development,
the hurricane effect was unused.

02:25:16.940 --> 02:25:20.190
This particular zoo habitat was added late
in development and required a

02:25:20.190 --> 02:25:24.000
critical extension to the procedural foliage
animation system.

02:25:24.000 --> 02:25:26.990
Prior to adding this habitat, the foliage
didn't need to deform in response

02:25:26.990 --> 02:25:30.830
to enemy animation or grenade explosions,
but of course this area is

02:25:30.830 --> 02:25:33.660
all about throwing grenades at zombies.

02:25:33.660 --> 02:25:36.601
To address this, we added a low-resolution
voxel field that can be

02:25:36.601 --> 02:25:40.770
sampled by the foliage shader to apply additional
deformations.

02:25:40.770 --> 02:25:44.420
This low resolution voxel field, which is
about one cubic foot per voxel,

02:25:44.420 --> 02:25:47.280
is fixed in space and spans the entire habitat.

02:25:47.280 --> 02:25:51.430
For comparison, the voxel field that travels
with player is about two cubic inches per

02:25:51.430 --> 02:25:52.840
voxel.

02:25:52.840 --> 02:25:55.880
Enemies and grenades within this space can
write motion trails and impacts

02:25:55.880 --> 02:25:59.760
into the low resolution voxel field, which
are ultimately interpreted by the foliage

02:25:59.760 --> 02:26:01.610
shader as wind forces.

02:26:01.610 --> 02:26:04.320
This is when preparation met opportunity.

02:26:04.320 --> 02:26:08.320
When grenades explode, they are creating localized
hurricane-force winds.

02:26:08.320 --> 02:26:11.070
a4_01_antlion_sounds
There were very few existing Antlion sounds

02:26:11.070 --> 02:26:15.050
to work with coming
into Half-Life: Alyx, so unlike most of the

02:26:15.050 --> 02:26:18.521
familiar Half-Life
creatures, which often had legacy sounds layered

02:26:18.521 --> 02:26:21.670
in, nearly all of the
sounds for the Antlions were completely new

02:26:21.670 --> 02:26:23.290
material.

02:26:23.290 --> 02:26:26.420
Starting with an almost blank canvas, we drew
inspiration from

02:26:26.420 --> 02:26:29.980
the behavior of the Antlions to inform their
sound design.

02:26:29.980 --> 02:26:33.250
Since the Antlions attack in waves, it seemed
reasonable to us

02:26:33.250 --> 02:26:36.980
that they communicated and organized into
groups for attack.

02:26:36.980 --> 02:26:41.760
This idea led us to incorporate complex insect-like
clicking sounds,

02:26:41.760 --> 02:26:45.590
the final version of which began as recordings
of European Starlings,

02:26:45.590 --> 02:26:50.210
which turned out to provide more variation
and character than insect recordings.

02:26:50.210 --> 02:27:06.550
Here, you can hear the original European Starling
sounds.

02:27:06.550 --> 02:27:10.760
We took those recordings and layered them
with designed elements to create the Antlion

02:27:10.760 --> 02:27:11.950
sounds used in the game.

02:27:11.950 --> 02:27:14.561
a4_01_anim_interactables
In traditional games, analog controls like

02:27:14.561 --> 02:27:18.830
levers, dials and crank wheels
usually play simple animations in response

02:27:18.830 --> 02:27:21.450
to a player's mouse click or key press.

02:27:21.450 --> 02:27:24.880
In VR, however, players with tracked hand
controllers expect to be able

02:27:24.880 --> 02:27:29.350
to control analog interfaces by grabbing and
moving them directly.

02:27:29.350 --> 02:27:34.351
To support this, we developed a new system
that we call 'Anim Interactables.'

02:27:34.351 --> 02:27:38.740
The opening mechanism of this health station
is a good example of an Anim Interactable.

02:27:38.740 --> 02:27:41.990
To your left and right, you can see some more
examples, such as a large

02:27:41.990 --> 02:27:46.221
railroad switch, the Vortigaunt's fire alarm
doorbell, a crank wheel,

02:27:46.221 --> 02:27:51.910
a rolling cabinet door and both the antenna
and tuning dial on the radio.

02:27:51.910 --> 02:27:54.990
Anim Interactables like these allow us to
give the player control of analog

02:27:54.990 --> 02:27:59.670
interfaces while constraining their input
to an authored range of motion.

02:27:59.670 --> 02:28:02.930
The system uses the position and orientation
of the player's hand to

02:28:02.930 --> 02:28:07.460
drive the animation of a model which defines
the analog interface.

02:28:07.460 --> 02:28:10.121
As the player moves their tracked controller,
the Anim Interactable

02:28:10.121 --> 02:28:13.480
continuously performs a search to determine
if playing the animation

02:28:13.480 --> 02:28:16.890
forward or backward will place the interaction
point closer to the

02:28:16.890 --> 02:28:19.840
tracked controller's position in space.

02:28:19.840 --> 02:28:24.711
This allows the player to drag the animation
forward, backward, or hold it stationary.

02:28:24.711 --> 02:28:30.500
he result is an intuitive correspondence between
the player's body and the virtual interface.

02:28:30.500 --> 02:28:33.780
Furthermore, these interactive animations
don't necessarily have to make

02:28:33.780 --> 02:28:37.980
physical sense; artists simply animate the
range of motion they want the player to be

02:28:37.980 --> 02:28:39.800
able to control.

02:28:39.800 --> 02:28:43.200
These Anim Interactables have their debug
visualizations turned on,

02:28:43.200 --> 02:28:46.420
so you can see the authored range of motion,
including the non-linear paths

02:28:46.420 --> 02:28:50.851
used on the railroad switch, the health station
and the crank wheel.

02:28:50.851 --> 02:28:54.110
Anim Interactables allowed us to make all
kinds of objects in the world

02:28:54.110 --> 02:28:58.960
interactive, from mundane man-made devices
to intricate Combine mechanisms,

02:28:58.960 --> 02:29:02.360
without the need to run complex physical simulations.

02:29:02.360 --> 02:29:05.900
Feel free to play around with the Anim Interactables
in this area before moving on.

02:29:05.900 --> 02:29:07.990
a4_01_storage_toner1
Sometimes large chunks of a level are shifted

02:29:07.990 --> 02:29:10.820
around or re-ordered as we work on the game.

02:29:10.820 --> 02:29:13.970
That's the case for this entire area and the
toner puzzle it's centered around.

02:29:13.970 --> 02:29:19.471
In fact, for much of its existence, this area
didn't even have a home anywhere in the game;

02:29:19.471 --> 02:29:23.540
it was originally built just to help prototype
the toner puzzle mechanic.

02:29:23.540 --> 02:29:26.700
Over the course of a few months, the raw gameplay
mechanics of toner puzzles

02:29:26.700 --> 02:29:30.790
were developed and playtested in a series
of small test levels.

02:29:30.790 --> 02:29:33.670
Each test level explored a single gameplay
concept.

02:29:33.670 --> 02:29:36.200
Those were things like using the toner puzzle
to draw the player's

02:29:36.200 --> 02:29:39.740
eyes to something interesting, such as Xen
flora and fauna.

02:29:39.740 --> 02:29:43.400
Creating moments of surprise when an enemy
headcrab popped out of a vent.

02:29:43.400 --> 02:29:48.800
Or requiring the player to thread their arm
and hand in behind pipes or other tight spaces.

02:29:48.800 --> 02:29:51.410
Once we figured out which of these ideas were
working well, we combined

02:29:51.410 --> 02:29:53.920
them into a single test level with a toner
puzzle that strung

02:29:53.920 --> 02:29:57.351
all of these concepts together in one single
puzzle.

02:29:57.351 --> 02:30:00.020
Further playtesting in this level made us
confident that toner puzzles

02:30:00.020 --> 02:30:04.100
should be included in the game and we started
adding them in to other areas,

02:30:04.100 --> 02:30:07.500
but we didn't include the test level itself
anywhere in the game.

02:30:07.500 --> 02:30:11.980
It wasn't until later that it became apparent
that the zoo was in need of a pace break.

02:30:11.980 --> 02:30:15.460
We pay careful attention to player fatigue
when we playtest and look for

02:30:15.460 --> 02:30:18.410
cases where a puzzle or some exploration and
resource gathering

02:30:18.410 --> 02:30:21.390
can be used to give the player a break from
combat.

02:30:21.390 --> 02:30:24.110
There was such a need toward the end of the
zoo level, and thankfully in this

02:30:24.110 --> 02:30:27.730
case we were able to make use of the toner
prototype level that had already been built.

02:30:27.730 --> 02:30:30.450
a4_01_storage_toner2
When we decided to resurrect our toner prototype

02:30:30.450 --> 02:30:34.200
level for use as a pacing
break in the zoo, we had the opportunity to

02:30:34.200 --> 02:30:37.480
make some improvements and
integrate elements that hadn't existed when

02:30:37.480 --> 02:30:40.350
we had first experimented with the toner mechanic.

02:30:40.350 --> 02:30:44.160
For example, the newly-developed explosive
Xen bloaters were added at various

02:30:44.160 --> 02:30:47.810
points along the puzzle to surprise players
and remind them that they need to keep an

02:30:47.810 --> 02:30:49.490
eye out for these hazards.

02:30:49.490 --> 02:30:52.980
Also, in the intervening months, the toner
mechanic itself had evolved

02:30:52.980 --> 02:30:56.520
from one where players merely push a ball
of energy along a path,

02:30:56.520 --> 02:31:00.360
to one which included branching paths and
rotating junctions.

02:31:00.360 --> 02:31:03.990
This junction here in the breaker box is the
linchpin of the whole puzzle,

02:31:03.990 --> 02:31:08.020
as the player must interact with it three
times: once to open the roller door

02:31:08.020 --> 02:31:12.160
to this back area, once to switch power away
from the roller door to the last

02:31:12.160 --> 02:31:16.150
leg of the puzzle and once to re-open the
roller door in order to exit.

02:31:16.150 --> 02:31:20.000
Because this toner puzzle covers such a large
physical area,

02:31:20.000 --> 02:31:23.761
including some zombie combat in the middle,
players frequently lost track of

02:31:23.761 --> 02:31:27.700
the correspondence between the state of this
junction and the roller door.

02:31:27.700 --> 02:31:32.070
This meant that they would trap themselves
back here and not understand how to proceed.

02:31:32.070 --> 02:31:35.590
To address this, we made a lot of changes
to the presentation of this junction over

02:31:35.590 --> 02:31:36.930
the course of development.

02:31:36.930 --> 02:31:40.471
The unique breaker box and tangle of conduit
are designed to make

02:31:40.471 --> 02:31:45.051
the junction recognizable as the same junction
from both sides of the wall.

02:31:45.051 --> 02:31:47.480
The shape of the hole in the wall and the
orientation of the breaker

02:31:47.480 --> 02:31:50.771
box are designed to make sure that the player
stands in the right location

02:31:50.771 --> 02:31:54.090
to have line of sight to the roller door and
see that it closes when

02:31:54.090 --> 02:31:57.590
they route power away from it to the last
leg of the puzzle.

02:31:57.590 --> 02:32:01.070
The door itself also makes a lot of noise
and an unreasonable amount

02:32:01.070 --> 02:32:05.380
of sparks to draw attention to itself each
time it moves.

02:32:05.380 --> 02:32:09.450
This particular puzzle twist was in danger
of being cut for a long time,

02:32:09.450 --> 02:32:12.311
but with this series of refinements, enough
playtesters understood its

02:32:12.311 --> 02:32:15.560
behavior that we were comfortable with shipping
it in the final game.

02:32:15.560 --> 02:32:17.630
a4_02_tractor_beam
The emergence from the zoo gave us an opportunity

02:32:17.630 --> 02:32:21.460
to present players with a
dramatic vista and to show them how much progress

02:32:21.460 --> 02:32:23.310
they had made in their journey to vault.

02:32:23.310 --> 02:32:26.370
This was challenging because the vault's position
in the world was

02:32:26.370 --> 02:32:29.230
already determined to be above the parking
garage,

02:32:29.230 --> 02:32:31.260
which the player would reach at the end of
the map.

02:32:31.260 --> 02:32:35.210
But placing the vault correctly in support
of that location left the vault

02:32:35.210 --> 02:32:39.280
looking too distant to create the dramatic
view we wanted here.

02:32:39.280 --> 02:32:43.090
We also intended to portray the cutting of
the vault's power cables by the

02:32:43.090 --> 02:32:45.900
Vortigaunts at this point, but having this
take place at such a

02:32:45.900 --> 02:32:49.440
distant location was unclear and confused
playtesters.

02:32:49.440 --> 02:32:51.670
The solution was to move everything closer.

02:32:51.670 --> 02:32:54.580
The vault looked more imposing, looming above
the player, and the effects

02:32:54.580 --> 02:32:57.280
of the Vortigaunts' cable cutting efforts
were much clearer.

02:32:57.280 --> 02:33:01.740
All we needed then was a conceit to justify
the two vault locations:

02:33:01.740 --> 02:33:06.360
the location here at the start of the map
and the eventual location at the end of the

02:33:06.360 --> 02:33:07.360
map.

02:33:07.360 --> 02:33:09.980
Around this same phase of development, we
were in the process of defining

02:33:09.980 --> 02:33:13.261
exactly how the player would bring down the
vault, and our solution to

02:33:13.261 --> 02:33:17.620
that problem also helped justify the two vault
locations.

02:33:17.620 --> 02:33:20.760
Tractor beams are a familiar cliché in science
fiction and often share a

02:33:20.760 --> 02:33:26.640
common visual style of an enveloping force
field, emitted net-like from a single point.

02:33:26.640 --> 02:33:30.690
Using a tractor beam as a Combine failsafe
device to catch the vault served us well here.

02:33:30.690 --> 02:33:33.800
It allowed us to prevent Alyx from being crushed
by the vault dropping out

02:33:33.800 --> 02:33:37.270
of the sky when the Vortigaunts cut the final
power cable and it gave us a

02:33:37.270 --> 02:33:42.130
satisfactory mechanism for moving the vault
to the location we needed it to be at the

02:33:42.130 --> 02:33:43.740
end of the map.

02:33:43.740 --> 02:33:47.180
Throughout the map, the beam itself also serves
as a beacon, ensuring that

02:33:47.180 --> 02:33:51.400
the players are always able to recognize their
new goal: the tractor beam control station.

02:33:51.400 --> 02:33:53.520
a4_02_collaborator
The final twist of the game presented quite

02:33:53.520 --> 02:33:56.030
a few story-telling challenges for the writers.

02:33:56.030 --> 02:33:59.860
The game is long – much longer than a movie
– and the surprise reveal of the

02:33:59.860 --> 02:34:04.771
G-Man at the end felt almost impossible to
sustain over the entire experience.

02:34:04.771 --> 02:34:07.050
Everyone on the team assumed that through
basic deduction,

02:34:07.050 --> 02:34:09.381
most players were going to figure it out along
the way.

02:34:09.381 --> 02:34:13.540
So the solution, like in most good stories,
was to just add more stuff:

02:34:13.540 --> 02:34:17.340
More details, more questions and more nooks
and crannies to the plot.

02:34:17.340 --> 02:34:19.950
The writers quickly realized that there was
one big,

02:34:19.950 --> 02:34:22.980
distracting name – Gordon Freeman – that
would pull the player's attention

02:34:22.980 --> 02:34:26.440
away from any theories they may be cooking
up on their own.

02:34:26.440 --> 02:34:29.472
We then realized that we could use the absence
of the G-Man in the story

02:34:29.472 --> 02:34:33.440
and the eerie parallels between the G-Man
and Gordon's experiences at

02:34:33.440 --> 02:34:38.540
Black Mesa to redirect player attention and
propel the story towards the final act.

02:34:38.540 --> 02:34:41.210
If the player thought they had uncovered a
new mission, to save

02:34:41.210 --> 02:34:45.710
Gordon Freeman from stasis, then the twist
at the end could be secure.

02:34:45.710 --> 02:34:49.460
To pull it off, the writers created a new,
mysterious character:

02:34:49.460 --> 02:34:52.190
A scientist collaborating with The Combine.

02:34:52.190 --> 02:34:55.540
This addition came late in the project, so
the writers relied on another

02:34:55.540 --> 02:34:58.971
tried and true tact: base the character on
an actor we're fond of and

02:34:58.971 --> 02:35:02.851
then do our absolute best to cast and record
with that person.

02:35:02.851 --> 02:35:07.450
The chosen actor had struck a particular tone
with the writers: she has an easy,

02:35:07.450 --> 02:35:10.920
in-command confidence and a smooth southern,
Missouri drawl.

02:35:10.920 --> 02:35:13.600
This not only sets her apart from the other
characters in the Half-Life

02:35:13.600 --> 02:35:16.920
universe but makes the character easy to hear
on the page,

02:35:16.920 --> 02:35:20.740
which is incredibly important when time is
short at the end of the project.

02:35:20.740 --> 02:35:24.920
And once she was in the studio, her pitch-perfect
performance assured us that we'd made the

02:35:24.920 --> 02:35:26.410
correct decision.

02:35:26.410 --> 02:35:29.270
Of course, then the scene still had to be
built.

02:35:29.270 --> 02:35:32.590
Level designers figured out what existing
chunk of level track could be bumped out to

02:35:32.590 --> 02:35:34.090
fit the scene.

02:35:34.090 --> 02:35:37.030
The area we chose previously held some barnacles
and a few light

02:35:37.030 --> 02:35:40.690
puzzle elements which the designers weren't
upset to lose.

02:35:40.690 --> 02:35:43.650
The level track had to be altered to funnel
you into a place where the scene

02:35:43.650 --> 02:35:47.970
could unfold and the player could eavesdrop
without getting a good look at the character.

02:35:47.970 --> 02:35:49.490
This served two purposes.

02:35:49.490 --> 02:35:51.760
One was that it would benefit the mystery
for this

02:35:51.760 --> 02:35:55.280
scene to feel furtive and for the identity
of the character to remain secret.

02:35:55.280 --> 02:35:58.900
But also we could shroud the performance and
the intricate facial animation

02:35:58.900 --> 02:36:04.240
necessary to bring a character to life, all
in shadow for the animators and the choreographers.

02:36:04.240 --> 02:36:07.290
Like in a lot of cases, not doing the work,
was the correct thing

02:36:07.290 --> 02:36:09.880
for both the story and our tight shipping
schedule.

02:36:09.880 --> 02:36:12.040
a4_02_scavenger_hideout
The purpose of this hideout was to give players

02:36:12.040 --> 02:36:14.980
a nice
reward for being observant and exploring the

02:36:14.980 --> 02:36:15.980
environment.

02:36:15.980 --> 02:36:19.280
We wanted to highlight the story of scavengers
living behind the quarantine wall,

02:36:19.280 --> 02:36:23.010
what their lives might look like, and why
they might choose to live there.

02:36:23.010 --> 02:36:27.200
The person who lived here was referred to
internally as the 'Combine Killer,'

02:36:27.200 --> 02:36:29.620
imagined as a person with a grudge against
the Combine,

02:36:29.620 --> 02:36:31.851
who has chosen to eke out a living in the
quarantine zone,

02:36:31.851 --> 02:36:35.970
pending their time exacting revenge on unsuspecting
Combine patrols,

02:36:35.970 --> 02:36:40.000
perhaps eventually joining the organized resistance
movement portrayed in Half-Life 2.

02:36:40.000 --> 02:36:42.460
a4_02_spectator_hud
In recent years, video game streaming has

02:36:42.460 --> 02:36:46.280
become one of the main ways that
players engage with the culture of games and

02:36:46.280 --> 02:36:50.311
this is even more true for
VR titles where players who do not have VR

02:36:50.311 --> 02:36:52.400
equipment want to participate.

02:36:52.400 --> 02:36:56.240
To address this audience, we created a spectator
HUD, which displays the

02:36:56.240 --> 02:37:01.721
VR player's health, ammunition, resin and
the contents of their wrist pockets.

02:37:01.721 --> 02:37:05.820
This information is overlaid on the 3D scene
in the desktop window and

02:37:05.820 --> 02:37:09.311
lets viewers track the state of the player's
resources, without having to

02:37:09.311 --> 02:37:12.680
rely on the player to look at their wrist
or open up a menu.

02:37:12.680 --> 02:37:16.590
The spectator HUD started off as a tool used
by the development team to

02:37:16.590 --> 02:37:19.800
display resources, as well as the state of
controller buttons

02:37:19.800 --> 02:37:22.270
and other diagnostic information.

02:37:22.270 --> 02:37:25.521
An interesting side effect of this HUD was
that it made playtests way more

02:37:25.521 --> 02:37:29.061
exciting to watch, because we as an audience
had information that

02:37:29.061 --> 02:37:31.270
the player wasn't necessarily aware of.

02:37:31.270 --> 02:37:34.360
For example, playtest observers might realize
that a player had barely

02:37:34.360 --> 02:37:38.800
survived an encounter, when the players themselves
had never even checked their health.

02:37:38.800 --> 02:37:42.271
We even saw cases where players killed an
enemy with their very last bullet without

02:37:42.271 --> 02:37:44.580
realizing it themselves.

02:37:44.580 --> 02:37:47.960
The highs and lows we felt watching our own
playtests were something that

02:37:47.960 --> 02:37:52.090
we wanted to share with the streaming audience
and it's been exciting to see streamers using

02:37:52.090 --> 02:37:53.090
it in the wild.

02:37:53.090 --> 02:37:55.370
a4_02_tanker_combat
Creating a climactic battle that capped off

02:37:55.370 --> 02:37:59.020
the antlion combat
proved to be a challenging design problem.

02:37:59.020 --> 02:38:02.720
We wanted to create a sustained combat experience
that was substantial enough

02:38:02.720 --> 02:38:06.520
to challenge players who had refined their
skills against the antlions.

02:38:06.520 --> 02:38:09.870
But simply throwing a large number of antlions
at the player all at once

02:38:09.870 --> 02:38:14.230
was not a particularly engaging experience,
nor was it compatible with our established

02:38:14.230 --> 02:38:15.940
combat balancing.

02:38:15.940 --> 02:38:18.680
Rather than containing the player in a restricted
space and spawning

02:38:18.680 --> 02:38:22.150
waves of enemies, we wanted to keep the player
engaged and moving

02:38:22.150 --> 02:38:25.110
through a gauntlet of constant pressure toward
a goal.

02:38:25.110 --> 02:38:28.770
To do this, we needed an arena with a clear
linear flow and a porous

02:38:28.770 --> 02:38:31.540
layout that would allow the player to maintain
sight of their goal

02:38:31.540 --> 02:38:35.850
while being flanked by swarms of antlions
as they moved toward it.

02:38:35.850 --> 02:38:38.760
This tanker yard, which we had originally
built for a narrative scene

02:38:38.760 --> 02:38:42.280
that was cut from the game, had the properties
we were looking for.

02:38:42.280 --> 02:38:45.761
The long tanker cars lead toward the intended
direction of travel,

02:38:45.761 --> 02:38:47.971
and their positioning requires the player
to pay careful

02:38:47.971 --> 02:38:51.380
attention to their sides and rear as they
advance.

02:38:51.380 --> 02:38:54.680
The Combine tractor beam tower is framed at
the end of the tracks,

02:38:54.680 --> 02:38:56.620
providing a clear goal for the player.

02:38:56.620 --> 02:39:00.020
The placement of the glowing red sign in the
direction of the tractor beam

02:39:00.020 --> 02:39:03.820
was also a deliberate decision, a technique
used by the environment artists

02:39:03.820 --> 02:39:08.730
to subconsciously pull players in the desired
direction via visual contrast,

02:39:08.730 --> 02:39:13.190
even in the event that they've momentarily
lost their bearings in the chaos of the battle.

02:39:13.190 --> 02:39:16.561
The pacing of the combat here was carefully
balanced to leave most

02:39:16.561 --> 02:39:19.980
playtesters on the brink of being overwhelmed,
and feeling like they made

02:39:19.980 --> 02:39:22.540
it through by just the skin of their teeth.

02:39:22.540 --> 02:39:26.311
It took many iterations of enemy placement,
ammo and item placement,

02:39:26.311 --> 02:39:29.170
and player path adjustments to achieve this
balance.

02:39:29.170 --> 02:39:32.480
We ultimately settled on a design that featured
a steady stream of

02:39:32.480 --> 02:39:36.360
advancing antlions, with antlion spitters
placed at the far end of the

02:39:36.360 --> 02:39:41.090
space applying enough pressure to prevent
players from rushing toward the goal.

02:39:41.090 --> 02:39:44.150
Combine soldiers up on the raised platforms
divert player attention

02:39:44.150 --> 02:39:47.950
to the right hand flank and provide verticality
in target prioritization,

02:39:47.950 --> 02:39:50.510
adding to the sense of frenzy we sought to
create.

02:39:50.510 --> 02:39:52.450
a4_03_chasm_puzzle
The goal of this puzzle was to create a moment

02:39:52.450 --> 02:39:55.510
of heightened stress by
requiring players to deal with a sudden threat

02:39:55.510 --> 02:39:59.160
after being
placed in a precariously disadvantageous position.

02:39:59.160 --> 02:40:02.260
In the original design of the puzzle, players
would stand on a platform

02:40:02.260 --> 02:40:05.980
which was suspended from a cable and pull
themselves across the chasm.

02:40:05.980 --> 02:40:09.250
Halfway across, antlion spitters would emerge
and start attacking.

02:40:09.250 --> 02:40:12.110
The platform provided limited cover and players
would have to make

02:40:12.110 --> 02:40:16.130
trade-offs between taking cover and continuing
to propel themselves forward.

02:40:16.130 --> 02:40:19.010
This succeeded in creating the desired spike
in intensity,

02:40:19.010 --> 02:40:21.750
but unfortunately, the lateral movement of
the platform caused

02:40:21.750 --> 02:40:24.540
considerable motion sickness for most playtesters.

02:40:24.540 --> 02:40:27.270
Even players who were comfortable with the
vertical movement of elevators

02:40:27.270 --> 02:40:30.070
elsewhere in the game were adversely affected
here.

02:40:30.070 --> 02:40:32.840
The puzzle that we shipped retains the same
sense of tension, due to being

02:40:32.840 --> 02:40:36.950
stranded in the middle of a chasm under attack,
but does not rely on physically moving the

02:40:36.950 --> 02:40:37.970
player.

02:40:37.970 --> 02:40:41.030
The upper portion of the wooden platform is
deliberately devoid of any

02:40:41.030 --> 02:40:45.521
substantial cover, which encourages players
to jump onto the platforms below.

02:40:45.521 --> 02:40:48.970
On the lower platforms, we provide enough
cover to protect players from the

02:40:48.970 --> 02:40:52.900
antlions, but also leave them feeling vulnerable,
out in the middle of the chasm.

02:40:52.900 --> 02:40:55.729
The cover here is porous and breakable, increasing
the sense of urgency

02:40:55.729 --> 02:40:59.400
and requiring players to engage the antlions
from their compromised position.

02:40:59.400 --> 02:41:01.360
a4_03_combine_combat
In cases where the player had to face off

02:41:01.360 --> 02:41:05.210
against Combine in relatively
open spaces like this one, we often found

02:41:05.210 --> 02:41:08.270
that playtesters would backtrack
to safety and attempt to pick away at the

02:41:08.270 --> 02:41:10.050
soldiers from a distance.

02:41:10.050 --> 02:41:14.330
The playtesters would ultimately be successful
in clearing the combat space,

02:41:14.330 --> 02:41:16.670
but would feel unsatisfied with the encounter
due to the

02:41:16.670 --> 02:41:20.000
repetitive and static nature of the tactics
they employed.

02:41:20.000 --> 02:41:24.690
To address this, we used a few design tools
to discourage this type of player behavior.

02:41:24.690 --> 02:41:27.900
The placement of the Combine suppressor standing
on the bed of the truck at the

02:41:27.900 --> 02:41:32.280
far end of the courtyard here is the key to
the designed flow of this battle.

02:41:32.280 --> 02:41:34.790
The suppressor does not move from his planted
position on the far

02:41:34.790 --> 02:41:37.720
side of the courtyard while the player is
on the near side.

02:41:37.720 --> 02:41:41.221
His positioning, combined with his unrelenting
wall of suppressing

02:41:41.221 --> 02:41:44.480
fire, forces the player to advance in order
to close the distance

02:41:44.480 --> 02:41:48.080
required to take him out, all while under
intense pressure from a

02:41:48.080 --> 02:41:51.710
carefully crafted combination of other Combine
units.

02:41:51.710 --> 02:41:54.840
Additionally, if the player backtracks far
enough from the fight,

02:41:54.840 --> 02:41:57.970
the more mobile Combine soldiers in the encounter
are told to retreat

02:41:57.970 --> 02:42:02.360
and hold their ground until the player once
again advances into the combat.

02:42:02.360 --> 02:42:05.790
These elements together keep players moving
forward into locations that

02:42:05.790 --> 02:42:10.391
provide for more interesting opportunities
for both the player and the enemy soldiers.

02:42:10.391 --> 02:42:12.920
a4_03_vertical_tower
After the intense combat that precedes it,

02:42:12.920 --> 02:42:16.980
this tower initially serves as
a pacing break before once again ramping up

02:42:16.980 --> 02:42:21.229
the intensity on the player's
final fight up to the Combine tractor beam

02:42:21.229 --> 02:42:22.229
controls.

02:42:22.229 --> 02:42:25.540
While the lower portion of the tower does
contain combat, it has been

02:42:25.540 --> 02:42:28.940
designed to cause players to engage with the
space in a

02:42:28.940 --> 02:42:31.600
different way than the preceding courtyard
fight.

02:42:31.600 --> 02:42:36.010
The focus here is on navigating the chaotic
verticality of the tower,

02:42:36.010 --> 02:42:41.310
which we found to be particularly successful
for playtesters to experience in VR.

02:42:41.310 --> 02:42:45.570
The manhacks that initially engage the player
from above fly a path that

02:42:45.570 --> 02:42:50.060
deliberately has them crashing around the
pipes and boards overhead,

02:42:50.060 --> 02:42:53.720
causing sparks and splinters to rain down
on the player.

02:42:53.720 --> 02:42:56.920
Combined with the disorienting nature of the
space, the Combine suppressor

02:42:56.920 --> 02:43:01.170
that emerges at the top of the stairwell is
used to reintroduce an appropriate

02:43:01.170 --> 02:43:06.480
level of intensity and to create a sense of
a struggle as the player continues to scramble

02:43:06.480 --> 02:43:07.660
closer to their goal.

02:43:07.660 --> 02:43:10.050
a4_03_vault_panel
At the start of the previous map, we introduced

02:43:10.050 --> 02:43:13.710
the tractor beam,
a fail-safe device used to catch the falling

02:43:13.710 --> 02:43:17.300
vault when the
last substation was knocked offline.

02:43:17.300 --> 02:43:20.590
In this location, the console looks like it
could be used to control the

02:43:20.590 --> 02:43:25.210
tractor beam to dock the Vault for entry via
the nearby gantry on the left.

02:43:25.210 --> 02:43:28.800
We decided to have the vault crash to the
ground spectacularly,

02:43:28.800 --> 02:43:34.160
but for this to work emotionally, we needed
the scene to increase in tension beforehand.

02:43:34.160 --> 02:43:37.710
The whole vault docking console is absurd
by design.

02:43:37.710 --> 02:43:41.229
If the console appeared too much like a legitimate
puzzle,

02:43:41.229 --> 02:43:44.400
playtesters could take too long and let the
air out of the scene,

02:43:44.400 --> 02:43:47.940
unsure if there was something that they could
actually solve.

02:43:47.940 --> 02:43:50.931
The more inscrutable the machine looked, the
more likely they were to

02:43:50.931 --> 02:43:55.768
interact with the levers and play along with
the unfolding disaster.

02:43:55.768 --> 02:43:59.630
The first phase of the puzzle extends the
catwalk and unlocks the main

02:43:59.630 --> 02:44:04.630
lever bank which responds to the player’s
input, engaging them in the solution.

02:44:04.630 --> 02:44:07.590
Every subsequent lever thrown does nothing
except increase

02:44:07.590 --> 02:44:10.700
the tension as things continue to go wrong.

02:44:10.700 --> 02:44:14.229
The back and forth radio dialog between Alyx
and Russell completes

02:44:14.229 --> 02:44:18.630
the scene with growing panic and keeps players
in the moment,

02:44:18.630 --> 02:44:21.120
flipping levers in an attempt to save the
Vault.

02:44:21.120 --> 02:44:23.261
a4_04_strider_intro
Five years ago, we placed playtesters beneath

02:44:23.261 --> 02:44:25.590
an idling Half-Life 2 Strider.

02:44:25.590 --> 02:44:29.630
It was a really simple prototype, but the
feedback was overwhelmingly positive.

02:44:29.630 --> 02:44:33.960
This experience convinced us we should pursue
a Strider encounter in VR.

02:44:33.960 --> 02:44:37.620
And as a result, we built dozens of prototypes
over the next few years.

02:44:37.620 --> 02:44:41.851
Some designs pressed playtesters into very
close contact with the Strider.

02:44:41.851 --> 02:44:46.120
Some had a Strider chase them through a simple
environment or shoot at them through porous

02:44:46.120 --> 02:44:47.120
cover.

02:44:47.120 --> 02:44:49.421
In other cases, the Strider would destroy
a wall the player was

02:44:49.421 --> 02:44:52.870
hiding behind or give a performance the player
was meant to observe.

02:44:52.870 --> 02:44:57.341
A prototype built in 2017 squared the player
directly off with the Strider

02:44:57.341 --> 02:45:01.730
in a quarry, combining many of our ideas into
one experience.

02:45:01.730 --> 02:45:04.729
Based on the success of this prototype, the
team moved on to building the

02:45:04.729 --> 02:45:08.720
shipping experience ahead of you, although
it would take another three years of refinement

02:45:08.720 --> 02:45:09.800
to complete.

02:45:09.800 --> 02:45:14.240
Each iteration brought us a new set of rules
and player expectations.

02:45:14.240 --> 02:45:18.380
The Strider encounter ahead is a carefully
crafted collection of beats shaped

02:45:18.380 --> 02:45:23.580
by our own ideas of what would be exciting
and fun, refined over a long period of playtesting.

02:45:23.580 --> 02:45:26.641
a4_04_lone_combine
Through many iterations of the strider encounter,

02:45:26.641 --> 02:45:30.930
we would find ourselves having
functionally solved a problem only to realize

02:45:30.930 --> 02:45:35.350
that we also had to fictionally
contextualize the solution so that it made

02:45:35.350 --> 02:45:36.380
sense.

02:45:36.380 --> 02:45:41.680
For example, we designed this entire map to
include combat with Combine soldiers,

02:45:41.680 --> 02:45:45.550
but how should we fictionally place them in
this destroyed space?

02:45:45.550 --> 02:45:48.950
How could we put a disabled strider close
to the player?

02:45:48.950 --> 02:45:52.960
What were the Combine doing here prior to
the vault collapse?

02:45:52.960 --> 02:45:57.150
This soldier was placed here to communicate
to the player that some Combine were

02:45:57.150 --> 02:46:01.900
in the area investigating an antlion infestation
before the vault fell on them.

02:46:01.900 --> 02:46:05.080
In fact, you can see some dead antlions nearby.

02:46:05.080 --> 02:46:10.610
If you listen closely, you'll hear the soldier
trying to contact the rest of his squad,

02:46:10.610 --> 02:46:15.660
letting the player know that he is not alone
and that there is likely to be more combat

02:46:15.660 --> 02:46:16.660
ahead.

02:46:16.660 --> 02:46:18.780
a4_04_disabled_strider
Although we could excuse a few random combine

02:46:18.780 --> 02:46:23.040
in this area left over from
investigating the antlion infestation, we

02:46:23.040 --> 02:46:26.490
needed a way to justify the large
squads of Combine in the combat encounters

02:46:26.490 --> 02:46:28.680
that occur later in the level.

02:46:28.680 --> 02:46:32.090
These two combine are part of a squad sent
down looking for survivors after

02:46:32.090 --> 02:46:35.990
the vault collapse and their dialog is designed
to inform the player

02:46:35.990 --> 02:46:38.740
that this strider they have found may not
be dead.

02:46:38.740 --> 02:46:42.580
Fictionally, it is the player's combat with
these two soldiers that alerts

02:46:42.580 --> 02:46:45.490
the rest of the combine in this area to the
player's presence.

02:46:45.490 --> 02:46:47.800
a4_04_lion_and_mouse
For many players, Striders are the most feared

02:46:47.800 --> 02:46:50.790
and powerful enemies in
the Half-Life franchise, and we thought it

02:46:50.790 --> 02:46:54.140
would be interesting to
flip player expectations on their head and

02:46:54.140 --> 02:46:58.050
experience what it would feel like to team
up and cooperate with a Strider.

02:46:58.050 --> 02:47:01.950
Our initial concept was inspired by the Aesop's
fable of The Lion and the Mouse,

02:47:01.950 --> 02:47:05.650
with the player freeing a disabled Strider,
who would later help the player escape by

02:47:05.650 --> 02:47:07.090
clearing the way forward.

02:47:07.090 --> 02:47:10.870
Initially, the parking garage was a Combine
silo, and it was much easier

02:47:10.870 --> 02:47:15.700
fictionally to have some type of Combine device
which could constrain a Strider without injury.

02:47:15.700 --> 02:47:19.340
When the location changed to a collapsed parking
garage, we constrained the

02:47:19.340 --> 02:47:23.850
Strider by trapping it in the elevator shaft,
held down by the elevator car.

02:47:23.850 --> 02:47:26.800
After being freed, the injured Strider would
lash out at anything

02:47:26.800 --> 02:47:30.630
nearby, Combine and player alike, as it tried
to escape.

02:47:30.630 --> 02:47:33.720
This was a strong story when told aloud, but
expressing it in a way

02:47:33.720 --> 02:47:36.410
that was understood by players turned out
to be difficult.

02:47:36.410 --> 02:47:39.710
Keeping the Strider around throughout the
level, alternating between injured

02:47:39.710 --> 02:47:43.380
and angry states as the player slowly picked
their way through the garage,

02:47:43.380 --> 02:47:48.840
was confusing to playesters and we had a very
hard time trying to find the right balance.

02:47:48.840 --> 02:47:52.040
Playtesting feedback was really consistent
around this, so although we

02:47:52.040 --> 02:47:55.090
loved the idea, we let it go in favor of a
more straightforward and

02:47:55.090 --> 02:47:58.780
achievable dynamic of Hunter and Prey that
eventually shipped.

02:47:58.780 --> 02:48:03.440
The year we spent pursuing the Lion and Mouse
idea wasn't wasted, however,

02:48:03.440 --> 02:48:07.570
as we created and refined many of the different
story beats that shipped.

02:48:07.570 --> 02:48:12.030
We also crafted some performances and technology
that led to the success of the final experience.

02:48:12.030 --> 02:48:14.620
a4_04_ai_animation
The Strider encounter boils down to a set

02:48:14.620 --> 02:48:18.431
of performances, and the Strider's AI-driven
minigun.

02:48:18.431 --> 02:48:21.250
Maintaining the illusion that the Strider
was aware of, and hunting, the

02:48:21.250 --> 02:48:25.110
player while delivering these performances
required a delicate coordination between Animation

02:48:25.110 --> 02:48:26.351
and AI systems.

02:48:26.351 --> 02:48:30.460
We used a variety of approaches to make the
Strider feel like an intelligent creature,

02:48:30.460 --> 02:48:31.850
hunting the player.

02:48:31.850 --> 02:48:35.170
Players in VR move very slowly by first person
shooter standards,

02:48:35.170 --> 02:48:38.720
so we have the Strider alternate between 'chasing'
performances, following

02:48:38.720 --> 02:48:41.830
the player through the level, and then 'hunting'
performances, focused

02:48:41.830 --> 02:48:45.421
on tracking and firing at the player, or looking
for them.

02:48:45.421 --> 02:48:47.630
Triggering the different stages of this pursuit
took a lot of playtesting.

02:48:47.630 --> 02:48:51.050
We paid close attention to how playtesters
would move through the level and

02:48:51.050 --> 02:48:55.260
then built the triggers and iterated on the
animations to support the most common behaviors.

02:48:55.260 --> 02:48:59.180
It was very much a back and forth conversation
to evolve a beat into something

02:48:59.180 --> 02:49:02.510
that most players would believe the Strider
was truly hunting them.

02:49:02.510 --> 02:49:05.670
Early playtesters would grow weary of being
shot at without any downtime

02:49:05.670 --> 02:49:09.450
or contrasting experiences, so we identified
the beats that would create

02:49:09.450 --> 02:49:13.760
that contrast and layer them in when we saw
playtesters become fatigued.

02:49:13.760 --> 02:49:16.891
Things like the strider bashing the wall,
walking over you, chasing you up

02:49:16.891 --> 02:49:21.650
an elevator shaft, all acted as ways to break
up the experience and surprise the player.

02:49:21.650 --> 02:49:25.479
Whenever we had feedback a playtester was
growing or looked bored or fatigued,

02:49:25.479 --> 02:49:28.150
we would brainstorm different ways to insert
these beats,

02:49:28.150 --> 02:49:30.770
and then test it to verify that they were
paced correctly.

02:49:30.770 --> 02:49:34.820
One of the tools we used was a sophisticated
lookat system on the strider.

02:49:34.820 --> 02:49:38.931
This system tracked the player and would procedurally
orient different parts of the strider to face

02:49:38.931 --> 02:49:40.140
the player.

02:49:40.140 --> 02:49:43.430
The body, minigun and gauss cannon all had
different lookats to

02:49:43.430 --> 02:49:46.650
breakup the effect when the strider lost or
acquired the player.

02:49:46.650 --> 02:49:50.160
These could be independently enabled or disabled
at specific times,

02:49:50.160 --> 02:49:53.040
to make it feel like the strider was directly
addressing the player.

02:49:53.040 --> 02:49:56.170
In each animation of the sequence, the lookat
on the minigun and body

02:49:56.170 --> 02:50:00.160
would be enabled or disabled based on whether
the strider should be able to see the player

02:50:00.160 --> 02:50:01.160
at that point.

02:50:01.160 --> 02:50:02.680
a4_04_elevator
It was important to communicate clearly to

02:50:02.680 --> 02:50:05.020
the player that
they were being hunted by the Strider at this

02:50:05.020 --> 02:50:06.170
point.

02:50:06.170 --> 02:50:09.229
We wanted players to see the Strider dust
itself off and fixate on

02:50:09.229 --> 02:50:12.931
them so that there would be no doubt that
they were now the prey.

02:50:12.931 --> 02:50:16.110
This elevator ride was a useful tool to achieve
this, as it is one of

02:50:16.110 --> 02:50:19.470
the few moments of the game that we have a
captive audience.

02:50:19.470 --> 02:50:22.440
The elevator door facing the Strider was carefully
designed to

02:50:22.440 --> 02:50:25.260
provide enough protection so that players
would feel safe,

02:50:25.260 --> 02:50:27.650
but enough visibility to see the Strider's
performance.

02:50:27.650 --> 02:50:30.040
a4_04_destructible_cover
During development, we talked a lot about

02:50:30.040 --> 02:50:33.550
the experience of the Strider
minigun blasting through the walls and floors

02:50:33.550 --> 02:50:36.460
of the parking garage
while hunting the player, but weren't sure

02:50:36.460 --> 02:50:40.030
we would be able to deliver
destructible cover until around six months

02:50:40.030 --> 02:50:41.730
prior to shipping.

02:50:41.730 --> 02:50:44.390
To address this, we designed the destructible
cover to visually

02:50:44.390 --> 02:50:47.110
communicate that it was being broken down
prior to actually

02:50:47.110 --> 02:50:48.979
fracturing and breaking.

02:50:48.979 --> 02:50:51.881
Initially, the cover would look like an unremarkable
piece of

02:50:51.881 --> 02:50:55.240
concrete or other building material, but when
it took sufficient damage,

02:50:55.240 --> 02:50:58.430
it would start to visibly crack and throw
off dust particles.

02:50:58.430 --> 02:51:01.820
When the same piece of cover took further
damage, it would then fracture

02:51:01.820 --> 02:51:06.210
into pre-cut physics objects and generate
more dust and debris particles.

02:51:06.210 --> 02:51:09.590
Through playtesting, we determined how much
damage the cover should be

02:51:09.590 --> 02:51:13.370
able to take before breaking down and forcing
the player to move.

02:51:13.370 --> 02:51:16.540
In the end, the destructible cover system
allowed us to deliver on our

02:51:16.540 --> 02:51:20.460
goal of giving players the impression that
the Strider was capable of

02:51:20.460 --> 02:51:24.070
tearing through the parking garage with its
minigun as it pursued them.

02:51:24.070 --> 02:51:26.221
a4_04_minigun
In this area of the map, we began hearing

02:51:26.221 --> 02:51:30.910
from some playtesters
that they found the minigun firing monotonous.

02:51:30.910 --> 02:51:36.351
Originally, the gun would fire at a set rate,
regardless of the player's behavior.

02:51:36.351 --> 02:51:43.000
To address the monotony, we made several improvements
to the Strider AI running just the minigun.

02:51:43.000 --> 02:51:47.610
The minigun will fire if it sees the player,
but take a moment to acquire them,

02:51:47.610 --> 02:51:51.250
play an acquired sound to warn them they've
locked on,

02:51:51.250 --> 02:51:55.970
and then 'stitch' the bullets towards the
player in its first volley.

02:51:55.970 --> 02:51:59.471
The minigun volleys themselves were broken
up to become more staccato,

02:51:59.471 --> 02:52:02.330
with random intervals in between volleys.

02:52:02.330 --> 02:52:05.270
If the player ducks behind cover, the Strider
will keep firing

02:52:05.270 --> 02:52:08.550
where it last saw the player for a short period.

02:52:08.550 --> 02:52:12.261
These changes to the minigun behavior eliminated
the monotony and

02:52:12.261 --> 02:52:15.180
gave players the impression that they were
fleeing a living creature.

02:52:15.180 --> 02:52:17.360
a4_04_doorframe_bash
To keep the strider engagement interesting

02:52:17.360 --> 02:52:20.090
throughout the level,
we knew that we wanted to challenge the player's

02:52:20.090 --> 02:52:22.990
understanding
of what the strider was capable of.

02:52:22.990 --> 02:52:25.740
We identified this upcoming room as a space
where most players

02:52:25.740 --> 02:52:28.260
felt they understood the Strider's capabilities
and

02:52:28.260 --> 02:52:30.721
built this next beat to surprise them.

02:52:30.721 --> 02:52:33.260
To create the doorframe bash moment, we used
the Strider's

02:52:33.260 --> 02:52:36.180
animation to drive an offline destruction
simulation that

02:52:36.180 --> 02:52:39.351
could be played back by the game engine in
real time.

02:52:39.351 --> 02:52:42.130
We coupled this with particle effects and
physics impulses on

02:52:42.130 --> 02:52:46.180
interactive physics objects in the room to
make the player feel unsafe.

02:52:46.180 --> 02:52:49.430
To fully sell the idea that the Strider has
been searching for and has

02:52:49.430 --> 02:52:52.970
found the player, we gave the Strider a moment
to pause after tearing

02:52:52.970 --> 02:52:56.240
away the wall to look at the player before
it started to fire again.

02:52:56.240 --> 02:52:58.300
a4_04_strider_sounds
When playtesting the Strider encounter, we

02:52:58.300 --> 02:53:00.970
learned that players enjoyed
fighting a challenging enemy but responded

02:53:00.970 --> 02:53:04.050
negatively if they
took damage in a way that seemed unfair.

02:53:04.050 --> 02:53:07.580
To address this, we used audio to communicate
the state of the Strider.

02:53:07.580 --> 02:53:10.260
For example, when the Strider did not have
line of sight to the player,

02:53:10.260 --> 02:53:13.070
it would emit a periodic hunting sound.

02:53:13.070 --> 02:53:16.970
This sound was meant to communicate to the
player that they were currently safe,

02:53:16.970 --> 02:53:18.930
but that the Strider was still there.

02:53:18.930 --> 02:53:20.880
Once the Strider had seen the player, it would
switch to

02:53:20.880 --> 02:53:27.100
its 'chasing' state and make corresponding
chasing sounds.

02:53:27.100 --> 02:53:29.250
When the Strider was ready to start firing
at the player,

02:53:29.250 --> 02:53:33.860
it would emit a 'target acquired' sound before
firing.

02:53:33.860 --> 02:53:36.510
Players quickly learned this pattern of sound
cues and no longer

02:53:36.510 --> 02:53:39.210
complained about being caught unaware of the
Strider's targeting state.

02:53:39.210 --> 02:53:41.550
a4_04_gauss_cannon
We needed to familiarize the player with the

02:53:41.550 --> 02:53:44.940
Strider's Gauss cannon firing
sequence so they'd be able to recognize it

02:53:44.940 --> 02:53:47.010
when they saw it again later in the map.

02:53:47.010 --> 02:53:50.011
This particular area of the map went through
a lot of iteration,

02:53:50.011 --> 02:53:53.030
as we searched for ways to reliably place
the player in the right spot

02:53:53.030 --> 02:53:58.280
at the right time looking at the right thing:
the Gauss Cannon as it charged up and fired.

02:53:58.280 --> 02:54:02.030
To achieve this, we ultimately settled on
using a two-handed roller door.

02:54:02.030 --> 02:54:05.900
It requires the player to stand in a specific
position and orientation and

02:54:05.900 --> 02:54:10.370
occupies their hands so we know they aren't
teleporting or interacting with anything else.

02:54:10.370 --> 02:54:13.521
We even know the precise moment that the door
clears their eye line,

02:54:13.521 --> 02:54:16.391
so we can be assured that they're going to
see what we want them to see.

02:54:16.391 --> 02:54:18.650
a4_04_destroy_strider
When the central theme of this map was The

02:54:18.650 --> 02:54:22.110
Lion and the Mouse,
the encounter with the Strider ended at this

02:54:22.110 --> 02:54:23.110
point.

02:54:23.110 --> 02:54:26.160
The Strider simply exited off into the sunset.

02:54:26.160 --> 02:54:29.330
We intended for the Strider to reappear in
a subsequent fight,

02:54:29.330 --> 02:54:32.560
perhaps even attacking the Combine alongside
the player,

02:54:32.560 --> 02:54:35.560
but this idea was only pursued briefly before
being cut.

02:54:35.560 --> 02:54:38.910
The problem was that playtesters felt cheated
that they didn't get to

02:54:38.910 --> 02:54:43.591
destroy the Strider after being hunted and
harassed by it for so long.

02:54:43.591 --> 02:54:47.130
In the final map, once the player steps into
the rat maze below,

02:54:47.130 --> 02:54:50.650
the Strider reappears and begins hunting the
player again.

02:54:50.650 --> 02:54:54.250
Because Alyx does not possess any weapons
that can damage the Strider,

02:54:54.250 --> 02:54:56.630
we explored a variety of ways that the player
could take

02:54:56.630 --> 02:55:00.250
out the Strider using something found in the
environment.

02:55:00.250 --> 02:55:04.630
These included ideas like triggering missiles
on a downed combine helicopter,

02:55:04.630 --> 02:55:07.490
using a Combine mortar emplacement or kicking
off some

02:55:07.490 --> 02:55:11.160
type of physics event to sweep the Strider's
legs out and kill it.

02:55:11.160 --> 02:55:14.060
We built prototypes of many of these ideas
and eventually

02:55:14.060 --> 02:55:17.500
narrowed down to the mounted gun solution
that shipped.

02:55:17.500 --> 02:55:22.440
It was simple, repeatable and was one of the
easier solutions to train under fire.

02:55:22.440 --> 02:55:24.230
a4_04_mounted_gun
In this sequence, we wanted the player to

02:55:24.230 --> 02:55:27.810
end the encounter by killing the
Strider in a dramatic boss fight using a weapon

02:55:27.810 --> 02:55:29.570
found in the environment.

02:55:29.570 --> 02:55:31.470
This meant that we had to introduce a new
weapon which

02:55:31.470 --> 02:55:34.110
was more powerful than the player's equipped
weapons and

02:55:34.110 --> 02:55:36.530
which had to remain in the environment after
the fight.

02:55:36.530 --> 02:55:39.990
A mounted gun on an abandoned Combine vehicle
made the most sense and

02:55:39.990 --> 02:55:43.181
after some initial tests with a downed Combine
helicopter repurposed

02:55:43.181 --> 02:55:46.479
from Half-Life 2, we settled on the armored
personnel carrier

02:55:46.479 --> 02:55:49.641
since one had already been built for use in
another section of the game.

02:55:49.641 --> 02:55:52.150
Since the gun mechanism itself would be seen
for the first time

02:55:52.150 --> 02:55:54.960
while under pressure from the Strider, it
had to have an aiming and

02:55:54.960 --> 02:55:57.930
firing mechanism that the player would understand
immediately.

02:55:57.930 --> 02:56:01.380
We prototyped a variety of designs from a
machine gun that auto-fired as

02:56:01.380 --> 02:56:04.280
soon as the player grabbed the handle, to
a gun that fired a sticky-bomb

02:56:04.280 --> 02:56:07.811
that had to be detonated by the player after
it had hit its target.

02:56:07.811 --> 02:56:11.190
The auto-firing machine gun was too simple
to use and lacked dramatic effect,

02:56:11.190 --> 02:56:13.920
and the extra interaction required to detonate
the sticky bomb

02:56:13.920 --> 02:56:16.860
was too much to teach the player while under
duress.

02:56:16.860 --> 02:56:19.400
We settled on a design that requires one hand
to aim

02:56:19.400 --> 02:56:22.350
and another to both reload and fire.

02:56:22.350 --> 02:56:24.890
Requiring the player to push the firing mechanism
all the way forward to

02:56:24.890 --> 02:56:28.820
reload was necessary to prevent the player
from firing too rapidly.

02:56:28.820 --> 02:56:31.910
The gun's shell reloading animation is directly
driven by the player's

02:56:31.910 --> 02:56:34.790
motion and communicates to the player that
they must move their

02:56:34.790 --> 02:56:38.420
hand all the way forward to reload before
being able to pull it back to fire.

02:56:38.420 --> 02:56:40.870
a5_01_broken_vault_music
As the player enters the vault and is immersed

02:56:40.870 --> 02:56:45.080
in its
warped reality, they lose contact with Russell.

02:56:45.080 --> 02:56:49.120
With Russell no longer available to explain
what the player was seeing,

02:56:49.120 --> 02:56:52.300
we had to communicate the important concepts
of the vault through

02:56:52.300 --> 02:56:56.979
the use of visual effects, physics, sound
design and music.

02:56:56.979 --> 02:57:00.500
The most important concept was that the errant
bursts of uncontrolled

02:57:00.500 --> 02:57:05.410
energy causing anti-reality events was spillover
of the Vortigaunt energy

02:57:05.410 --> 02:57:08.930
being used to keep the prisoner in the vault
as his influence

02:57:08.930 --> 02:57:13.431
occasionally gained the upper hand through
cracks in the field.

02:57:13.431 --> 02:57:16.650
Starting from the vault crash in the prior
map, we used this piece

02:57:16.650 --> 02:57:20.290
of musical sound design to represent the newly
uncontrolled

02:57:20.290 --> 02:57:26.350
Vortigaunt energy being released from the
vault.

02:57:26.350 --> 02:57:36.610
And this piece
to accompany the reality bending bursts caused

02:57:36.610 --> 02:57:40.311
and swayed by the G-Man's increasing reach.

02:57:40.311 --> 02:57:44.270
Within the vault, we repeat these motifs to
reinforce the Vortigaunt

02:57:44.270 --> 02:57:48.260
energy mechanic that the player is ultimately
able to use as a weapon

02:57:48.260 --> 02:57:54.230
against the Combine, short circuit to release
the prisoner, save Eli and complete the game.

02:57:54.230 --> 02:57:56.650
a5_01_sideways_music
Right from the earliest days of the story

02:57:56.650 --> 02:58:00.940
design, the audio team
established a corresponding aesthetic plan

02:58:00.940 --> 02:58:06.190
that employed increasingly
strange sound design, soundscapes and music,

02:58:06.190 --> 02:58:10.100
culminating in a soundscape
appropriate to the broken reality of the vault

02:58:10.100 --> 02:58:13.280
and Alyx's encounter with the G-Man.

02:58:13.280 --> 02:58:16.850
While the vault level was being designed,
it became apparent that the

02:58:16.850 --> 02:58:22.240
accompanying music would be crucial to selling
the surreal nature of the vault.

02:58:22.240 --> 02:59:15.660
Most music in the vault is based on the musical
palindrome you hear in the sideways room.

02:59:15.660 --> 02:59:19.780
By utilizing variations of elements in this
piece and warping them with

02:59:19.780 --> 02:59:47.930
multiple types of pitch and time bending,
we created an appropriately abstract sonority.

02:59:47.930 --> 02:59:53.460
While strange and dislocating, it was still
consistent unto itself as

02:59:53.460 --> 02:59:57.190
well as conceptually relevant to the multi-dimensional
nature

02:59:57.190 --> 03:00:01.110
of the G-Man and the Vortigaunt energy imprisoning
him.

03:00:01.110 --> 03:00:03.360
a5_01_surreal_vault
Half-Life: Alyx was set in a realistic type

03:00:03.360 --> 03:00:05.150
world with familiar rules.

03:00:05.150 --> 03:00:08.830
The G-Man's presence in the vault gave us
fictional justification to

03:00:08.830 --> 03:00:14.240
experiment with different distortions of reality
and embrace surrealism in these spaces.

03:00:14.240 --> 03:00:17.980
Early experiments with gravity anomalies and
distorting spatial expectations

03:00:17.980 --> 03:00:22.880
were promising, as the immersiveness of VR
worked to exaggerate their impact.

03:00:22.880 --> 03:00:26.470
The surreal environments proved to be fertile
for puzzle solving as well,

03:00:26.470 --> 03:00:29.261
but they tended to stall the pacing at the
point in game we needed

03:00:29.261 --> 03:00:33.200
it to be ramping up so they weren't included
in the final release.

03:00:33.200 --> 03:00:36.330
The most notable of these was in the mirrored
apartment.

03:00:36.330 --> 03:00:39.610
Players originally had to spot and correct
differences between the top and

03:00:39.610 --> 03:00:43.760
bottom apartment spaces in order to escape
that area.

03:00:43.760 --> 03:00:47.460
Instead of surreal interactive puzzles, the
first half of the vault became a journey

03:00:47.460 --> 03:00:50.500
through an increasingly bizarre set of spaces
that were once

03:00:50.500 --> 03:00:52.711
the apartment building the G-Man was captured
in.

03:00:52.711 --> 03:00:55.220
a5_01_vort_lightning
Supercharging the Russells with Vortigaunt

03:00:55.220 --> 03:00:58.400
energy helped us
achieve the goal of having an unarmed player

03:00:58.400 --> 03:01:01.280
overpower the last
few Combine soldiers that were guarding the

03:01:01.280 --> 03:01:02.840
G-man.

03:01:02.840 --> 03:01:06.750
Initially grenades and a zero gravity hallway
was how we achieved this.

03:01:06.750 --> 03:01:10.061
Separately, we'd been experimenting with using
the Russells to pull

03:01:10.061 --> 03:01:13.931
and throw energy bolts from Vortigaunt energy
nodes.

03:01:13.931 --> 03:01:17.420
This was a natural fit for the vault combat
section since it evokes the

03:01:17.420 --> 03:01:21.270
concept of super charging the gravity gun
at the end of Half-Life 2

03:01:21.270 --> 03:01:25.610
and works with the player's well mastered
Russells throwing mechanic.

03:01:25.610 --> 03:01:29.290
This new mechanic was introduced at a point
where pacing was critical,

03:01:29.290 --> 03:01:33.000
so it had to be intuitive to pick up and quick
to master.

03:01:33.000 --> 03:01:35.990
Giving the player the Vortigaunt energy attack
not only helped to tie

03:01:35.990 --> 03:01:39.320
together that this was Vortigaunt energy containing
the G-Man,

03:01:39.320 --> 03:01:43.610
but helped to further solidify the deep Vortigaunt
connection to Alyx.

03:01:43.610 --> 03:01:48.250
It also gave the player a chance to briefly
feel like they had super powers,

03:01:48.250 --> 03:01:53.920
and be able to use that ability in a visceral,
deeply physical way that's impossible in real

03:01:53.920 --> 03:01:54.920
life.

03:01:54.920 --> 03:01:56.850
a5_02_coetaneous
As the player first views and approaches the

03:01:56.850 --> 03:02:01.140
cage, the object of their
long quest, we wanted to give them a dramatic

03:02:01.140 --> 03:02:05.810
musical sense of the moment,
yet have it remain unsettling and consistent

03:02:05.810 --> 03:02:08.430
with the experience of the rest of the vault.

03:02:08.430 --> 03:02:12.930
We continued to utilize variations of previously
heard musical elements but

03:02:12.930 --> 03:02:17.420
this time more clearly and slightly less abstracted.

03:02:17.420 --> 03:02:21.240
As in previous pieces, the swirling string
elements are taken from the

03:02:21.240 --> 03:02:26.021
musical palindrome heard in the sideways room,
but these pieces are

03:02:26.021 --> 03:02:29.820
dislocated, out of sync and juxtaposed against
each other to

03:02:29.820 --> 03:02:52.090
represent multiple clashing realities and
timelines.

03:02:52.090 --> 03:02:57.300
As the player approaches the cage, a piano
piece is introduced that works

03:02:57.300 --> 03:03:03.229
with all three, almost binding them together
but also standing apart,

03:03:03.229 --> 03:03:21.521
just as the G-Man binds the various threads
and eras of the Half-Life series.

03:03:21.521 --> 03:03:48.770
a5_02_gman_room
The 

03:03:48.770 --> 03:03:52.160
more surreal things got in the vault, the
harder it became to design

03:03:52.160 --> 03:03:56.330
puzzles that felt appropriately abstract but
still understandable.

03:03:56.330 --> 03:03:59.729
We found through testing that, at this stage
of the game, players were hungry

03:03:59.729 --> 03:04:05.330
for a narrative payoff and further puzzles
ran the risk of becoming frustrations.

03:04:05.330 --> 03:04:08.960
After some experimentation with more complex
interactions, we settled on a

03:04:08.960 --> 03:04:12.950
more theatrical presentation of the moment
that the player frees the G-Man.

03:04:12.950 --> 03:04:16.660
We simplified the design to a single interaction
of the player gripping two

03:04:16.660 --> 03:04:20.460
handles, sending Vortigaunt energy back into
the system, destroying

03:04:20.460 --> 03:04:23.561
the structure that was keeping the G-Man imprisoned.

03:04:23.561 --> 03:04:26.771
The ending came together rather late in the
process, since all of the

03:04:26.771 --> 03:04:29.911
elements of art, story, level design, interaction
and music all

03:04:29.911 --> 03:04:32.280
had to be in place for it to succeed.

03:04:32.280 --> 03:04:37.440
Nevertheless, we were happy with the outcome
and the response of players has been overwhelmingly

03:04:37.440 --> 03:04:37.940
positive.

